; ======================================================================
; CYW43455 - the Raspberry Pi 4's on-board WiFi part, over SDIO.
; Library, not a compiler builtin.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THIS FILE STOPS AT THE FIRMWARE WALL AND IT STOPS LOUDLY. Read the
; section called THE WALL before writing anything that calls it. What is
; here is real and citable: bring the backplane clock up, read the chip
; ID out of the silicon, and walk the chip's own core table. What is not
; here - association, scanning, a single byte of network traffic - is
; not here because it CANNOT be here, and the reason is written down
; with evidence rather than left as a TODO for somebody to trip over.
;
; ======================================================================
; USE - this file names no other file (house rule: a library never
; includes a library). The MAIN program lists what it needs, and hands
; this file its four SDIO primitives:
;
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4"
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43.pi4"
;
;     MailboxInit()
;     SdioSetBaseClockKhz(MailboxClockRate(1) / 1000)   ; EMMC, not EMMC2
;     ; ... assert WL_ON, see sdio.pi4's THE POWER RAIL ...
;     If SdioInit() = 0 : PutS(SdioErrorText()) : ProcedureReturn : EndIf
;
;     Cyw43SetIo(@SdioCmd52Read, @SdioCmd52Write, @SdioCmd53Read, @SdioCmd53Write)
;     If Cyw43Attach() = 0
;       PutS(Cyw43ErrorText())
;     Else
;       PutS("chip ") : PutS(Cyw43ChipName())
;       PutS(" rev ") : PrintDec(Cyw43ChipRev())
;     EndIf
;
; ======================================================================
; THE SEAM - FOUR FUNCTION POINTERS, AND WHY
; ======================================================================
; This file never calls SdioCmd52Read by name. It calls whatever four
; addresses Cyw43SetIo() was handed, through variables declared with a
; leading '*'.
;
; THAT SPELLING IS LOAD-BEARING AND fat.pi4's HEADER RECORDS WHAT IT
; COST. The compiler refuses a call through a plain ".i" variable unless
; it can SEE an "x = @Something" assignment - a guard added after a
; bring-up branched to address $3. The guard cannot see through a
; setter whose assignment comes from a parameter, so "Global cyw43_rb.i"
; stops the build with "is a VARIABLE, not a procedure". A name declared
; with '*' already holds an address by declaration and is exempt.
; And the CALL is written with NO star: "cyw43_rb(fn, a)". Writing
; "*cyw43_rb(fn, a)" compiles clean and DEREFERENCES the return value.
;
; THREE REASONS FOR THE SEAM, in order of how much they matter:
;
;   1. IT IS THE ONLY WAY ANY OF THIS CAN BE TESTED. There is no board
;      available to this work. With the seam, tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py
;      can put a modelled CYW43455 behind it - one that answers a chip
;      ID and an EROM table - and the core walk gets a real test today.
;      Wired directly to sdio.pi4, which has itself never moved a byte,
;      this file would be untestable by construction.
;
;   2. A LIBRARY NEVER INCLUDES A LIBRARY. Naming SdioCmd52Read would
;      mean cyw43.pi4 could only ever be compiled beside sdio.pi4.
;
;   3. THE CYW43455 IS NOT ONLY AN SDIO PART. The same die appears on a
;      SPI bus on other boards, and the whole of this file above the
;      four primitives is bus-independent. Nothing here should have to
;      change to follow it there.
;
; THE FOUR CONTRACTS, which are exactly sdio.pi4's so that the four
; @-references can be passed with nothing in between:
;
;     Procedure.i Rb(fn.i, addr.i)
;        one byte from function fn's register space.
;        Returns 0..255, or NEGATIVE on failure.
;     Procedure.i Wb(fn.i, addr.i, val.i)
;        one byte in. Returns 1 or 0.
;     Procedure.i Rd(fn.i, addr.i, *buf, nbytes.i, incr.i)
;     Procedure.i Wr(fn.i, addr.i, *buf, nbytes.i, incr.i)
;        nbytes bytes, a multiple of 4, *buf 4-byte aligned.
;        incr <> 0 walks the card address; 0 hammers one address.
;        Returns 1 or 0.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT HAS RUN ON SILICON, AND WHAT HAS NOT. Corrected 2026-08-27.
; ======================================================================
; THIS BANNER USED TO READ "NOTHING IN THIS FILE HAS RUN ON SILICON",
; which was true when it was written on 2026-08-25 and has been false
; since 2026-08-26. It is corrected rather than deleted, because a
; banner claiming LESS than the truth is not harmless: it sends the next
; reader back to the datasheet to re-derive ground that has already been
; paid for on a bench. The vault carries a standing warning about
; exactly these banners in exactly this tree - see the note "Silicon
; results 2026-08-26".
;
; PROVEN ON THE BOARD. A full bring-up in one run on 2026-08-27:
; chip id $4345 rev 6, so cyw43_ReadChipId and the ALP clock handshake
; work; the EROM walk; F1 block 64 and F2 block 512 read back off the
; card; Cyw43PrepareDownload with ram base $00198000 and 819,200 bytes;
; 609,309 bytes of firmware uploaded AND READ BACK clean, reset vector
; $B83EF198; 1,748 cooked NVRAM bytes likewise; Cyw43Start, Cyw43HtClock
; and Cyw43AnnounceHost; and then F2 came ready - which is the firmware
; on the chip saying it booted.
;
; NOT PROVEN ON THE BOARD: EVERYTHING BELOW THE SDPCM TRANSPORT HEADING.
; The first attempt stopped the board dead inside Cyw43SetEventMask on
; an unaligned store, fixed offline - the whole argument is at
; cyw43_PutLE32. As of this line NO ioctl has ever had a reply from real
; firmware, no event has been decoded off the air, and no scan result
; has existed anywhere but in a model. The frame counters, the bss_info
; offsets and the escan layout are transcription plus a gate, and
; nothing stronger. Do not let the bring-up above lend them credit.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE PART
; ======================================================================
; A Raspberry Pi 4 Model B carries a Cypress (now Infineon) CYW43455:
; 802.11ac plus Bluetooth, on SDIO for the WiFi half and on the PL011
; UART for the Bluetooth half. Only the SDIO half is this file's
; business.
;
; ITS CHIP ID IS $4345, WHICH IS NOT 43455.
; Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmu_brcm_hw_ids.h line 34:
;
;     #define BRCM_CC_4345_CHIP_ID   0x4345
;
; and the "455" is a package and revision variant selected by the chip
; REVISION, not by the ID. Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.c
; lines 651-653:
;
;     BRCMF_FW_ENTRY(BRCM_CC_4345_CHIP_ID, 0x00000200, 43456),
;     BRCMF_FW_ENTRY(BRCM_CC_4345_CHIP_ID, 0xFFFFFDC0, 43455),
;     BRCMF_FW_ENTRY(BRCM_CC_43454_CHIP_ID, 0x00000040, 43455),
;
; THE SECOND FIELD IS A BITMASK OVER REVISION NUMBERS, not a revision.
; Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_firmware.h line 22 says so in
; as many words - "@revmask: bitmask of revisions, e.g. 0x10 means rev 4
; only, 0xf means rev 0-3" - and
; Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_firmware.c line 813 is the
; selection itself:
;
;     mapping_table[i].revmask & BIT(chiprev)
;
; So $FFFFFDC0 covers revisions 6, 7, 8 and 10 upward - everything
; except 0-5 and 9 - and that is the "43455" firmware; $00000200 is bit
; 9 alone, and that is the CYW43456, a different part. A chip reporting
; id $4345 and revision 6 IS a CYW43455, and anybody expecting to read
; 43455 out of a register will spend an afternoon deciding the bus is
; broken. Cyw43IsCyw43455() below encodes exactly that rule and nothing
; more.
;
; READING THE TABLE TOP-DOWN AND TAKING THE FIRST $4345 ENTRY GIVES THE
; WRONG ANSWER - it gives the 43456, because that entry comes first.
; That is not hypothetical: the gate for this file did exactly that on
; its first run and announced that a revision-6 part needed
; brcmfmac43456-sdio.bin.
;
; The device tree agrees the part is there and calls it a 4329, which is
; also not a lie - "brcm,bcm4329-fmac" is the generic compatible string
; brcmfmac binds to for every part in the family.
; Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711-rpi-ds.dtsi lines 193-196.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE THREE SDIO FUNCTIONS
; ======================================================================
; A CYW43-family part presents itself as an SDIO card with two I/O
; functions on top of the mandatory function 0:
;
;   F0   the CCCR. Standard SDIO housekeeping, plus three
;        Broadcom-private registers at $F0..$F2 that this file does not
;        touch (SDIO_CCCR_BRCM_CARDCAP and friends,
;        Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.h lines 33-49).
;
;   F1   THE BACKPLANE. This is the one that matters here. Its 17-bit
;        address space is a WINDOW onto the chip's internal 32-bit
;        backplane, plus a block of function-1 control registers at
;        $10000..$1001F (SBSDIO_FUNC1_MISC_REG_START and _LIMIT,
;        brcmfmac/sdio.h lines 116-117).
;
;   F2   WLAN DATA. Packets in and out, once there is firmware to
;        produce them. Nothing here uses it, and see THE WALL.
;
; brcmfmac never names these numerically - it carries sdiodev->func1 and
; sdiodev->func2 - but the numbering is forced: SDIO_FUNC_ENABLE_1 is
; $02 and SDIO_FUNC_ENABLE_2 is $04 (brcmfmac/sdio.h lines 16-17), which
; are bit 1 and bit 2 of CCCR IOEx, i.e. functions 1 and 2.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE BACKPLANE WINDOW - THE ONE MECHANISM WORTH UNDERSTANDING
; ======================================================================
; The chip's internal bus is 32-bit addressed. SDIO gives 17 bits. The
; bridge is a sliding window, and it is three CMD52 writes plus a flag.
;
; Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_bcmsdh.c lines 223-242,
; brcmf_sdiod_set_backplane_window():
;
;     u32 v, bar0 = addr & SBSDIO_SBWINDOW_MASK;
;     if (bar0 == sdiodev->sbwad) return 0;
;     v = bar0 >> 8;
;     for (i = 0 ; i < 3 && !err ; i++, v >>= 8)
;         brcmf_sdiod_writeb(sdiodev, SBSDIO_FUNC1_SBADDRLOW + i,
;                            v & 0xff, &err);
;     if (!err) sdiodev->sbwad = bar0;
;
; and then lines 243-262, brcmf_sdiod_readl():
;
;     addr &= SBSDIO_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK;
;     addr |= SBSDIO_SB_ACCESS_2_4B_FLAG;
;     data = sdio_readl(sdiodev->func1, addr, &retval);
;
; READ THAT CAREFULLY, because three things in it are easy to get wrong
; and each produces a different flavour of silent nonsense:
;
;   1. THE WINDOW IS 32 KiB, NOT 64 KiB. SBSDIO_SBWINDOW_MASK is
;      $FFFF8000 and SBSDIO_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK is $07FFF
;      (brcmfmac/sdio.h lines 128 and 122). Fifteen bits of offset.
;      Assume 16 and every access above the halfway mark of a window
;      lands in the next one.
;
;   2. THE WINDOW REGISTERS TAKE THE ADDRESS SHIFTED RIGHT BY EIGHT.
;      "v = bar0 >> 8" before the loop. SBADDRLOW is address bits 15:8,
;      SBADDRMID is 23:16, SBADDRHIGH is 31:24. Writing the unshifted
;      address puts the window a factor of 256 away from where it was
;      asked for, at an address that usually still reads as zeroes.
;
;   3. BIT 15 OF THE SDIO ADDRESS MEANS "THIS IS A 2- OR 4-BYTE ACCESS".
;      SBSDIO_SB_ACCESS_2_4B_FLAG is $08000 - the bit immediately above
;      the 15-bit offset (brcmfmac/sdio.h line 125). Without it a
;      four-byte CMD53 to the backplane is not a 32-bit backplane read;
;      it is four byte reads, which the backplane does not always
;      present the same way. This file sets it on every 32-bit access,
;      and nowhere else.
;
; THE CACHED WINDOW IS AN OPTIMISATION WITH TEETH. Skipping the three
; writes when the window has not moved saves three CMD52s per access,
; which is most of the cost of a backplane read. It is also a cache of
; hardware state held in software, and if anything else ever writes
; those registers it goes stale and every subsequent read comes from the
; wrong place while looking perfectly healthy. Cyw43InvalidateWindow()
; exists so that anything which might have disturbed them can say so.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE CLOCK - WHY THE FIRST THING TO DO IS ASK FOR ALP
; ======================================================================
; A CYW43 part comes out of reset with its backplane clock off. The chip
; ID register does not read as garbage in that state; it reads as
; nothing, because the block that would answer is not clocked. So the
; order is: ask for a clock, wait for the chip to say it has one, and
; only then read anything.
;
; Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.c lines 3843-3886,
; brcmf_sdio_buscoreprep(), and it is worth having the whole shape:
;
;     clkset = SBSDIO_FORCE_HW_CLKREQ_OFF | SBSDIO_ALP_AVAIL_REQ;
;     write CHIPCLKCSR = clkset
;     clkval = read CHIPCLKCSR
;     if ((clkval & ~SBSDIO_AVBITS) != clkset)  -> -EACCES
;     SPINWAIT(!SBSDIO_ALPAV(clkval), PMU_MAX_TRANSITION_DLY)
;     if (!SBSDIO_ALPAV(clkval))                -> -EBUSY
;     clkset = SBSDIO_FORCE_HW_CLKREQ_OFF | SBSDIO_FORCE_ALP;
;     write CHIPCLKCSR = clkset
;     udelay(65)
;     write SBSDIO_FUNC1_SDIOPULLUP = 0
;
; FOUR THINGS IN THAT ARE NOT OBVIOUS:
;
;   * THE READ-BACK CHECK IS THE ONLY PROOF THE BUS WORKS AT ALL. It
;     masks off the two AVAILABLE status bits (SBSDIO_AVBITS, sdio.c
;     line 193) and demands the rest reads back exactly as written. A
;     bus that is not working returns $00 or $FF and fails this
;     immediately, before anything has been believed. It is the single
;     most valuable line in the sequence and it is the one a hurried
;     port leaves out.
;
;   * ALP IS THE *SLOW* CLOCK AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. ALP is the
;     crystal; HT is the PLL. Asking for HT before the PMU is programmed
;     is asking for a clock that cannot start. The driver's own comment
;     at sdio.c line 3968-3971 says "Force PLL off until
;     brcmf_chip_attach() programs PLL control regs".
;
;   * FORCE_HW_CLKREQ_OFF SQUELCHES THE HARDWARE'S OWN REQUESTS
;     (sdio.c lines 186-187) so that what software asked for is what the
;     chip does. Without it the clock state is a race between the driver
;     and the chip's own logic.
;
;   * THE 65-MICROSECOND DELAY IS NOT A GUESS AND IT IS NOT ROUNDED. It
;     is "udelay(65)" at sdio.c line 3881, immediately after the switch
;     to FORCE_ALP, and it is there because the clock mux takes time to
;     settle. Rounding it to 100 would be harmless; dropping it is what
;     produces an intermittent chip ID of zero.
;
; THE SPIN BOUND. SPINWAIT's limit is PMU_MAX_TRANSITION_DLY. There are
; TWO definitions of that name in this source tree and they differ by a
; factor of sixty-six:
;
;     brcmu_chipcommon.h line 309:  15000
;     brcmfmac/sdio.c lines 311-312: #undef, then 1000000
;
; The #undef is deliberate and sdio.c's value wins for SDIO parts - one
; second, in microseconds. Its own comment at sdio.c line 3856 says the
; wait "may take up to 15 milliseconds", which is the other number, so
; the driver allows sixty-six times the expected worst case. This file
; uses the SDIO value, and says so, because picking the smaller one
; would make a slow-but-healthy part look dead.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE CORE TABLE - WHY THE CHIP IS WALKED RATHER THAN TABULATED
; ======================================================================
; The chip is a collection of cores on an AXI backplane, and where each
; one lives is not a constant - it is read out of the chip, from a table
; called the EROM. Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_chip.c lines
; 904-960, brcmf_chip_dmp_erom_scan(), starting from
;
;     eromaddr = read32(CORE_CC_REG(enum_base, eromptr));
;
; where eromptr is offset $FC in the ChipCommon core
; (Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmu_chipcommon.h line 107) and
; enum_base is $18000000 (v6.12_brcmu_soc.h line 9, SI_ENUM_BASE_DEFAULT
; - returned unconditionally by brcmf_chip_enum_base, chip.c lines
; 963-966).
;
; A HARD-CODED TABLE OF CORE ADDRESSES WOULD BE THE WRONG ANSWER even
; though it would work on this exact part. brcmfmac supports a dozen
; chips through one walker precisely because the addresses move between
; revisions, and this project's rule is to cite rather than to guess -
; a table of addresses read off somebody's forum post is a guess with a
; confident tone.
;
; WHAT IS IMPLEMENTED HERE IS THE COMPONENT PASS ONLY, and that is a
; deliberate reduction, stated so nobody assumes otherwise:
;
;   * every component descriptor is decoded: part number, revision,
;     and the wrapper/port counts;
;   * for each, the FIRST 4K or 8K slave address descriptor is taken as
;     the core's register base;
;   * the WRAPPER base is recorded when one is seen, and it is not
;     hunted for the way brcmf_chip_dmp_get_regaddr() hunts for it
;     (chip.c lines 832-899, which loops until BOTH a regbase and a
;     wrapbase have been found).
;
; The wrapper is what a driver uses to RESET a core. Nothing here resets
; anything - see THE WALL - so hunting for a wrapper this file could
; never use would be code with no way to be wrong out loud.
;
; SOCI_AI ONLY. chip.c lines 21-22 define SOCI_SB 0 and SOCI_AI 1, and
; lines 998-1002 refuse an SB chip that is not a 4329. The 4345 is AXI,
; so this file refuses anything that is not SOCI_AI rather than
; pretending the SB layout is close enough. It is not; SB has no EROM at
; all and its core addresses are the hard-coded constants at chip.c
; lines 1005-1019.
;
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  THE FIRMWARE. WHAT USED TO BE CALLED THE WALL, AND IS NOT ONE.
;
;  THIS SECTION SAID THE OPPOSITE UNTIL 2026-08-26 and the old text is
;  quoted rather than deleted, because a reader who met the refusal on a
;  bench and comes back to this file deserves to see what changed and
;  why - and because the reasoning was correct on the facts it had.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;
; THE PART OF IT THAT WAS ALWAYS TRUE, and still is:
;
; The CYW43455 has no on-chip firmware. Its internal ARM core is held in
; reset with empty RAM, and it stays that way until a host writes a
; binary image into that RAM over this same SDIO bus and then releases
; the core at a reset vector taken from the image's first four bytes.
;
; THAT IS NOT AN OPINION. Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.c
; lines 3384-3421, brcmf_sdio_download_firmware():
;
;     rstvec = get_unaligned_le32(fw->data);
;     brcmf_dbg(SDIO, "firmware rstvec: %x\n", rstvec);
;     bcmerror = brcmf_sdio_download_code_file(bus, fw);
;     ...
;     bcmerror = brcmf_sdio_download_nvram(bus, nvram, nvlen);
;     ...
;     /* Take arm out of reset */
;     if (!brcmf_chip_set_active(bus->ci, rstvec)) {
;         brcmf_err("error getting out of ARM core reset\n");
;
; and lines 3346-3363, which is the write itself:
;
;     err = brcmf_sdiod_ramrw(bus->sdiodev, true, bus->ci->rambase,
;                             (u8 *)fw->data, fw->size);
;
; Read the ordering: THE RESET VECTOR IS DATA INSIDE THE BLOB. There is
; no entry point to jump to that a driver could know on its own.
;
; THE THREE FILES, for this exact part, from sdio.c line 652, the naming
; convention at sdio.c lines 630-633, and the CLM at line 4225:
;
;     brcmfmac43455-sdio.bin       the firmware image, 609,309 bytes
;     brcmfmac43455-sdio.txt       the board NVRAM, 2,074 bytes, written
;                                  to the TOP of the chip's RAM (sdio.c
;                                  line 3373: ramsize - varsz + rambase)
;     brcmfmac43455-sdio.clm_blob  the regulatory tables, 2,676 bytes,
;                                  and OPTIONAL - brcmfmac marks it
;                                  BRCMF_FW_REQF_OPTIONAL
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TWO REASONS IT WAS A BLOCKER, AND WHAT HAPPENED TO EACH
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; ONE - "THE FILE IS NOT ON THIS DISK." True on 2026-08-25 and false the
; next day. All three are now IN THIS REPOSITORY at
; RaspberryPi4/Reference/firmware/, downloaded from RPi-Distro's
; firmware-nonfree, bookworm branch, with the licence text they must
; travel with beside them. That folder's README records the two traps
; that were hit getting them: the obvious filenames upstream are 22-byte
; git symlinks, and the image is called cyfmac43455-sdio-standard.bin
; rather than brcmfmac43455-sdio.bin.
;
; TWO - "THE LICENCE PERMITS REDISTRIBUTION, BUT NOT SILENTLY, AND THAT
; IS A DECISION NOBODY HERE IS ENTITLED TO MAKE." The decision has been
; made, and the analysis that stalled it had one specific error in it
; worth naming, because it is a common one:
;
;     "NON-FREE" AND "NON-DISTRIBUTABLE" ARE DIFFERENT THINGS.
;
; Debian's non-free-firmware component means NO SOURCE. It does not mean
; no redistribution - Debian keeps genuinely undistributable things out
; of the archive entirely, so the fact that Debian ships these files and
; RPi-Distro hosts them in a public repository is itself the answer. The
; old text here called them "Cypress binaries under a proprietary
; end-user licence" and treated that as a wall. Proprietary, yes.
; Undistributable, no.
;
; The grant, from RaspberryPi4/Reference/firmware/LICENCE-firmware-nonfree.txt,
; which is Debian's own copyright file for these exact files:
;
;   :588-596   the 43455 files are licensed "binary-redist-Cypress" -
;              the name says it
;   :1974-1977 "Cypress ... grants you a non-exclusive, non-transferable
;              license under its copyright rights in the Software to
;              reproduce and distribute the Software in object code form
;              only, solely for use in connection with Cypress
;              integrated circuit products"
;   :602-606   the NVRAM .txt is GPL-2, copyright Linaro Ltd and
;              Raspberry Pi (Trading) Ltd. Not Cypress at all.
;
; "Solely for use in connection with Cypress integrated circuit
; products" is exactly what a Pi 4's CYW43455 is. THE GRANT IS
; CONDITIONAL and the conditions are met by keeping the licence file
; beside the binaries, which is why it is in that folder and not
; referenced from somewhere else. Keep them together. No reverse
; engineering, decompilation or disassembly of those three files.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT IS AND IS NOT REACHABLE NOW, STATED PLAINLY
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; IMPLEMENTED HERE, and each step is separately callable so that a
; diagnostic can put a line against it:
;     enable F1 and F2, set their block sizes, READ THE F1 SIZE BACK
;     bring the ALP clock up and PROVE the bus works by reading a
;         register back
;     read the chip ID, revision, package and backplane type
;     walk the EROM and list every core with its base and wrapper
;     bulk transfer across the backplane, byte mode or block mode, with
;         the 32 KB window handled                    Cyw43ReadMem/WriteMem
;     put the ARM CR4 down halted and the d11 core with it, then ask the
;         chip how much RAM it has                    Cyw43PrepareDownload
;     write the image at the RAM base and verify it   Cyw43UploadFirmware
;     cook the NVRAM text, write it at the top of RAM with its token,
;         and verify that too                         Cyw43UploadNvram
;     release the ARM at the image's own reset vector Cyw43Start
;     force the HT clock                              Cyw43HtClock
;     announce the host protocol version              Cyw43AnnounceHost
;
; NOT IMPLEMENTED, AND NOT FOR THE OLD REASON: any scan, any join, any
; frame in either direction, the CLM upload, the country code, and the
; MAC address as the firmware reports it. Every one of those is an
; ioctl or an event exchange inside the SDPCM framing layer, and that
; layer is not in this file. It is not a wall, it is a queue: the
; transport exists twice on this disk, bench-proven through WPA2, DHCP
; and TLS 1.3, at RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico line 2544 and its RP2350 twin.
;
; THE MILESTONE THAT SEPARATES THE TWO LISTS IS FUNCTION 2 GOING READY.
; There is no mailbox, no magic word and no version read: after the ARM
; is released, the host enables F2 and polls CCCR IORx for bit 2. That
; bit going from 0 to 1 is the firmware saying it booted. Before the
; upload it reads 0 and that is CORRECT - a bench log showing
; "enable F2 (want 1) 0" is a chip with no firmware in it, not a fault.
;
; A DRIVER THAT PRETENDED OTHERWISE WOULD BE WORSE THAN NONE. That
; sentence survives from the old text unchanged, and it is the reason
; the list above is split rather than blurred.
;
; ======================================================================
; CITATIONS
; ======================================================================
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.h
;       every SBSDIO_ register and CHIPCLKCSR bit. Lines inline.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.c
;       the CHIPCLKCSR bit values, buscoreprep, the firmware table and
;       the firmware download sequence.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_bcmsdh.c
;       the backplane window, the 32-bit access form, the block sizes.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_chip.c
;       SOCI_SB/SOCI_AI, chip recognition, the DMP descriptor format,
;       the EROM walk.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmu_chipcommon.h
;       the ChipCommon register layout, CID_* masks, PMU_MAX_TRANSITION_DLY.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmu_soc.h
;       SI_ENUM_BASE_DEFAULT.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmu_brcm_hw_ids.h
;       BRCM_CC_4345_CHIP_ID.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_firmware.h
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_firmware.c
;       revmask is a bitmask over revisions, and the selection rule.
;       ALSO the whole NVRAM parser: the state machine at lines 87-185,
;       is_nvram_char and is_whitespace at 72-85, and the padding and
;       token arithmetic at 434-446.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/RPi-Distro_firmware-nonfree_bookworm_debian_copyright
;       the 43455 firmware's licence. The SAME DOCUMENT is now also in
;       this repository, beside the binaries it covers, at
;       RaspberryPi4/Reference/firmware/LICENCE-firmware-nonfree.txt -
;       and it is there rather than only here because the Cypress grant
;       is conditional on the notice travelling with the files.
;   RaspberryPi4/Reference/bcma_regs.h
;       BCMA_IOCTL $0408, BCMA_IOST $0500, BCMA_RESET_CTL $0800,
;       BCMA_RESET_ST $0804 and their bits. Fetched 2026-08-26; brcmfmac
;       uses these names throughout chip.c and defines none of them.
;   RaspberryPi4/Reference/bcma.h
;       the BCMA_CORE_* identifiers, which is how the EROM walk's raw
;       numbers become the ARM CR4, the 802.11 core and the SDIO device
;       core. Fetched with the file above.
;   RaspberryPi4/Reference/firmware/README.md
;       what the three blobs are, where they came from, the two
;       download traps, and the licence argument in full.
;   Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711-rpi-ds.dtsi
;       the wifi@1 node under mmcnr.
;   CNTPCT_EL0 / CNTFRQ_EL0
;       ARMv8-A architectural registers, for the bounded waits. Same
;       form as sdio.pi4, emmc.pi4 and safety.pi4.
;
;   NOT CITED AND THEREFORE NOT PRESENT: the SDPCM header format, the
;   ioctl/iovar protocol, the event framing, the CLM blob format, and
;   the PMU chip-control tables. Core reset used to be on this list and
;   came off it on 2026-08-26, when bcma_regs.h arrived.
; ======================================================================


; ======================================================================
;  SDIO function numbers. See THE THREE SDIO FUNCTIONS.
; ======================================================================
; CCCR registers this file reads directly. From
; Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h, line given as "q:".
#CYW43_CCCR_CAPS    = $08    ; SDIO_CCCR_CAPS            q:108
#CYW43_CCCR_CAP_SMB = $02    ; SDIO_CCCR_CAP_SMB         q:111
                             ; "can do multi-block xfers (CMD53)"

#CYW43_FN_CCCR      = 0
#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE = 1      ; SDIO_FUNC_ENABLE_1 $02 = bit 1  s:16
#CYW43_FN_WLAN      = 2      ; SDIO_FUNC_ENABLE_2 $04 = bit 2  s:17

; Block sizes, from bcmsdh.c lines 44-45. Set on the card so its
; firmware frames correctly; nothing here uses block mode.
#CYW43_F1_BLKSIZE = 64       ; SDIO_FUNC1_BLOCKSIZE   b:44
#CYW43_F2_BLKSIZE = 512      ; SDIO_FUNC2_BLOCKSIZE   b:45

; ======================================================================
;  Function-1 control registers. All from
;  Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.h, line given.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_SBADDRLOW   = $1000A  ; SBSDIO_FUNC1_SBADDRLOW    s:75
#CYW43_SBADDRMID   = $1000B  ; SBSDIO_FUNC1_SBADDRMID    s:77
#CYW43_SBADDRHIGH  = $1000C  ; SBSDIO_FUNC1_SBADDRHIGH   s:79
#CYW43_FRAMECTRL   = $1000D  ; SBSDIO_FUNC1_FRAMECTRL    s:81  unused
#CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR  = $1000E  ; SBSDIO_FUNC1_CHIPCLKCSR   s:83
#CYW43_SDIOPULLUP  = $1000F  ; SBSDIO_FUNC1_SDIOPULLUP   s:85

#CYW43_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK = $00007FFF  ; SBSDIO_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK   s:122
#CYW43_SB_OFT_ADDR_LIMIT = $00008000 ; SBSDIO_SB_OFT_ADDR_LIMIT  s:123
#CYW43_SB_ACCESS_2_4B   = $00008000  ; SBSDIO_SB_ACCESS_2_4B_FLAG s:125
#CYW43_SBWINDOW_MASK    = $FFFF8000  ; SBSDIO_SBWINDOW_MASK      s:128
; SB_OFT_ADDR_LIMIT AND SB_ACCESS_2_4B_FLAG ARE THE SAME NUMBER, $8000,
; and they are not the same thing. The first is the SIZE of the window -
; fifteen bits of offset, so 32 KB - and the second is bit 15 of the
; seventeen-bit SDIO address field, which asks the card for a 4-byte
; access. They coincide because the offset uses bits 14:0 and the flag
; is the next bit up. Both names are kept because a windowing loop needs
; the size and every transfer needs the flag, and folding them into one
; constant would make a future edit to either one silently change the
; other.

; ======================================================================
;  AXI backplane wrapper registers, for holding a core in reset and
;  letting it go. RaspberryPi4/Reference/bcma_regs.h, fetched 2026-08-26
;  because brcmfmac uses these names and defines none of them.
;
;  EVERY ONE OF THESE IS AN OFFSET FROM A CORE'S *WRAPPER* BASE, NOT ITS
;  REGISTER BASE. On this board those differ by $00100000 - core 2 is at
;  $18002000 with its wrapper at $18102000 - so getting it wrong does not
;  fault, it writes to a completely different core's registers. That is
;  the single most expensive mistake available in this section, which is
;  why Cyw43CoreWrap() is what the reset code reads and why a core with
;  no wrapper is refused rather than defaulted to its base.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL        = $0408   ; BCMA_IOCTL             r:29
#CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_CLK    = $0001   ; BCMA_IOCTL_CLK         r:30
#CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_FGC    = $0002   ; BCMA_IOCTL_FGC         r:31
#CYW43_BCMA_IOST         = $0500   ; BCMA_IOST              r:35
#CYW43_BCMA_RESET_CTL    = $0800   ; BCMA_RESET_CTL         r:41
#CYW43_BCMA_RESET_ACTIVE = $0001   ; BCMA_RESET_CTL_RESET   r:42
#CYW43_BCMA_RESET_ST     = $0804   ; BCMA_RESET_ST          r:43

; ======================================================================
;  Core identifiers. RaspberryPi4/Reference/bcma.h, fetched with the
;  file above and for the same reason.
;
;  ONLY THE ONES THIS FILE ACTS ON ARE NAMED. There are ninety in that
;  header; a table of the other eighty-two would be eighty-two chances
;  to mistype a number nothing checks.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_CORE_CHIPCOMMON   = $800    ; BCMA_CORE_CHIPCOMMON     m:97
#CYW43_CORE_INTERNAL_MEM = $80E    ; BCMA_CORE_INTERNAL_MEM   m:111  SOCSRAM
#CYW43_CORE_80211        = $812    ; BCMA_CORE_80211          m:115
#CYW43_CORE_PMU          = $827    ; BCMA_CORE_PMU            m:136
#CYW43_CORE_SDIO_DEV     = $829    ; BCMA_CORE_SDIO_DEV       m:138
#CYW43_CORE_ARM_CR4      = $83E    ; BCMA_CORE_ARM_CR4        m:155
#CYW43_CORE_GCI          = $840    ; BCMA_CORE_GCI            m:156
#CYW43_CORE_ARM_CA7      = $847    ; BCMA_CORE_ARM_CA7        m:158
#CYW43_CORE_SYS_MEM      = $849    ; BCMA_CORE_SYS_MEM        m:159

; The 802.11 core's two IOCTL bits, chip.c lines 81-82. Used only as the
; prereset / reset / postreset arguments when the d11 cores are put down
; before a firmware download.
#CYW43_D11_PHYCLOCKEN    = $0004   ; D11_BCMA_IOCTL_PHYCLOCKEN  k:81
#CYW43_D11_PHYRESET      = $0008   ; D11_BCMA_IOCTL_PHYRESET    k:82

; ======================================================================
;  The ARM CR4's own registers, at the CORE base this time and not the
;  wrapper. chip.c lines 78 and 205-217.
;
;  These are how the chip is asked how much RAM it has. brcmfmac does
;  not carry a table of RAM sizes - it walks the CR4's memory banks and
;  adds them up - and neither does this file, for the reason the header
;  gives about every other number here: a size that is asked for cannot
;  be wrong about a part it has never seen.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_ARMCR4_CAP        = $04     ; ARMCR4_CAP              k:205
#CYW43_ARMCR4_BANKIDX    = $40     ; ARMCR4_BANKIDX          k:206
#CYW43_ARMCR4_BANKINFO   = $44     ; ARMCR4_BANKINFO         k:207
#CYW43_ARMCR4_TCBBNB_MASK = $00F0  ; ARMCR4_TCBBNB_MASK      k:210
#CYW43_ARMCR4_TCBBNB_S   = 4       ; ARMCR4_TCBBNB_SHIFT     k:211
#CYW43_ARMCR4_TCBANB_MASK = $000F  ; ARMCR4_TCBANB_MASK      k:212
#CYW43_ARMCR4_TCBANB_S   = 0       ; ARMCR4_TCBANB_SHIFT     k:213
#CYW43_ARMCR4_BSZ_MASK   = $007F   ; ARMCR4_BSZ_MASK         k:215
#CYW43_ARMCR4_BSZ_MULT   = 8192    ; ARMCR4_BSZ_MULT         k:216
#CYW43_ARMCR4_BLK_1K     = $0200   ; ARMCR4_BLK_1K_MASK      k:217
; The CPU halt bit, in the WRAPPER's IOCTL register even though it is
; named after the CR4. chip.c line 78.
#CYW43_ARMCR4_CPUHALT    = $0020   ; ARMCR4_BCMA_IOCTL_CPUHALT k:78

; Where the firmware image goes on THIS part, and it is a table lookup
; in brcmfmac rather than anything the chip reports. chip.c lines
; 713-714 give $198000 for BRCM_CC_4345_CHIP_ID and BRCM_CC_43454.
; NOTHING ELSE IN THAT TABLE APPLIES HERE and none of it is copied:
; twenty other chips have twenty other bases and this file supports one
; part. A chip that is not a $4345 gets a refusal, not a guess.
#CYW43_RAMBASE_4345      = $00198000  ; k:713-714
; BRCMF_CHIP_MAX_MEMSIZE, chip.c line 94. A sanity bound on the walked
; bank total, and brcmfmac refuses above it too (chip.c line 804).
#CYW43_MAX_MEMSIZE       = 4194304    ; k:94

; The SDIO device core's intstatus, offset $020 in struct sdpcmd_regs
; (v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.h lines 199-216). brcmf_sdio_buscore_activate()
; writes $FFFFFFFF here to clear every interrupt immediately before the
; ARM is released - sdio.c lines 3893-3895.
#CYW43_SD_INTSTATUS      = $020       ; s:216

; MEMBLOCK, sdio.c line 131 - "Block size used for downloading of dongle
; image". 2048 is the size of ONE readback compare buffer in
; brcmf_sdio_verifymemory, not a bus limit.
#CYW43_MEMBLOCK          = 2048       ; c:131

; How many blocks one CMD53 can carry. The block count in the argument
; is nine bits and 0 means the infinite form, so 511.
;
; THIS IS THE SAME NUMBER AS sdio.pi4's #SDIO_BLOCKS_MAX AND IT IS
; DELIBERATELY NOT SHARED. A library never includes a library, so the
; alternative would be for this file to read a constant it cannot see;
; the only honest options are to duplicate it with the same citation or
; to add a fifth accessor to the seam. It is duplicated, because both
; copies come from the SAME line - v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_ops.c line 142,
;     cmd.arg |= 0x08000000 | blocks;
; which has no special case for 512 and no room for one. If the two ever
; disagree the transfers do not silently misbehave: this file would ask
; for more blocks than sdio.pi4 accepts and get #SDIO_ERR_BAD_COUNT
; back, which surfaces here as #CYW43_ERR_XFER on the first bulk call.
#CYW43_BLOCKS_PER_CMD    = 511        ; sdio_ops.c:142

; ======================================================================
;  The last few registers, all for the step between "the image is in
;  RAM" and "F2 says the firmware is running".
; ======================================================================
; Two more function-1 registers. Named here rather than with the block
; at the top because nothing above the wall ever touched them.
#CYW43_SBSDIO_WATERMARK  = $10008     ; SBSDIO_WATERMARK        s:70
#CYW43_SBSDIO_DEVICE_CTL = $10009     ; SBSDIO_DEVICE_CTL       s:72

; Offsets inside the SDIO device core's register block, struct
; sdpcmd_regs at v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.h lines 199-227. Counted from the
; struct, not guessed: intstatus is commented "0x020" and tosbmailbox
; "0x040" in the header itself, and the four mailbox words follow in
; order, so tosbmailboxdata is $048.
#CYW43_SD_INTSTATUS_OFF  = $020       ; s:216
#CYW43_SD_HOSTINTMASK    = $024       ; s:217
#CYW43_SD_TOSBMBDATA     = $048       ; s:225

; The host protocol version the driver announces to the firmware, and
; where in the mailbox word it goes. sdio.c lines 290 and 260.
#CYW43_SDPCM_PROT_VERSION = 4         ; c:290
#CYW43_SMB_DATA_VERSION_S = 16        ; c:260

; HOSTINTMASK, sdio.c line 780: I_HMB_SW_MASK | I_CHIPACTIVE.
; I_HMB_SW_MASK is $000000F0 (c:211) and I_CHIPACTIVE is 1 << 29
; (c:229), so the composed value is $200000F0. It is written out whole
; because the two halves come from lines sixteen apart in the source and
; an Or at the point of use would put the citation in the wrong place.
#CYW43_HOSTINTMASK       = $200000F0  ; c:780, c:211, c:229

; How long to wait for the HT clock. PMU_MAX_TRANSITION_DLY again -
; sdio.c lines 311-312 - the same one microsecond-count the ALP wait
; uses, and brcmf_sdio_htclk's own timeout at sdio.c lines 845-846.
#CYW43_HT_WAIT_US        = 1000000    ; c:312, c:845-846

; ======================================================================
;  Characters the NVRAM parser has to recognise, by CODE.
;
;  Named constants because this project does not use character literals,
;  and because a bare 61 in the middle of a state machine is unreadable
;  in a way that #CYW43_CH_EQUALS is not. The values are ASCII and the
;  parser they serve is brcmf_nvram_handle_* in firmware.c, whose own
;  predicates are quoted where they are used.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_CH_NUL    = 0
#CYW43_CH_TAB    = 9
#CYW43_CH_LF     = 10
#CYW43_CH_CR     = 13
#CYW43_CH_SPACE  = 32
#CYW43_CH_HASH   = 35
#CYW43_CH_EQUALS = 61
; is_nvram_char(), firmware.c lines 72-80: printable ASCII, $20 to $7E
; inclusive, with '#' excluded because it opens a comment.
#CYW43_CH_PRINT_LO = 32
#CYW43_CH_PRINT_HI = 126

; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  THE SDPCM TRANSPORT - constants.
;
;  EVERY NUMBER IN THIS BLOCK COMES FROM A VENDOR HEADER, and the line
;  is given. That is not decoration on this section in particular: the
;  framing is four headers deep and each layer has its own idea of where
;  its fields are, so a constant recalled rather than read is a frame
;  that assembles, transmits, and is silently discarded by firmware that
;  will never say why.
;
;  Line-number prefixes used below:
;    c:   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.c
;    d:   RaspberryPi4/Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_bcdc.c
;    w:   RaspberryPi4/Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_fweh.h
;    l:   RaspberryPi4/Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_fwil.h
;    t:   RaspberryPi4/Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_fwil_types.h
;    u:   RaspberryPi4/Reference/v6.12_brcmu_wifi.h
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Layer 1 - the SDPCM header. Twelve bytes, little-endian.
;
;  DERIVED TWICE AND THE TWO AGREE, which is the only reason the byte
;  offsets below are written as byte offsets at all. brcmfmac reads the
;  same twelve bytes as a 4-byte hardware header plus two 32-bit
;  software words, and its masks (c:1333-1349) place every field:
;
;      SDPCM_HWHDR_LEN 4, SDPCM_SWHDR_LEN 8, SDPCM_HDRLEN 12  c:1328-1331
;      swheader = the u32 at +4:
;          SEQ_MASK     $000000FF shift 0    -> byte 4
;          CHANNEL_MASK $00000F00 shift 8    -> byte 5, low nibble
;          NEXTLEN_MASK $00FF0000 shift 16   -> byte 6
;          DOFFSET_MASK $FF000000 shift 24   -> byte 7
;      the second u32 at +8:
;          FCMASK_MASK  $000000FF shift 0    -> byte 8
;          WINDOW_MASK  $0000FF00 shift 8    -> byte 9
;
;  and RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico lines 2550-2557 lists the same twelve bytes
;  field by field from two other drivers. Two independent derivations
;  landing on the same layout is what this file accepts as settled.
;
;  THE HARDWARE HEADER IS A LENGTH AND ITS OWN COMPLEMENT. c:1381-1391:
;      len = get_unaligned_le16(header);
;      checksum = get_unaligned_le16(header + sizeof(u16));
;      if (!(len | checksum))  -> no more to read
;      if ((u16)(~(len ^ checksum)))  -> HW header checksum error
;  Note what the FIRST of those two tests means and do not lose it: an
;  all-zero header is not a corrupt frame, it is the documented way the
;  firmware says "there is nothing here". It is how this file polls.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_SDPCM_HWHDR    = 4          ; c:1328
#CYW43_SDPCM_SWHDR    = 8          ; c:1330
#CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN   = 12         ; c:1331
#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_LEN  = 0
#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_COMP = 2
#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_SEQ  = 4          ; c:1333
#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_CHAN = 5          ; c:1335-1336
#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_NEXT = 6          ; c:1343-1344
#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_DOFF = 7          ; c:1345-1346
#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_FC   = 8          ; c:1347
#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_WIN  = 9          ; c:1348-1349
#CYW43_SDPCM_CHAN_MASK = $0F       ; c:1335, after the shift

#CYW43_CHAN_CONTROL = 0            ; SDPCM_CONTROL_CHANNEL   c:1337
#CYW43_CHAN_EVENT   = 1            ; SDPCM_EVENT_CHANNEL     c:1338
#CYW43_CHAN_DATA    = 2            ; SDPCM_DATA_CHANNEL      c:1339
#CYW43_CHAN_GLOM    = 3            ; SDPCM_GLOM_CHANNEL      c:1340
#CYW43_CHAN_TEST    = 15           ; SDPCM_TEST_CHANNEL      c:1342

; BRCMF_FIRSTREAD, c:136, "(1 << 6)" = 64. The number of bytes read
; before the frame's own length is known. It must be at least
; SDPCM_HDRLEN and is 64 so that a short control reply arrives whole in
; one command.
#CYW43_FIRSTREAD = 64              ; c:136

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Layer 2 - the BCDC dcmd header. Sixteen bytes, little-endian, and it
;  sits immediately behind the SDPCM header on the CONTROL channel.
;
;  struct brcmf_proto_bcdc_dcmd, d:71-77:
;      __le32 cmd;     __le32 len;     __le32 flags;   __le32 status;
;
;  THE FLAGS WORD IS FOUR FIELDS AND THE ID IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS,
;  d:34-41:
;      BCDC_DCMD_ERROR  $01        1 = the firmware refused
;      BCDC_DCMD_SET    $02        0 = get, 1 = set
;      BCDC_DCMD_IF_MASK  $F000, shift 12
;      BCDC_DCMD_ID_MASK  $FFFF0000, shift 16
;
;  A REPLY IS MATCHED BY ID AND BY NOTHING ELSE. d:152 loops
;  `while (BCDC_DCMD_ID(...) != id)` - it reads and discards frames until
;  the id matches. That is the whole correlation mechanism and it is why
;  the id must never be 0: a zero id is indistinguishable from an
;  uninitialised header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_BCDC_HDRLEN    = 16
#CYW43_BCDC_OFF_CMD   = 0
#CYW43_BCDC_OFF_LEN   = 4
#CYW43_BCDC_OFF_FLAGS = 8
#CYW43_BCDC_OFF_STATUS = 12
#CYW43_BCDC_ERROR     = $01        ; d:34
#CYW43_BCDC_SET       = $02        ; d:35
#CYW43_BCDC_IF_SHIFT  = 12         ; d:37
#CYW43_BCDC_ID_SHIFT  = 16         ; d:39

; The whole control frame's header, both layers: 12 + 16 = 28.
;
; WRITTEN AS THE SUM, which is what it means. It was the literal 28 with the
; sum sitting in a comment beside it, because a constant declared in terms of
; other constants was refused - "Invalid constant declaration" - and a comment
; was the only place the arithmetic could then live. That refusal is gone
; (2026-09-05), and a sum that has to be re-checked by hand whenever either
; layer changes is exactly what the compiler should be doing instead.
#CYW43_CTRL_HDRLEN = #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BCDC_HDRLEN

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Layer 3 - the ioctl command numbers. l:12-79.
;
;  A SHORT LIST, AND TWO OF THESE ARE NOT ISSUED YET. fwil.h names about
;  seventy; a table of the rest would be seventy chances to mistype a
;  number nothing here would ever check. What is below is what this file
;  sends today - WLC_UP and SET_VAR - plus GET_VAR, which the next
;  caller will need and which is cited from the same line block, so that
;  adding a query does not mean reopening the header to find one number.
;  Nothing else is carried on speculation.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_C_UP          = 2           ; l:13   issued by Cyw43WifiUp
#CYW43_C_GET_VAR     = 262         ; l:78   not issued yet
#CYW43_C_SET_VAR     = 263         ; l:79   issued for every iovar

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE JOIN'S OWN IOCTLS. Added 2026-08-27 when association went in.
;
;  EVERY ONE IS TRIPLE-SOURCED - Linux's fwil.h, and the two drivers this
;  project already runs on silicon (RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:511-522 and
;  RP2350/Lib/cyw43.pico2). The Pico numbers matter more than they look:
;  they are not a second reading of the same header, they are numbers
;  that have been sending real frames to real access points for months.
;  Where a number below carries only one citation, it is because the
;  Pico driver does not send it either and it is quoted from fwil.h
;  alone - said so on the line rather than left to be assumed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_C_SET_INFRA    = 20    ; l:31   1 = infrastructure BSS. pico:513
#CYW43_C_SET_AUTH     = 22    ; l:33   0 = 802.11 OPEN SYSTEM. pico:514
#CYW43_C_GET_BSSID    = 23    ; l:34   6 raw bytes, or -17. pico:515
#CYW43_C_GET_SSID     = 25    ; l:36   36 bytes, the SET_SSID layout
#CYW43_C_SET_SSID     = 26    ; l:37   36 bytes - THE JOIN. pico:517
#CYW43_C_GET_CHANNEL  = 29    ; l:40   i32 LE. fwil.h only - the Pico
                              ;        driver never asks for it, so this
                              ;        one number has a single source
#CYW43_C_DISASSOC     = 52    ; l:52   zero length. pico:518
#CYW43_C_GET_RSSI     = 127   ; l:59   i32 LE, dBm, signed. pico:519
#CYW43_C_SET_WSEC     = 134   ; l:61   $04 = AES for WPA2. pico:520
#CYW43_C_SET_WPA_AUTH = 165   ; l:70   $0080 = WPA2_PSK. pico:521
#CYW43_C_SET_WSEC_PMK = 268   ; l:81   68 bytes, ALWAYS. pico:522

; The security values. pico:525-537, and each is one bit or one small
; enum in the firmware's own vocabulary.
#CYW43_WSEC_OPEN         = $00
#CYW43_WSEC_AES          = $04     ; WPA2-AES-PSK. TKIP ($02) is not
                                   ; offered: it is deprecated, this
                                   ; bench's AP does not need it, and an
                                   ; untested cipher in the table is a
                                   ; branch nobody has ever run
#CYW43_WPA_AUTH_DISABLED = $0000
#CYW43_WPA_AUTH_WPA2_PSK = $0080
#CYW43_MFP_CAPABLE       = 1
#CYW43_AUTH_OPEN         = 0       ; 802.11 OPEN SYSTEM. NOT "no
                                   ; authentication" - WPA2's
                                   ; authentication is the four-way
                                   ; handshake, not the 802.11 auth
                                   ; frames. 1 is WEP shared key and 3
                                   ; is WPA3 SAE
#CYW43_SUP_WPA_TMO       = 5000    ; ms the firmware's supplicant waits
                                   ; for the handshake. pico:544 - G's
                                   ; number, not E's 2500

; The two 802.11 length gates, and the PMK struct. pico:551-559.
#CYW43_PASS_MIN     = 8
#CYW43_PASS_MAX     = 63
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PMK STRUCT HAS TWO SIZES AND THE BENCH DECIDES WHICH.
;
;  MEASURED 2026-08-27: this firmware answered -2 BCME_BADARG to a
;  68-byte WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK. Note WHICH refusal that is - BADARG, not
;  -23 BCME_UNSUPPORTED. The ioctl EXISTS on this part; the argument was
;  the wrong size. Every other supplicant-shaped thing that day came
;  back -23, so this one code is the outlier that says "keep going".
;
;  68 is 2 + 2 + 64 and is what the twin sends
;  (RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:553), matching the CYW43439's firmware.
;
;  132 is 2 + 2 + 128 and is what brcmfmac's own header declares:
;  struct brcmf_wsec_pmk_le is { __le16 key_len; __le16 flags;
;  u8 key[BRCMF_WSEC_MAX_SAE_PASSWORD_LEN]; } at fwil_types.h:584-588,
;  and BRCMF_WSEC_MAX_SAE_PASSWORD_LEN is 128 at fwil_types.h:68.
;  brcmf_set_wsec sends sizeof(pmk) - the WHOLE struct, always, whatever
;  the key length (cfg80211.c:1710-1712).
;
;  THE KEY OFFSET AND THE 802.11i LENGTH GATE DO NOT CHANGE. The key
;  still starts at +4 and a passphrase is still 8..63 bytes; only the
;  amount of zero padding behind it differs. That is why the size is a
;  parameter of one procedure rather than two procedures.
;
;  WHICH ONE IS RIGHT HERE IS A BENCH QUESTION, so this file carries
;  both and Cyw43SetPmk takes the size. A library that picked one and
;  retried the other on failure would be making a decision it has no
;  evidence for, inside a call whose failure is the evidence.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_68  = 68    ; 2 + 2 + 64.  cyw43.pico:553, CYW43439
#CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_132 = 132   ; 2 + 2 + 128. fwil_types.h:584-588
#CYW43_PMK_STRUCT   = 132   ; the default: brcmfmac's, because
                            ; brcmfmac is the driver that works with
                            ; THIS firmware image
#CYW43_PMK_KEYOFF   = 4
#CYW43_PMK_KEYMAX   = 128   ; the LARGER of the two key fields, so the
                            ; wipe below always covers whichever was
                            ; sent. Wiping 64 after sending 132 would
                            ; leave half a passphrase in DRAM
#CYW43_PMK_PASSPHRASE = 1   ; flags: these bytes are ASCII and the
                            ; FIRMWARE runs PBKDF2 over them. 0 would
                            ; mean a 32-byte binary PMK is already here,
                            ; which neither reference driver ever sends
                            ; and this file therefore does not implement
#CYW43_SSID_MIN     = 1
#CYW43_SSID_MAX     = 32
#CYW43_SSID_STRUCT  = 36    ; u32 LE len + 32 raw bytes. The LENGTH is
                            ; the real one, not 32

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The join's events, and the two enumerations that share the number 6.
;
;  READ THE PSK_SUP WARNING BEFORE TOUCHING cyw43_JoinClassify. Event 46
;  status 6 is WLC_SUP_KEYED, a SUPPLICANT state; event 3 status 6 is
;  WLC_E_STATUS_UNSOLICITED, an escan-space code. They are different
;  vocabularies that collide on one number, and decoding 46 against the
;  escan table is a silent wrong answer aimed at the whole milestone.
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:5199-5215 is where that argument was settled.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_EV_SET_SSID     = 0     ; w:26. Association accepted, or not
#CYW43_EV_JOIN         = 1     ; w:27. DIAGNOSTIC ONLY - see below
#CYW43_EV_ASSOC        = 7     ; w:33. DIAGNOSTIC ONLY - see below
#CYW43_EV_AUTH         = 3     ; w:29
#CYW43_EV_DEAUTH       = 5     ; w:31
#CYW43_EV_DEAUTH_IND   = 6     ; w:32
#CYW43_EV_DISASSOC     = 11    ; w:37
#CYW43_EV_DISASSOC_IND = 12    ; w:38
#CYW43_EV_LINK         = 16    ; w:42. FLAGS BIT 0 is up/down
; ROAM (19) - THE FIRMWARE MOVED THE ASSOCIATION BY ITSELF, and on this
; part that is a link about to stop decrypting. The supplicant runs in
; the HOST here (this blob has no in-dongle supplicant), so the pairwise
; key was derived against the BSSID the host handshook with; a firmware
; roam changes the BSSID and does not, and cannot, rekey. The reference
; driver's brcmf_bss_roaming_done does no keying either - it reports the
; new BSSID upward and expects the host's supplicant to run a fresh
; four-way. The bring-up asks the firmware not to roam at all, so this
; event arriving at all is a fact worth having rather than a routine one.
#CYW43_EV_ROAM         = 19    ; w:45
#CYW43_EV_PRUNE        = 23    ; w:49
#CYW43_EV_PSK_SUP      = 46    ; w:72. THE SUPPLICANT'S OWN STATE
#CYW43_EV_ICV_ERROR    = 49    ; w:75

; The supplicant states, pico:590-601. The ENTIRE status field of event
; 46 lives in this space and in no other.
#CYW43_SUP_AUTHENTICATED    = 4
#CYW43_SUP_KEYED            = 6     ; THE WPA2 SUCCESS SIGNAL
#CYW43_SUP_LAST_BASIC_STATE = 8
#CYW43_SUP_KEYXCHANGE_WAIT_G1 = 10

; The three reason codes that name a cause rather than describing one.
; pico:612-614.
#CYW43_RSN_SUP_WPA_PSK_TMO    = 15  ; our supplicant gave up waiting
#CYW43_RSN_DEAUTH_WRONGKEY    = 2   ; the AP threw us off instead
#CYW43_RSN_PRUNE_RSN_MISMATCH = 8   ; our wsec/wpa_auth vs its RSN IE

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Layer 4 - the event packet, and IT IS A BIG-ENDIAN ISLAND.
;
;  READ THIS BEFORE TOUCHING THE EVENT PATH. The SDPCM header, the BDC
;  header, the BCDC header, the escan header and the entire bss_info are
;  LITTLE-endian. The ten-byte BCMETH header and the 48-byte event
;  message between them are NETWORK order, every multi-byte field.
;
;  The island's coastline is exact. From P, the start of the Ethernet
;  frame carried on the event channel - struct brcmf_event, w:240-244:
;
;      P+0    struct ethhdr        14 bytes   dst 6, src 6, ethertype 2
;      P+14   struct brcm_ethhdr   10 bytes   BIG-ENDIAN, w:225-231
;      P+24   brcmf_event_msg_be   48 bytes   BIG-ENDIAN, w:206-219
;      P+72   the payload begins, little-endian again
;
;  brcmf_event_msg_be, w:206-219, all __be:
;      +0  version u16     +2  flags u16      +4  event_type u32
;      +8  status u32      +12 reason u32     +16 auth_type u32
;      +20 datalen u32     +24 addr[6]        +30 ifname[16]
;      +46 ifidx u8        +47 bsscfgidx u8
;  so from P: event_type is at P+28, status at P+32, datalen at P+44.
;
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico lines 2604-2624 draws the same coastline from
;  two other drivers and records the exact bug it exists to prevent:
;  read event_type at P+28 little-endian and 69 comes back as
;  $45000000, "which is not a number anyone recognises as wrong at a
;  glance". Separate named readers, not a remembered rule.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_EV_ETHHDR   = 14
#CYW43_EV_BCMETH   = 10
#CYW43_EV_MSGLEN   = 48
#CYW43_EV_PKTLEN   = 72            ; 14 + 10 + 48, w:240-244
#CYW43_EV_OFF_FLAGS  = 26          ; P + 14 + 10 + 2. A u16, NOT a u32
#CYW43_EV_OFF_TYPE   = 28          ; P + 14 + 10 + 4
#CYW43_EV_OFF_STATUS = 32
#CYW43_EV_OFF_REASON = 36
#CYW43_EV_OFF_DATALEN = 44

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE FLAGS FIELD IS TWO BYTES AND IT IS THE WHOLE OF THE LINK TEST.
;
;  Added 2026-08-27 with the join. Until then this file read three
;  fields out of the island and ignored the other two, which was correct
;  for a scan and is fatal for a join: G's entire "are we linked" test
;  is `flags & 1` on event 16, and a driver that never reads flags sees
;  0 & 1 = 0 on every LINK event and NEVER COMPLETES A JOIN. It does not
;  fail either - it waits, and then times out, which is the most
;  expensive shape of bug there is.
;
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:3007 calls this out as "M3 EDIT 1" and says the
;  same thing in the same words, because it was learned there first.
;
;  BE16 AND NOT BE32. brcmf_event_msg_be's flags is a u16 at offset +2
;  of the message (w:206-219), so at P+26. Reading it as a 32-bit
;  big-endian word would pick up the version field beside it and shift
;  the bit that matters sixteen places out of reach.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_EV_LINK_UP_FLAG = 1

; The one event this file acts on. w:89, BRCMF_ENUM_DEF(ESCAN_RESULT, 69)
#CYW43_E_ESCAN_RESULT = 69         ; w:89
; And the one it merely reports. w:26 is SET_SSID 0; scan completion
; arrives as an ESCAN_RESULT carrying a terminal status, not as a
; separate event, so there is no SCAN_COMPLETE case below.

; Event status codes, w:114-129. Only the four that end or continue a
; scan are named; the rest are reported as a raw number, because naming
; a code this file cannot produce would be a table nobody can test.
#CYW43_E_STATUS_SUCCESS     = 0    ; w:114
#CYW43_E_STATUS_FAIL        = 1    ; w:115
#CYW43_E_STATUS_NO_NETWORKS = 3    ; w:117
#CYW43_E_STATUS_ABORT       = 4    ; w:118
#CYW43_E_STATUS_PARTIAL     = 8    ; w:122

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Layer 5 - the escan request, and the escan result behind the event.
;
;  struct brcmf_escan_params_le, t:  version u32, action u16, sync_id
;  u16, then struct brcmf_scan_params_le. The offsets below are that
;  struct laid out with ORDINARY C ALIGNMENT, which is what the firmware
;  expects because the kernel struct is not packed:
;
;      +0   version      u32
;      +4   action       u16
;      +6   sync_id      u16
;      +8   ssid.len     u32        \ struct brcmf_ssid_le, t: u32 + 32
;      +12  ssid.ssid    [32]       /
;      +44  bssid        [6]
;      +50  bss_type     s8
;      +51  scan_type    u8
;      +52  nprobes      s32
;      +56  active_time  s32
;      +60  passive_time s32
;      +64  home_time    s32
;      +68  channel_num  u32
;      +72  channel_list u16 (the union's padding member)
;      = 74, rounded to 76 by the struct's 4-byte alignment
;
;  THE SIZE IS CROSS-CHECKED AGAINST A BENCH-PROVEN DRIVER:
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico line 4381 sends 82 bytes for this iovar, and 82
;  is exactly 6 for "escan\0" plus the 76 above. Two derivations, one
;  number.
;
;  ACTION 1 IS "START". t:73's BRCMF_ESCAN_REQ_VERSION is 1 and is the
;  version field; the action value is WL_SCAN_ACTION_START, which is not
;  in any header on this disk - it is taken from cyw43.pico line 4359,
;  where it is bench-proven, and it is NAMED here rather than left as a
;  bare 1 so that its weaker provenance is visible.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_ESCAN_VERSION     = 1       ; BRCMF_ESCAN_REQ_VERSION  t:73
#CYW43_ESCAN_ACTION_START = 1      ; cyw43.pico:4359, NOT in a header here
#CYW43_ESCAN_SYNC_ID     = 1       ; our choice; echoed back in results
#CYW43_ESCAN_PARAMS_LEN  = 76
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_VERSION = 0
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_ACTION  = 4
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_SYNCID  = 6
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_SSIDLEN = 8
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_BSSID   = 44
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_BSSTYPE = 50
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_SCANTYPE = 51
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_NPROBES = 52
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_CHANNUM = 68

; bss_type: DOT11_BSSTYPE_ANY. Not in a header on this disk either;
; cyw43.pico line 4366 sends 2 and it is bench-proven.
#CYW43_BSSTYPE_ANY = 2
; scan_type, t:61-63.
#CYW43_SCANTYPE_ACTIVE  = 0        ; t:62
#CYW43_SCANTYPE_PASSIVE = 1        ; t:63

; struct brcmf_escan_result_le, t: buflen u32, version u32, sync_id u16,
; bss_count u16, then the first bss_info. Twelve bytes.
#CYW43_ESCAN_HDRLEN     = 12
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_BUFLEN = 0
#CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_COUNT  = 10

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  struct brcmf_bss_info_le, and THE PADDING IS PART OF THE LAYOUT.
;
;  The kernel struct is NOT __packed, so the compiler inserts padding and
;  the wire format contains it. Laying it out by hand:
;
;      +0   version        u32
;      +4   length         u32
;      +8   BSSID          [6]
;      +14  beacon_period  u16
;      +16  capability     u16
;      +18  SSID_len       u8
;      +19  SSID           [32]      ends at +51
;      ---- 1 byte of padding, because rateset.count is a u32 ----
;      +52  rateset.count  u32
;      +56  rateset.rates  [16]      ends at +72
;      +72  chanspec       u16
;      +74  atim_window    u16
;      +76  dtim_period    u8
;      ---- 1 byte of padding, because RSSI is a u16 ----
;      +78  RSSI           s16
;      +80  phy_noise      s8
;      +81  n_cap          u8
;      ---- 2 bytes of padding, because nbss_cap is a u32 ----
;      +84  nbss_cap       u32
;      +88  ctl_ch         u8
;
;  THREE OF THOSE OFFSETS ARE CONFIRMED INDEPENDENTLY. RP2040/Lib/
;  cyw43.pico reads chanspec at +72 (line 2846), RSSI at +78 (line 2860)
;  and ctl_ch at +88 (line 2847) - bench-proven against real firmware on
;  a sibling part. Those are exactly the three the padding above
;  produces. A hand-computed layout that reproduces three measured
;  offsets, including the two that only exist because of padding, is as
;  close to proof as this gets without a bench of our own.
;
;  #CYW43_BSSINFO_MIN is how much must be present before any of it may
;  be read. 89 is ctl_ch's offset plus one - the last field this file
;  touches - not the struct's full size, because the fields beyond it
;  are never read and demanding them would reject short-but-usable
;  records.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_VERSION = 0
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_LENGTH  = 4
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_BSSID   = 8
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_CAP     = 16
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_SSIDLEN = 18
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_SSID    = 19
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_CHANSPEC = 72
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_RSSI    = 78
#CYW43_BSS_OFF_CTLCH   = 88
#CYW43_BSSINFO_MIN     = 89

; The channel out of a chanspec: brcmu_wifi.h u:42 defines
; WL_CHANSPEC_CHAN_MASK as $00FF and u:97 defines CHSPEC_CHANNEL(c) as
; ((u8)((c) & WL_CHANSPEC_CHAN_MASK)). The upper bits are band, sideband
; and bandwidth; nothing here decodes them, so the raw halfword is kept
; for printing rather than being interpreted.
#CYW43_CHANSPEC_CHAN_MASK = $00FF  ; u:42

; The privacy bit of the 802.11 capability field. Not in any header on
; this disk; cyw43.pico line 2801 uses $0010 and calls it
; DOT11_CAP_PRIVACY, which agrees with the 802.11 standard's Capability
; Information layout. Reported, never acted on.
#CYW43_DOT11_CAP_PRIVACY = $0010

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Buffers, bounds and budgets.
;
;  THE RX BUFFER IS THE ONE SIZE THAT MATTERS. A scan result is an event
;  packet (72) plus an escan header (12) plus a bss_info (89 read, ~126
;  present) plus every information element the beacon carried, and the
;  IEs are the unbounded part. 2048 is four F2 blocks and comfortably
;  larger than anything a beacon can produce; a frame claiming more than
;  this is REFUSED rather than truncated, because a truncated frame
;  parses into a plausible network with a wrong name.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TX BUFFER IS SIZED BY A FULL-MTU 802.3 FRAME, AND SINCE 2026-09-07
; THAT IS WHAT IT HOLDS.
;
; It was 256 until 2026-08-27, which was ample for every ioctl this file
; then sent - the escan request is the largest of those at 28 + 82 = 110.
; The CLM changed it: one clmload chunk is 28 bytes of frame header, 8
; of iovar name, 12 of brcmf_dload_data_le and #CYW43_CLM_CHUNK of blob,
; which for a 1024-byte chunk is 1076 after the align-to-8 padding. That
; is where 1152 came from - the twin's number, RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:413.
;
; 1152 WAS THEN THE THING THAT CAPPED EVERY TRANSMITTED FRAME AT ABOUT A
; KILOBYTE, and the data path's own header at SECTION 9 said so in as
; many words: "the transmit buffer has to hold [1514] plus both headers -
; which is what fixes #CYW43_TXBUF's 1152 as TOO SMALL for a full-size
; frame ... the day something wants one, #CYW43_TXBUF is the number to
; raise." Something wants one.
;
; THE ARITHMETIC, WHICH IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT:
;
;     #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN  12   the SDPCM header, c:1331
;   + #CYW43_BDC_HDRLEN     4   the BDC header, bcdc.c
;   + #CYW43_ETH_MAX     1514   802.3 without the FCS
;   =                    1530   what Cyw43Send builds
;     rounded up to a word     1532   the bus moves whole words
;
; 1536 is that, rounded to a multiple of 32 so the buffer starts and ends
; on a cache line the day the caches are on. It is still comfortably
; inside #CYW43_MAX_FRAME (2048), which is what cyw43_F2Xfer bounds every
; transfer by, and it still holds a CLM chunk (1076) with room for a
; chunk raised toward brcmfmac's MAX_CHUNK_LEN of 1400 (t:185).
;
; WHAT IT BUYS, MEASURED THROUGH THE SEAM RATHER THAN ASSUMED: LinkTxMax()
; asks Cyw43DataMax(), which is this number minus the sixteen bytes of
; headers. It was 1136, so TcpTxCap() answered 1136 - 14 - 20 - 20 = 1082
; and every TCP segment this board SENT carried 1082 octets - the number
; the bench read back as `send MSS 1082` on 2026-09-04. It is now 1520,
; which link.pi4 clamps to #LINK_ETH_MAX 1514, so TcpTxCap() answers 1460:
; a full Ethernet MTU. That is 35% more payload per frame, and per frame
; is what costs - one SDPCM header, one CMD53 command phase, one PIO drain
; and one 802.11 air access, whatever the frame carries.
;
; THE COST IS 384 MORE BYTES OF BSS AND NOTHING ELSE. Cyw43Ioctl zeroes
; only the `padded` bytes it is about to send, never the whole buffer, so
; a larger buffer does not make a small ioctl slower.
;
; THE TWIN IS DELIBERATELY NOT FOLLOWED HERE. RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:413 is
; still 1152 and this file no longer matches it, because the two parts do
; not have the same problem: the Pico moves this bus at 400 kbit/s over
; three wires and its own note calls the bus the bottleneck, while this
; part runs four bits at 25 MHz and is nowhere near it. Divergence with a
; reason written down beats sameness nobody can explain.
#CYW43_TXBUF     = 1536
#CYW43_RXBUF     = 2048
#CYW43_MAX_FRAME = 2048

; How many networks the scan table holds. A refusal at the limit, not a
; wrap: see Cyw43ScanPoll.
#CYW43_MAX_BSS = 32

; The interface index every ioctl here carries. Station interface 0.
#CYW43_IFACE_STA = 0

; Return codes from the transport, kept apart from #CYW43_ERR_* because
; a timeout on a poll is not a driver error - it is the normal answer
; when nothing has arrived yet.
#CYW43_IO_OK      = 0
#CYW43_IO_TIMEOUT = -1
#CYW43_IO_TOOBIG  = -2
#CYW43_IO_STATUS  = -3   ; well-framed reply, firmware said no
#CYW43_IO_BADFRAME = -4
; The upload completed, every chunk was accepted, and then the firmware
; said the blob it assembled is not usable. That is a DIFFERENT fault
; from a refused ioctl and it gets its own code: a refusal blames the
; transport, this blames the bytes. The raw word is in Cyw43ClmStatus().
#CYW43_IO_CLMBAD  = -5

; What Cyw43ScanPoll returns.
#CYW43_SCAN_RUNNING = 0
#CYW43_SCAN_RESULT  = 1
#CYW43_SCAN_DONE    = 2
#CYW43_SCAN_FAILED  = 3

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE JOIN'S THREE-BIT ACCUMULATOR, AND IT IS NOT A SEQUENCER.
;
;  G accumulates three independent bits and fires when all three are
;  set, ORDER-INDEPENDENT ON PURPOSE. The usual order - AUTH, SET_SSID,
;  LINK, PSK_SUP - IS UNVERIFIED on this part: neither reference driver
;  asserts any order, and the Pico's note (cyw43.pico:5127-5137) says
;  the instruction was to print every event as it arrives so the real
;  order becomes a bench fact rather than a state machine's assumption.
;  This file inherits that. Nothing in cyw43_JoinClassify cares what
;  came first.
;
;  THE BIT VALUES ARE G'S, INCLUDING THE GAP. $0200 / $0400 / $0800 with
;  the kind in the bottom nibble is not a layout anybody would choose
;  fresh, and it is copied exactly so that the success test can be G's
;  literal `state = $0E01` rather than a mask test that has to be
;  re-argued. Renumbering them would be tidier and would mean this file
;  and the two working drivers no longer describe the same thing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK = $000F
#CYW43_JS_ACTIVE    = $0001
#CYW43_JS_FAIL      = $0002
#CYW43_JS_NONET     = $0003
#CYW43_JS_BADAUTH   = $0004
#CYW43_JS_AUTH      = $0200
#CYW43_JS_LINK      = $0400
#CYW43_JS_KEYED     = $0800
#CYW43_JS_ALL       = $0E01     ; ACTIVE | AUTH | LINK | KEYED

; What Cyw43JoinPoll returns.
#CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING = 0
#CYW43_JOIN_JOINED  = 1
#CYW43_JOIN_FAILED  = 2

; Why it failed. ONE CODE PER DISTINCT CAUSE, because "join failed"
; sends somebody to retype a passphrase that was never wrong.
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_NONE      = 0
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_NONET     = 1   ; SET_SSID status 3 - the SSID was not
                                ; on the air on any channel
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_WRONGPASS = 2   ; DEAUTH_IND reason 2 - the AP said so
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_PRUNE     = 3   ; PRUNE reason 8 - RSN mismatch. DO NOT
                                ; retry unchanged; this file has exactly
                                ; one security configuration to offer
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_TIMEOUT   = 4   ; OUR guard fired, not the firmware's
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_BADAUTH   = 5   ; AUTH status not 0/6, or PSK_SUP other
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_ASSOC     = 6   ; SET_SSID status not 0 and not 3
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_LINKLOST  = 7   ; DEAUTH / DISASSOC / LINK-down

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  KEYTIMEOUT IS ITS OWN CLASS AND IT IS RETRYABLE. THIS IS NOT
;  "WRONG PASSPHRASE" AND CALLING IT ONE COSTS A BENCH SESSION.
;
;  PSK_SUP status 4 / 8 / 10 with reason 15 is the FIRMWARE'S SUPPLICANT
;  TIMING OUT, and on the Pico bench a CORRECT passphrase produced this
;  exact event on 21 of 22 FIRST attempts and then keyed in 13 ms on the
;  retry (cyw43.pico:5209-5215). The mechanism was root-caused there
;  too: a board that vanishes while associated leaves the AP believing
;  the station is still present, so on the next association the AP does
;  not drive the four-way handshake and our supplicant sits at
;  SUP_AUTHENTICATED until sup_wpa_tmo expires. A disassociation clears
;  the AP's stale state, which is why the retry is the CURE being
;  applied rather than a second roll of the dice.
;
;  A WRONG PASSPHRASE CANNOT KEY, EVER. So one sample of this event
;  carries NO information about the passphrase and only a ladder - which
;  is the caller's, not this file's - has enough evidence to say the
;  word "wrong".
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_JOINFAIL_KEYTIMEOUT = 8

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE GUARDS ARE OURS AND EVERY ONE IS UNVERIFIED ON THIS PART.
;  Neither reference driver has a join timeout at all - G spins on state
;  flags with no deadline and E awaits events for ever. These exist
;  because a bare-metal board with a 16-second deadman cannot wait for
;  ever, and the instrument prints elapsed ms per phase so they become
;  measured numbers instead of guesses.
;
;  THE HANDSHAKE GUARD IS 6000 AGAINST THE FIRMWARE'S OWN 5000 AND THAT
;  RELATION IS NOT NEGOTIABLE. A guard SHORTER than sup_wpa_tmo converts
;  every wrong passphrase into a mute timeout, because our deadline
;  fires before the firmware has had its chance to say PSK_SUP reason
;  15 - and then the one reason code that names the fault is lost. Same
;  class of mistake as ending a scan on a delay instead of on its
;  completion event.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_JOIN_ASSOC_MS     = 4000
#CYW43_JOIN_HANDSHAKE_MS = 6000
#CYW43_JOIN_TOTAL_MS     = 12000

#CYW43_PHASE_IDLE      = 0
#CYW43_PHASE_ASSOC     = 1
#CYW43_PHASE_HANDSHAKE = 2
#CYW43_PHASE_HELD      = 3

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT KIND OF JOIN THIS IS, AND WHY THERE ARE THREE ANSWERS RATHER
;  THAN A FLAG.
;
;  MEASURED ON THIS BOARD, 2026-08-27, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS
;  ENUMERATION EXISTS AT ALL. The CYW43455's firmware answers -23
;  BCME_UNSUPPORTED to `bsscfg:sup_wpa`, to `bsscfg:sup_wpa2_eapver` and
;  to `bsscfg:sup_wpa_tmo`, and -2 BCME_BADARG to WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK.
;  There is NO SUPPLICANT IN THIS FIRMWARE. The Pico W's CYW43439 has
;  one and this part does not, which is exactly why the twin's code
;  could not simply be copied.
;
;  So a WPA2 join here gets as far as 802.11 AUTHENTICATION AND
;  ASSOCIATION - which really do complete, and really are what the word
;  "associate" means in 802.11 - and then stops, because the RSN
;  four-way handshake that follows has nobody to run it. The AP waits,
;  gets no EAPOL, and eventually deauthenticates.
;
;  A BOOLEAN "secured" FLAG COULD NOT EXPRESS THAT AND WOULD LIE.
;  Presetting the KEYED bit is the right mechanical behaviour for both
;  the OPEN case and this one - no PSK_SUP will ever arrive either way,
;  and waiting for one would time out every single time. But the two
;  mean utterly different things: on an open network KEYED means "no key
;  was needed", and here it means "a key WAS needed and nothing
;  negotiated one". A single flag would let an instrument print JOINED
;  for an unkeyed link on a WPA2 network with nothing in the log to say
;  so. Three values, and Cyw43JoinExpectation() is readable, so a caller
;  cannot report the outcome without being able to report which
;  question it answered.
;
;    SECURED  the four-way handshake is expected. KEYED must be EARNED,
;             and JOINED means associated AND keyed.
;    OPEN     no security at all. KEYED is preset and means "not
;             needed". JOINED means associated, which is all there is.
;    NOKEY    WPA2 configured, NO SUPPLICANT ANYWHERE. KEYED is preset
;             and means NOTHING WAS NEGOTIATED. JOINED means
;             802.11-ASSOCIATED ONLY - no pairwise key exists, no data
;             frame will pass, and the AP is entitled to throw us off at
;             any moment. It is a measurement, not a working link.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_JOINEXP_OPEN    = 0
#CYW43_JOINEXP_SECURED = 1
#CYW43_JOINEXP_NOKEY   = 2

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE EVENT RING. Sixteen entries, four fields each.
;
;  WHY A RING AT ALL, WHEN THE SCAN NEEDED NONE. A scan asks one
;  question and reads one kind of answer; a join has an ioctl reply and
;  a stream of unsolicited events in flight AT THE SAME TIME, and the
;  events that decide the join routinely land inside WLC_SET_SSID's own
;  reply window. Without a ring those events are decoded, overwritten by
;  the next frame, and lost - and a join that never sees its LINK event
;  waits out its guard and reports a timeout for a link that came up
;  perfectly. The Pico driver learned this as "M3 EDIT 3"
;  (cyw43.pico:3012-3016).
;
;  SIXTEEN. A join delivers four to six events; sixteen is four times
;  the observed traffic and costs 512 bytes. A ring that overflows
;  counts the loss in cyw43_evrDropped rather than overwriting silently,
;  because an overflow means the caller has stopped draining and that is
;  a different bug from a quiet radio.
;
;  ESCAN RESULTS DO NOT GO IN IT. They are bulky, they are parsed inline
;  into the BSS table by cyw43_ParseBss, and a scan result in a join
;  ring would be a row nobody reads. Same exclusion the Pico makes and
;  for the same reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_EVRING = 16

; The iovar names, and their lengths WITHOUT the terminating NUL.
; Kept as constants beside the DataSection they describe, because a loop
; bound and a Data.b row that disagree produce a name the firmware does
; not recognise and no message anywhere.
#CYW43_EVMSGS_NAMELEN  = 17        ; "bsscfg:event_msgs"
#CYW43_ESCAN_NAMELEN   = 5         ; "escan"
#CYW43_CLMLOAD_NAMELEN = 7         ; "clmload"
#CYW43_CLMSTAT_NAMELEN = 14        ; "clmload_status"
#CYW43_CLMVER_NAMELEN  = 6         ; "clmver"
#CYW43_COUNTRY_NAMELEN = 7         ; "country"
#CYW43_SCANVER_NAMELEN = 8         ; "scan_ver"
#CYW43_SUPWPA_NAMELEN  = 7         ; "sup_wpa"
#CYW43_AMPDU_NAMELEN   = 14        ; "ampdu_ba_wsize"
#CYW43_BSSSUPWPA_NAMELEN = 14      ; "bsscfg:sup_wpa"
#CYW43_EAPVER_NAMELEN  = 22        ; "bsscfg:sup_wpa2_eapver"
#CYW43_SUPTMO_NAMELEN  = 18        ; "bsscfg:sup_wpa_tmo"
#CYW43_MFP_NAMELEN     = 3         ; "mfp"
#CYW43_BSSAUTH_NAMELEN = 11        ; "bsscfg:auth"
#CYW43_BSSWSEC_NAMELEN = 11        ; "bsscfg:wsec"
#CYW43_BSSMFP_NAMELEN  = 10        ; "bsscfg:mfp"
#CYW43_BSSWPAAUTH_NAMELEN = 15     ; "bsscfg:wpa_auth"

; The iovar staging buffer. The largest payload built in it is the escan
; request - 6 for the name and its NUL, plus 76 of parameters = 82 - and
; the event mask is 18 + 4 + 19 = 41. The two text GETs ask for 128 back
; and that is what fixes the size. 128 leaves room without pretending to
; a generality this file does not have.
;
; THE CLM CHUNK IS NOT BUILT HERE. It is a thousand bytes and it has its
; own buffer, cyw43_clm - see THE CLM below.
;
; RAISED 128 -> 192 ON 2026-08-27, when the join went in. The PMK struct
; brcmfmac declares is 132 bytes (fwil_types.h:584-588 over a 128-byte
; key field), which does not fit 128 - and the failure would not have
; been a compile error or an overflow, it would have been Cyw43SetPmk
; quietly refusing with #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG on a board where everything
; else worked.
;
; 192 IS THE TWIN'S NUMBER (RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:490), taken rather
; than invented so the two files agree, and it leaves 60 bytes of head
; room above the largest payload either of them builds. The two 128-byte
; text GETs still fix the floor.
#CYW43_ARGBUF = 192

; ======================================================================
;  THE CLM DOWNLOADER'S WIRE FORMAT.
;
;  Every one of these is from Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_fwil_types.h,
;  which is the vendor's own header and not a transcription of somebody
;  else's driver:
;
;    :185   MAX_CHUNK_LEN      1400
;    :187   DLOAD_HANDLER_VER  1
;    :189   DLOAD_FLAG_VER_SHIFT 12
;    :191   DL_BEGIN           0x0002
;    :192   DL_END             0x0004
;    :194   DL_TYPE_CLM        2
;    :1106  struct brcmf_dload_data_le { le16 flag; le16 dload_type;
;                                        le32 len; le32 crc; u8 data[]; }
;
;  THE VERSION GOES IN THE TOP NIBBLE OF `flag`, which is why the
;  constant here is $1000 and not 1: brcmf_c_download does
;      flag |= (DLOAD_HANDLER_VER << DLOAD_FLAG_VER_SHIFT)
;  and a driver that wrote a bare 1 there would be setting DL_BEGIN's
;  neighbour bit and declaring downloader version 0.
;
;  THE CRC FIELD IS SENT AS ZERO, and that is the vendor's own choice,
;  not a shortcut taken here: brcmf_c_download writes
;  `dload_buf->crc = cpu_to_le32(0)` unconditionally. The blob's
;  integrity is the caller's problem, and on this board it is answered
;  by the CRC32 the payload takes over DRAM before handing the bytes in.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_DLOAD_VER   = $1000         ; DLOAD_HANDLER_VER << 12   t:187,189
#CYW43_DL_BEGIN    = $0002         ; t:191
#CYW43_DL_END      = $0004         ; t:192
#CYW43_DL_TYPE_CLM = 2             ; t:194
#CYW43_CLM_HDR     = 12            ; sizeof(brcmf_dload_data_le)  t:1106

; HOW BIG A CHUNK, AND WHY IT IS NOT 1400.
;
; brcmfmac's ceiling is MAX_CHUNK_LEN = 1400 and its comment says the
; firmware has a "small maximum size input payload restriction for
; ioctls" - so 1400 is a LIMIT, not a required size, and any smaller
; chunking is equally legal. 1024 is what pico-sdk sends and what
; RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:3596 has been sending to this same firmware
; family for months, so it is the one of the two numbers that has been
; on a bench. It also keeps the whole frame inside #CYW43_MAX_FRAME with
; room to spare, and it makes this board's 2676-byte blob three chunks -
; which EXERCISES the first/last flag logic that a single-chunk blob
; would leave untested. That last point is the argument: the twin sends
; its 984 bytes in one chunk with BEGIN and END both set, so its proof
; does not cover the middle chunk at all, and this file's does.
#CYW43_CLM_CHUNK = 1024

; The CLM staging buffer: the iovar name and its NUL (8), the download
; header (12), one chunk, and up to seven bytes of align-to-8 padding.
; 1056 rounds that up to a multiple of 32.
#CYW43_CLMBUF = 1056

; ======================================================================
;  CHIPCLKCSR bits. Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.c
;  lines 176-193.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_CLK_FORCE_ALP     = $01   ; SBSDIO_FORCE_ALP           c:177
#CYW43_CLK_FORCE_HT      = $02   ; SBSDIO_FORCE_HT            c:179
#CYW43_CLK_FORCE_ILP     = $04   ; SBSDIO_FORCE_ILP           c:181
#CYW43_CLK_ALP_AVAIL_REQ = $08   ; SBSDIO_ALP_AVAIL_REQ       c:183
#CYW43_CLK_HT_AVAIL_REQ  = $10   ; SBSDIO_HT_AVAIL_REQ        c:185
#CYW43_CLK_HW_REQ_OFF    = $20   ; SBSDIO_FORCE_HW_CLKREQ_OFF c:187
#CYW43_CLK_ALP_AVAIL     = $40   ; SBSDIO_ALP_AVAIL           c:189
#CYW43_CLK_HT_AVAIL      = $80   ; SBSDIO_HT_AVAIL            c:191
#CYW43_CLK_AVBITS        = $C0   ; SBSDIO_AVBITS = HT|ALP     c:193

; BRCMF_INIT_CLKCTL1, sdio.c lines 315-316.
#CYW43_INIT_CLKCTL1 = $28        ; HW_REQ_OFF | ALP_AVAIL_REQ

; ======================================================================
;  The backplane.
; ======================================================================
; SI_ENUM_BASE_DEFAULT, v6.12_brcmu_soc.h line 9. brcmf_chip_enum_base()
; (chip.c lines 963-966) returns it unconditionally for every device id,
; so there is no per-part variation to carry.
#CYW43_ENUM_BASE = $18000000

; ChipCommon register offsets, v6.12_brcmu_chipcommon.h.
#CYW43_CC_CHIPID  = $00   ; struct chipcregs.chipid       cc:14 "0x0"
#CYW43_CC_CAPS    = $04   ; capabilities
#CYW43_CC_EROMPTR = $FC   ; struct chipcregs.eromptr      cc:107 "0xfc"

; chipid field masks, v6.12_brcmu_chipcommon.h lines 222-230.
#CYW43_CID_ID_MASK   = $0000FFFF  ; cc:222
#CYW43_CID_REV_MASK  = $000F0000  ; cc:223
#CYW43_CID_REV_SHIFT = 16         ; cc:224
#CYW43_CID_PKG_MASK  = $00F00000  ; cc:225
#CYW43_CID_PKG_SHIFT = 20         ; cc:226
#CYW43_CID_CC_MASK   = $0F000000  ; cc:227  core count
#CYW43_CID_CC_SHIFT  = 24         ; cc:228
#CYW43_CID_TYPE_MASK = $F0000000  ; cc:229
#CYW43_CID_TYPE_SHIFT = 28        ; cc:230

; Backplane type, chip.c lines 21-22.
#CYW43_SOCI_SB = 0
#CYW43_SOCI_AI = 1

; The part this board carries. brcm_hw_ids.h line 34.
#CYW43_CHIP_4345 = $4345
; The revision mask that selects the "43455" firmware, sdio.c line 652.
; Used ONLY to answer Cyw43IsCyw43455() - see THE PART.
#CYW43_REVMASK_43455 = $FFFFFDC0

; ======================================================================
;  DMP / EROM descriptors. Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_chip.c
;  lines 25-71. Line numbers given as "k:".
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_DMP_TYPE_MSK      = $0000000F  ; k:25
#CYW43_DMP_EMPTY         = $00000000  ; k:26
#CYW43_DMP_VALID         = $00000001  ; k:27
#CYW43_DMP_COMPONENT     = $00000001  ; k:28
#CYW43_DMP_MASTER_PORT   = $00000003  ; k:29
#CYW43_DMP_ADDRESS       = $00000005  ; k:30
#CYW43_DMP_ADDRSIZE_GT32 = $00000008  ; k:31
#CYW43_DMP_EOT           = $0000000F  ; k:32

#CYW43_DMP_COMP_PARTNUM    = $000FFF00 ; k:36
#CYW43_DMP_COMP_PARTNUM_S  = 8         ; k:37
#CYW43_DMP_COMP_REVISION   = $FF000000 ; k:40
#CYW43_DMP_COMP_REVISION_S = 24        ; k:41
#CYW43_DMP_COMP_NUM_SWRAP  = $00F80000 ; k:42
#CYW43_DMP_COMP_NUM_SWRAP_S = 19       ; k:43
#CYW43_DMP_COMP_NUM_MWRAP  = $0007C000 ; k:44
#CYW43_DMP_COMP_NUM_MWRAP_S = 14       ; k:45

#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_ADDR_BASE = $FFFFF000 ; k:56
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE      = $000000C0 ; k:60
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE_S    = 6         ; k:61
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE_SLAVE = 0        ; k:62
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE_SWRAP = 2        ; k:64
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE_MWRAP = 3        ; k:65
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_TYPE = $00000030 ; k:66
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_TYPE_S = 4       ; k:67
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_4K   = 0         ; k:68
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_8K   = 1         ; k:69
#CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_DESC = 3         ; k:71

; How many cores this file will record. A 4345 has fewer than a dozen;
; sixteen leaves room and the walk REFUSES rather than overflowing.
#CYW43_MAX_CORES = 16

; The EROM walk's own step budget. The table is short - a few hundred
; words at most - and a corrupt read can otherwise walk for ever. This
; is a CHOICE, not a citation, and it is generous by two orders of
; magnitude on purpose.
#CYW43_EROM_MAX_STEPS = 4096

; ======================================================================
;  Timing.
; ======================================================================
; PMU_MAX_TRANSITION_DLY. TWO definitions exist and sdio.c's wins for
; SDIO parts - see THE CLOCK. sdio.c lines 311-312 #undef the
; chipcommon.h value of 15000 and define 1000000 microseconds.
#CYW43_ALP_WAIT_US = 1000000
; The settling delay after switching to FORCE_ALP. sdio.c line 3881,
; "udelay(65)". Not rounded.
#CYW43_ALP_SETTLE_US = 65
; How long to allow an SDIO function to report ready. sdio.pi4 has its
; own bound; this is only for the delay loop below.
#CYW43_HZ_MAX = 1000000000

; ======================================================================
;  Error codes.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_ERR_NONE        = 0
#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO       = 1    ; Cyw43SetIo was never called
#CYW43_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ   = 2
#CYW43_ERR_F1_ENABLE   = 3
#CYW43_ERR_F2_ENABLE   = 4
#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE     = 5
#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE   = 6
#CYW43_ERR_CLK_ECHO    = 7    ; the read-back did not match - bus dead
#CYW43_ERR_ALP_TMO     = 8
#CYW43_ERR_WINDOW      = 9
#CYW43_ERR_READ32      = 10
#CYW43_ERR_WRITE32     = 11
#CYW43_ERR_CHIPID      = 12   ; all-zero or all-ones
#CYW43_ERR_SOCI        = 13   ; not an AXI backplane
#CYW43_ERR_EROM        = 14
#CYW43_ERR_TOO_MANY    = 15
#CYW43_ERR_NOT_ATTACHED = 16
#CYW43_ERR_NO_FIRMWARE = 17   ; THE WALL
#CYW43_ERR_ALIGN       = 18
; Added 2026-08-26, when the wall came down. Numbered after 18 and
; nothing above was renumbered: codes 1 to 18 appear in bench logs
; already filed and in tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py.
#CYW43_ERR_NO_BLOCK_IO = 19   ; block mode asked for, seam never set
#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE     = 20   ; a core this file needs is not on the chip
#CYW43_ERR_NO_WRAPPER  = 21   ; the core exists but has no AXI wrapper
#CYW43_ERR_RAMINFO     = 22   ; the CR4 bank walk gave an impossible size
#CYW43_ERR_CORE_RESET  = 23   ; a core would not come out of reset
#CYW43_ERR_XFER        = 24   ; a windowed bulk transfer failed
#CYW43_ERR_VERIFY      = 25   ; the read-back did not match what was written
#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN     = 26   ; a length or buffer size the contract refuses
#CYW43_ERR_NVRAM       = 27   ; the NVRAM text produced nothing usable
#CYW43_ERR_HT_TMO      = 28   ; the chip never reported HT available
#CYW43_ERR_F2_TMO      = 29   ; F2 never reported ready - firmware did not run
#CYW43_ERR_NOT_LOADED  = 30   ; Cyw43Start before an image was uploaded

; ======================================================================
;  State.
; ======================================================================
; The four SDIO primitives. DECLARED WITH '*' ON PURPOSE - see THE SEAM.
Global *cyw43_rb        ; Rb(fn, addr) -> 0..255 or negative
Global *cyw43_wb        ; Wb(fn, addr, val) -> 1/0
Global *cyw43_rd        ; Rd(fn, addr, *buf, n, incr) -> 1/0
Global *cyw43_wr        ; Wr(fn, addr, *buf, n, incr) -> 1/0

; The OPTIONAL block-mode pair, added 2026-08-26. Separate from the four
; above and separately installed, because they are separately optional:
; everything this file did before today works without them, and a build
; that never uploads firmware never needs them. See Cyw43SetBlockIo.
Global *cyw43_rdb       ; Rdb(fn, addr, *buf, blkSize, blocks, incr) -> 1/0
Global *cyw43_wrb       ; Wrb(fn, addr, *buf, blkSize, blocks, incr) -> 1/0

; Whether the bulk paths are ALLOWED to use block mode. Both a
; capability and a switch: it goes to 0 if the seam was never installed,
; and a caller may clear it deliberately to force the byte-mode path -
; which is how the two are compared against each other on the bench.
Global cyw43_useBlocks.i = 0
; The block size this file told function 1 to use. Read back from the
; card rather than assumed; see Cyw43Attach.
Global cyw43_f1Blk.i = 0

; The longest BYTE-MODE transfer function 1 will accept. This is NOT
; 512, and assuming it was cost a bench run on 2026-08-26 - see THE
; BYTE-MODE CEILING above cyw43_XferOne. Zero until Cyw43Attach has read
; the block size back out of the card.
Global cyw43_byteMax.i = 0

; Whether the CARD says it can do multi-block CMD53 at all: CCCR CAPS
; bit SMB. Block mode is refused without it rather than attempted and
; blamed on something else.
Global cyw43_multiBlock.i = 0

; FUNCTION 2'S block size and byte-mode ceiling, and they are SEPARATE
; from function 1's on purpose. F1 is 64 and F2 is 512. The byte-mode
; ceiling is a property of the FUNCTION - that is the whole lesson of
; 2026-08-26 - and using one function's number for the other is the
; same bug pointing the other way. It would cap every WLAN frame at 64
; bytes, which is not wrong, merely eight times slower, and would
; therefore never be noticed.
;
; Declared HERE, beside F1's, rather than down in the transport section
; that uses them: Cyw43Attach reads them back and Cyw43Attach comes
; first in this file.
Global cyw43_f2Blk.i = 0
Global cyw43_f2ByteMax.i = 0

Global cyw43_err.i      = #CYW43_ERR_NONE
Global cyw43_attached.i = 0

Global cyw43_hz.i = 0

Global cyw43_window.i    = 0   ; the backplane window currently programmed
Global cyw43_windowOk.i  = 0   ; 0 = the cache is not to be trusted

Global cyw43_chipid.i  = 0     ; the whole 32-bit register, as read
Global cyw43_chip.i    = 0     ; CID_ID
Global cyw43_chiprev.i = 0
Global cyw43_chippkg.i = 0
Global cyw43_socitype.i = -1
Global cyw43_ccount.i  = 0     ; core count field of chipid - NOT the
                               ; number of cores the EROM walk found
Global cyw43_clkcsr.i  = 0     ; CHIPCLKCSR as last read

; The EROM pointer the walk started from, kept so a diagnostic can read
; a region of the table back through the bulk path and compare it with
; what the walk saw. It is the one large area of this chip that is both
; READ-ONLY and GUARANTEED NOT TO CHANGE between two reads, which makes
; it the only honest target for an A-versus-B comparison of two transfer
; modes. A ChipCommon register block would not do: several registers in
; it are live counters and a mismatch would prove nothing.
Global cyw43_eromptr.i = 0

; RAM, as the chip reports it. Zero until Cyw43ReadRamInfo has run.
Global cyw43_rambase.i = 0
Global cyw43_ramsize.i = 0

; The reset vector taken out of the first four bytes of the firmware
; image, and whether an image has actually been written. Cyw43Start
; refuses without both - see #CYW43_ERR_NOT_LOADED.
Global cyw43_rstvec.i  = 0
Global cyw43_fwLoaded.i = 0
Global cyw43_nvLoaded.i = 0
Global cyw43_nvBytes.i = 0     ; the COOKED length, token included

; The first mismatch a verify pass found, so a log line can name an
; address instead of a count. Only the first of a whole pass is kept:
; a per-chunk record would end up naming the first mismatch of the LAST
; bad chunk, which is a different and much less useful fact. The same
; reasoning, and the same bug, as RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico lines 1887-1890.
Global cyw43_badFound.i = 0
Global cyw43_badAddr.i  = 0
Global cyw43_badGot.i   = 0
Global cyw43_badWant.i  = 0

Global cyw43_ncores.i = 0
Global Dim cyw43_coreId.i[#CYW43_MAX_CORES]
Global Dim cyw43_coreRev.i[#CYW43_MAX_CORES]
Global Dim cyw43_coreBase.i[#CYW43_MAX_CORES]
Global Dim cyw43_coreWrap.i[#CYW43_MAX_CORES]

; A 4-byte staging buffer for the 32-bit backplane accesses. A Dim array
; lands 8-byte aligned on this backend, which is more than the 4 the
; CMD53 contract asks for - the same note fat.pi4 makes about its
; sector buffers.
Global Dim cyw43_word.l[1]


; ======================================================================
;  The clock, for bounded waits only. Two instructions each. Same form
;  as sdio.pi4 - and the same reason for not calling timer.pi4.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i cyw43_Ticks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cyw43_TickHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure cyw43_DelayUs(us.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define want.i
  If cyw43_hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  want = (cyw43_hz / 1000000) * us
  If want < 1
    want = 1
  EndIf
  t0 = cyw43_Ticks()
  While (cyw43_Ticks() - t0) < want
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cyw43_Fail(code.i)
  cyw43_err = code
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - Cyw43SetIo. Hand this file its four SDIO primitives.
;
;  ALL FOUR OR NOTHING. A partial set is refused rather than accepted
;  and discovered three procedures later, because "the chip ID reads as
;  zero" is a much worse symptom than "you did not wire the bus up".
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Cyw43SetIo(*rb, *wb, *rd, *wr)
  If *rb = 0 Or *wb = 0 Or *rd = 0 Or *wr = 0
    *cyw43_rb = 0
    *cyw43_wb = 0
    *cyw43_rd = 0
    *cyw43_wr = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
  EndIf
  *cyw43_rb = *rb
  *cyw43_wb = *wb
  *cyw43_rd = *rd
  *cyw43_wr = *wr
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43IoReady()
  If *cyw43_rb = 0 Or *cyw43_wb = 0 Or *cyw43_rd = 0 Or *cyw43_wr = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  Function-1 byte access, through the seam.
; ======================================================================

; Returns 0..255, or -1. See THE SEAM for why the call has no star.
Procedure.i Cyw43ReadByte(fn.i, addr.i)
  If Cyw43IoReady() = 0
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rb(fn, addr)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43WriteByte(fn.i, addr.i, val.i)
  If Cyw43IoReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_wb(fn, addr, val)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  cyw43_SetWindow - slide the backplane window to cover addr.
;
;  Three CMD52 writes of address bits 15:8, 23:16 and 31:24. The exact
;  loop brcmf_sdiod_set_backplane_window() runs, bcmsdh.c lines 223-242 -
;  see THE BACKPLANE WINDOW for the three ways this goes wrong.
;
;  THE CACHE IS SKIPPED WHEN cyw43_windowOk IS 0, which is the state
;  after a failure and after Cyw43InvalidateWindow(). A cached window
;  that is wrong reads the wrong part of the chip and looks fine doing
;  it, so the cache is cleared BEFORE the writes and only set after all
;  three have succeeded - never the other way round.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i cyw43_SetWindow(addr.i)
  Define bar0.i
  Define v.i
  Define i.i

  bar0 = addr & #CYW43_SBWINDOW_MASK
  If cyw43_windowOk <> 0 And bar0 = cyw43_window
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  cyw43_windowOk = 0
  v = (bar0 >> 8) & $FFFFFF
  For i = 0 To 2
    If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_SBADDRLOW + i, v & $FF) = 0
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_WINDOW)
    EndIf
    v = v >> 8
  Next

  cyw43_window = bar0
  cyw43_windowOk = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43InvalidateWindow() - say that the three window registers may
;  have been disturbed by something other than this file.
;
;  Cheap insurance and it exists because the alternative is a cache of
;  hardware state that can go stale without any symptom except wrong
;  answers. Call it after anything that resets the card.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Cyw43InvalidateWindow()
  cyw43_windowOk = 0
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  Cyw43Read32 / Cyw43Write32 - one 32-bit backplane register.
;
;  bcmsdh.c lines 243-262 and 264-282: set the window, mask the address
;  down to fifteen bits, OR IN THE 2/4-BYTE ACCESS FLAG, then a 32-bit
;  transfer on function 1.
;
;  THE FLAG IS NOT DECORATION - see point 3 of THE BACKPLANE WINDOW.
;
;  Read failure is reported through the return of Cyw43Read32Ok(), not
;  through the value, because every 32-bit pattern is a legitimate
;  register value and $FFFFFFFF in particular is what a dead bus
;  returns. brcmfmac has the same problem and solves it the same way -
;  chip.c line 974 names the constant READ_FAILED = 0xFFFFFFFF and
;  treats it as an error, which is a heuristic; this file returns the
;  status separately, which is not.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i cyw43_Read32Raw(addr.i, *okOut)
  Define off.i
  If Cyw43IoReady() = 0
    PokeL(*okOut, 0)
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If cyw43_SetWindow(addr) = 0
    PokeL(*okOut, 0)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  off = (addr & #CYW43_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK) | #CYW43_SB_ACCESS_2_4B
  ; incr = 1: four consecutive backplane bytes, not four reads of one.
  If cyw43_rd(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, off, @cyw43_word[0], 4, 1) <> 1
    PokeL(*okOut, 0)
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_READ32)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PokeL(*okOut, 1)
  ; PeekN, not the .l array element: .i is 64 bits here and a .l read
  ; sign-extends, so a register with bit 31 set would come back
  ; negative and every mask below it would be wrong.
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(@cyw43_word[0])
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43Read32(addr) - the plain form. Returns the value; a failure is
;  visible through Cyw43LastError() and returns 0.
;
;  USE cyw43_Read32Raw IF ZERO AND FAILURE MUST BE TOLD APART. Inside
;  this file the EROM walk does exactly that, because a descriptor of 0
;  is a legal EMPTY descriptor and a bus failure is not.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43Read32(addr.i)
  Define ok.l
  Define v.i
  v = cyw43_Read32Raw(addr, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43Write32(addr.i, value.i)
  Define off.i
  If Cyw43IoReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
  EndIf
  If cyw43_SetWindow(addr) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  off = (addr & #CYW43_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK) | #CYW43_SB_ACCESS_2_4B
  PokeL(@cyw43_word[0], value)
  If cyw43_wr(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, off, @cyw43_word[0], 4, 1) <> 1
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_WRITE32)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  Cyw43ClockPrepare - brcmf_sdio_buscoreprep(), sdio.c lines 3843-3886.
;
;  See THE CLOCK for the whole reasoning. In short: ask for ALP with the
;  hardware's own clock requests squelched, PROVE the write landed by
;  reading it back, wait for the chip to say ALP is available, force
;  ALP, settle, and drop the extra SDIO pull-ups.
;
;  THE READ-BACK IS THE BUS TEST. It is the first thing in this whole
;  stack that can distinguish "the card is there and talking" from
;  "something answered". Everything above it depends on it, so it gets
;  its own error code (#CYW43_ERR_CLK_ECHO) rather than being folded
;  into a general failure.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with cyw43_err set.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Cyw43ClockPrepare()
  Define clkset.i
  Define clkval.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i

  If Cyw43IoReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
  EndIf

  ; --- ask for ALP, hardware requests squelched -----------------------
  clkset = #CYW43_CLK_HW_REQ_OFF | #CYW43_CLK_ALP_AVAIL_REQ
  If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR, clkset) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE)
  EndIf

  ; --- prove it landed ------------------------------------------------
  clkval = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR)
  If clkval < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE)
  EndIf
  cyw43_clkcsr = clkval
  ; sdio.c line 3861: the two AVAILABLE status bits are the chip's, not
  ; ours, so they are masked out before the comparison. Everything else
  ; must read back exactly.
  If (clkval & (~#CYW43_CLK_AVBITS) & $FF) <> clkset
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_ECHO)
  EndIf

  ; --- wait for ALP -----------------------------------------------------
  ; SBSDIO_ALPAV(regval) is ((regval) & SBSDIO_AVBITS) - sdio.c line 194.
  ; Note it accepts EITHER available bit: HT implies ALP.
  If cyw43_hz <= 0
    cyw43_hz = cyw43_TickHz()
    If cyw43_hz <= 0 Or cyw43_hz > #CYW43_HZ_MAX
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  budget = (cyw43_hz / 1000000) * #CYW43_ALP_WAIT_US
  t0 = cyw43_Ticks()
  Repeat
    clkval = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR)
    If clkval < 0
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE)
    EndIf
    cyw43_clkcsr = clkval
    If (clkval & #CYW43_CLK_AVBITS) <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
    If (cyw43_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_ALP_TMO)
    EndIf
  ForEver

  ; --- force ALP and settle ------------------------------------------
  clkset = #CYW43_CLK_HW_REQ_OFF | #CYW43_CLK_FORCE_ALP
  If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR, clkset) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE)
  EndIf
  cyw43_DelayUs(#CYW43_ALP_SETTLE_US)

  ; --- and drop the extra pull-ups. sdio.c line 3884. A failure here is
  ;     ignored in the driver ("NULL" for the error pointer) and it is
  ;     ignored here for the same reason: it is a power optimisation on
  ;     a bus that is already working.
  Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_SDIOPULLUP, 0)

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  cyw43_ReadChipId - the chip ID, and what it decomposes into.
;
;  chip.c lines 982-991, brcmf_chip_recognition():
;      regdata = read32(CORE_CC_REG(enum_base, chipid));
;      ci->pub.chip    = regdata & CID_ID_MASK;
;      ci->pub.chiprev = (regdata & CID_REV_MASK) >> CID_REV_SHIFT;
;      socitype        = (regdata & CID_TYPE_MASK) >> CID_TYPE_SHIFT;
;
;  and chip.c line 983's comment records the assumption this rests on:
;  "Chipid is assume to be at offset 0 from SI_ENUM_BASE".
;
;  ALL-ZERO AND ALL-ONES ARE BOTH REFUSED. brcmfmac only checks for
;  $FFFFFFFF (chip.c line 974, "const u32 READ_FAILED = 0xFFFFFFFF"),
;  which catches a floating bus. Zero is the other half of the same
;  failure and it is the one that happens here: an unclocked backplane
;  reads as zeroes, not as ones, and a chip id of 0 would decode to a
;  perfectly well-formed SOCI_SB part with id 0 and no cores.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i cyw43_ReadChipId()
  Define ok.l
  Define v.i

  v = cyw43_Read32Raw(#CYW43_ENUM_BASE + #CYW43_CC_CHIPID, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If v = 0 Or v = $FFFFFFFF
    cyw43_chipid = v
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CHIPID)
  EndIf

  cyw43_chipid   = v
  cyw43_chip     = v & #CYW43_CID_ID_MASK
  cyw43_chiprev  = (v & #CYW43_CID_REV_MASK) >> #CYW43_CID_REV_SHIFT
  cyw43_chippkg  = (v & #CYW43_CID_PKG_MASK) >> #CYW43_CID_PKG_SHIFT
  cyw43_ccount   = (v & #CYW43_CID_CC_MASK) >> #CYW43_CID_CC_SHIFT
  cyw43_socitype = (v & #CYW43_CID_TYPE_MASK) >> #CYW43_CID_TYPE_SHIFT
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  The EROM walk. See THE CORE TABLE for what is and is not implemented.
;
;  The descriptor stream is 32-bit words read from the backplane
;  starting at the address in ChipCommon's eromptr. Each word's low four
;  bits are its type (DMP_DESC_TYPE_MSK, chip.c line 25), and
;  brcmf_chip_dmp_get_desc() (chip.c lines 812-829) folds the
;  "address, 64-bit" type back into plain "address" before returning it:
;
;      *type = (val & DMP_DESC_TYPE_MSK);
;      if ((*type & ~DMP_DESC_ADDRSIZE_GT32) == DMP_DESC_ADDRESS)
;          *type = DMP_DESC_ADDRESS;
;
;  which matters because $D is "address with a 64-bit extension word
;  following" and treating it as an unknown type desynchronises the
;  whole rest of the table.
;
;  EVERY READ IS CHECKED. A failed backplane read inside a walk is
;  indistinguishable from a descriptor of zero, and a zero descriptor is
;  legal (DMP_DESC_EMPTY). That is why cyw43_Read32Raw exists.
; ======================================================================

; Read one descriptor, advance the cursor by four, and hand back its
; folded type. Returns the raw word; ok is 0 on a bus failure.
Procedure.i cyw43_Erom(*cursor, *typeOut, *okOut)
  Define a.i
  Define v.i
  Define ok.l
  Define t.i

  a = PeekI(*cursor)
  v = cyw43_Read32Raw(a, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    PokeL(*okOut, 0)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  PokeI(*cursor, a + 4)
  PokeL(*okOut, 1)

  t = v & #CYW43_DMP_TYPE_MSK
  If (t & (~#CYW43_DMP_ADDRSIZE_GT32)) = #CYW43_DMP_ADDRESS
    t = #CYW43_DMP_ADDRESS
  EndIf
  PokeI(*typeOut, t)
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_EromRegAddr - the address descriptors that follow a component.
;
;  A REDUCTION OF brcmf_chip_dmp_get_regaddr(), chip.c lines 832-899, and
;  the differences are listed rather than hidden:
;
;    * that function loops "while (*regbase == 0 || *wrapbase == 0)",
;      i.e. it keeps reading until it has BOTH. This one takes the first
;      slave it sees as the base and the first wrapper it sees as the
;      wrapper, and stops at the next component descriptor whether or
;      not it found either. Nothing here resets a core, so a missing
;      wrapper is information, not a failure.
;
;    * that function distinguishes a master-port wrapper from a slave
;      wrapper by whether the FIRST descriptor after the component was
;      DMP_DESC_MASTER_PORT (lines 843-851). This one records whichever
;      wrapper type turns up, in a single wrap field, and does not care
;      which flavour it was - again, because it never uses it.
;
;    * SIZE DESCRIPTORS ARE SKIPPED THE SAME WAY, and that part is NOT
;      reduced, because getting it wrong desynchronises the stream: a
;      slave whose size type is DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_DESC ($3) is followed by
;      an extra size word, and that word may itself have the 64-bit flag
;      and be followed by another (chip.c lines 878-885). Likewise an
;      address descriptor with DMP_DESC_ADDRSIZE_GT32 is followed by its
;      own upper-32-bits word (lines 875-876).
;
;    * ONLY 4K AND 8K REGIONS COUNT as a core's register space
;      (chip.c lines 886-889). A 16K region is something else and is
;      skipped, not used.
;
;  Writes the base and wrapper through the two out-pointers, leaves the
;  cursor on the next component descriptor, and returns 1, or 0 on a
;  bus failure.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_EromRegAddr(*cursor, *baseOut, *wrapOut)
  Define v.i
  Define t.i
  Define ok.l
  Define sztype.i
  Define stype.i
  Define szdesc.i
  Define guard.i
  Define regbase.i
  Define wrapbase.i

  regbase = 0
  wrapbase = 0
  guard = 0

  Repeat
    guard = guard + 1
    If guard > #CYW43_EROM_MAX_STEPS
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
    EndIf

    v = cyw43_Erom(*cursor, @t, @ok)
    If ok = 0
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
    EndIf

    If t = #CYW43_DMP_EOT
      ; Rewind so the caller's loop sees the end-of-table for itself.
      PokeI(*cursor, PeekI(*cursor) - 4)
      Break
    EndIf

    If t = #CYW43_DMP_COMPONENT
      ; The next core has started. Rewind and hand it back.
      PokeI(*cursor, PeekI(*cursor) - 4)
      Break
    EndIf

    If t <> #CYW43_DMP_ADDRESS
      ; A master-port descriptor, or anything else. Nothing to take
      ; from it here; keep walking.
      Continue
    EndIf

    ; A 64-bit address descriptor carries its top half in the NEXT word.
    ; Consume it, or the stream is off by one for ever after.
    If (v & #CYW43_DMP_ADDRSIZE_GT32) <> 0
      cyw43_Erom(*cursor, @t, @ok)
      If ok = 0
        ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
      EndIf
    EndIf

    sztype = (v & #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_TYPE) >> #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_TYPE_S

    If sztype = #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_DESC
      szdesc = cyw43_Erom(*cursor, @t, @ok)
      If ok = 0
        ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
      EndIf
      If (szdesc & #CYW43_DMP_ADDRSIZE_GT32) <> 0
        cyw43_Erom(*cursor, @t, @ok)
        If ok = 0
          ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf

    If sztype <> #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_4K And sztype <> #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_SIZE_8K
      Continue
    EndIf

    stype = (v & #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE) >> #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE_S
    If regbase = 0 And stype = #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE_SLAVE
      regbase = v & #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_ADDR_BASE
    EndIf
    If wrapbase = 0
      If stype = #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE_SWRAP Or stype = #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_TYPE_MWRAP
        wrapbase = v & #CYW43_DMP_SLAVE_ADDR_BASE
      EndIf
    EndIf
  ForEver

  PokeI(*baseOut, regbase)
  PokeI(*wrapOut, wrapbase)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43EnumerateCores - the walk itself.
;
;  brcmf_chip_dmp_erom_scan(), chip.c lines 904-960. Two component
;  descriptors per core: the first carries the part number, the second
;  the revision and the wrapper counts.
;
;  THE "NEEDS PORTS" FILTER IS KEPT (chip.c lines 936-941): a component
;  with no master and no slave wrappers is not a core with registers,
;  UNLESS it is the PMU or the GCI, which are exceptions that source
;  spells out. Dropping the filter would fill the table with entries
;  that have no address.
;
;  Returns the number of cores found, or 0 with cyw43_err set. Note that
;  0 IS ALSO A FAILURE VALUE and that is deliberate: a chip that
;  enumerates no cores at all is broken, so there is no useful
;  distinction to preserve.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43EnumerateCores()
  Define cursor.i
  Define v.i
  Define t.i
  Define ok.l
  Define id.i
  Define rev.i
  Define nmw.i
  Define nsw.i
  Define base.i
  Define wrap.i
  Define steps.i

  cyw43_ncores = 0

  If cyw43_socitype <> #CYW43_SOCI_AI
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_SOCI)
  EndIf

  cursor = cyw43_Read32Raw(#CYW43_ENUM_BASE + #CYW43_CC_EROMPTR, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
  EndIf
  ; An EROM pointer of zero or all-ones is not a pointer. brcmfmac does
  ; not check this, because by the time it gets here the bus has been
  ; proven several times over; here it is the cheapest possible guard
  ; against walking a table that does not exist.
  If cursor = 0 Or cursor = $FFFFFFFF
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
  EndIf
  cyw43_eromptr = cursor

  steps = 0
  Repeat
    steps = steps + 1
    If steps > #CYW43_EROM_MAX_STEPS
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
    EndIf

    v = cyw43_Erom(@cursor, @t, @ok)
    If ok = 0
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
    EndIf

    If t = #CYW43_DMP_EOT
      Break
    EndIf
    ; chip.c line 921: a descriptor without the VALID bit is skipped.
    If (v & #CYW43_DMP_VALID) = 0
      Continue
    EndIf
    If t <> #CYW43_DMP_COMPONENT
      Continue
    EndIf

    id = (v & #CYW43_DMP_COMP_PARTNUM) >> #CYW43_DMP_COMP_PARTNUM_S

    ; The SECOND component word. chip.c lines 934-937 warn and bail if
    ; it is not another component descriptor, and so does this: the
    ; stream is malformed and everything after it is meaningless.
    v = cyw43_Erom(@cursor, @t, @ok)
    If ok = 0
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
    EndIf
    If t <> #CYW43_DMP_COMPONENT
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
    EndIf

    nmw = (v & #CYW43_DMP_COMP_NUM_MWRAP) >> #CYW43_DMP_COMP_NUM_MWRAP_S
    nsw = (v & #CYW43_DMP_COMP_NUM_SWRAP) >> #CYW43_DMP_COMP_NUM_SWRAP_S
    rev = (v & #CYW43_DMP_COMP_REVISION) >> #CYW43_DMP_COMP_REVISION_S

    ; chip.c lines 939-943: a component with no wrappers at all "does not
    ; need ports" and is skipped - UNLESS it is the PMU or the GCI, which
    ; source registers without owning a port.
    ;
    ; THE TWO EXCEPTIONS ARE IN AS OF 2026-08-26 AND THEY WERE OUT
    ; BEFORE. The old comment here said so and said why: BCMA_CORE_PMU
    ; and BCMA_CORE_GCI live in include/linux/bcma/bcma.h, that header
    ; was not on the disk, and a constant this file cannot cite is a
    ; constant this file does not write. It was fetched to
    ; RaspberryPi4/Reference/bcma.h on 2026-08-26 for the core-reset
    ; work, so the exception is now citable and is applied. The stated
    ; consequence of leaving it out - "a PMU or GCI core with no
    ; wrappers will be missed by this walk" - is retired with it.
    If (nmw + nsw) = 0
      If id <> #CYW43_CORE_PMU And id <> #CYW43_CORE_GCI
        Continue
      EndIf
    EndIf

    If cyw43_EromRegAddr(@cursor, @base, @wrap) = 0
      ; chip.c line 951 does "continue" on an error from this, because a
      ; core whose address cannot be worked out is a core to skip rather
      ; than a reason to abandon the chip. The difference here is that
      ; cyw43_EromRegAddr only fails on a BUS error, which is not
      ; recoverable, so this stops.
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf

    If cyw43_ncores >= #CYW43_MAX_CORES
      ; REFUSE, do not wrap and do not silently drop. A chip with more
      ; cores than this table holds is a chip this file has never seen,
      ; and quietly truncating the list is how a missing core becomes a
      ; mystery instead of a message.
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_TOO_MANY)
    EndIf

    cyw43_coreId[cyw43_ncores]   = id
    cyw43_coreRev[cyw43_ncores]  = rev
    cyw43_coreBase[cyw43_ncores] = base
    cyw43_coreWrap[cyw43_ncores] = wrap
    cyw43_ncores = cyw43_ncores + 1
  ForEver

  If cyw43_ncores = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_EROM)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_ncores
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  Cyw43Attach - everything this file can honestly do, in order.
;
;    1. the four primitives are present
;    2. CNTFRQ is usable - every bounded wait below depends on it
;    3. enable function 1, the backplane
;    4. set the two block sizes (bcmsdh.c lines 902 and 924)
;    5. enable function 2, the WLAN data function
;    6. the ALP clock sequence, INCLUDING the read-back that proves the
;       bus works
;    7. read and decode the chip ID
;    8. refuse anything that is not an AXI backplane
;    9. walk the EROM
;
;  STEP 5 LOOKS PREMATURE AND IS NOT. brcmfmac enables both functions
;  before touching the clock (bcmsdh.c lines 900-930, and the mmc core
;  probes both functions before the driver runs at all). Function 2 is
;  useless without firmware, but ENABLING it is how the card is told the
;  host intends to use it, and the CYW43's own housekeeping depends on
;  that having happened. Leaving it out is the sort of change that works
;  until it does not.
;
;  WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, and see THE WALL: it does not reset a core, it
;  does not touch the PMU, it does not upload anything, and it does not
;  take the internal ARM out of reset. Cyw43Attach() succeeding means
;  "the chip is identified", not "the radio is up".
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with cyw43_err set.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Cyw43Attach()
  Define blkLo.i
  Define blkHi.i
  Define blk.i
  Define caps.i

  cyw43_attached = 0
  cyw43_err = #CYW43_ERR_NONE
  cyw43_windowOk = 0
  cyw43_ncores = 0

  ; --- 1 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  If Cyw43IoReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
  EndIf

  ; --- 2 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  cyw43_hz = cyw43_TickHz()
  If cyw43_hz <= 0 Or cyw43_hz > #CYW43_HZ_MAX
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ)
  EndIf

  ; --- 3 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Function enable lives in sdio.pi4 because it is generic SDIO, and
  ; this file reaches it the same way it reaches everything else: not at
  ; all directly. There is no seam entry for it because it can be
  ; expressed with the two byte primitives - IOEx read-modify-write then
  ; poll IORx - which is what SdioEnableFunction does, and duplicating
  ; that here would be two implementations of one thing.
  ;
  ; SO THE CALLER MUST HAVE ENABLED THE FUNCTIONS, OR THIS FAILS AT THE
  ; CLOCK STEP with #CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE. That is a real seam in the
  ; contract and it is called out in Cyw43ErrorText().
  ;
  ; What IS done here is the block sizes, because those are the DEVICE's
  ; numbers and not the bus's.

  ; --- 4 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ; FBR block size, low byte then high byte, on function 0's register
  ; space. SDIO_FBR_BASE(f) = f * $100, SDIO_FBR_BLKSIZE = $10.
  If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, $110, #CYW43_F1_BLKSIZE & $FF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, $111, (#CYW43_F1_BLKSIZE >> 8) & $FF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, $210, #CYW43_F2_BLKSIZE & $FF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, $211, (#CYW43_F2_BLKSIZE >> 8) & $FF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf

  ; --- 4b, ADDED 2026-08-26 WITH BLOCK MODE ----------------------------
  ; READ FUNCTION 1'S BLOCK SIZE BACK OUT OF THE CARD, and use THAT
  ; number for every block-mode transfer rather than the constant just
  ; written.
  ;
  ; This is not belt-and-braces. A block-mode CMD53 does not carry the
  ; block size - the host controller counts bytes using SDHCI_BLOCK_SIZE
  ; and the card counts them using its own FBR register - so if the two
  ; ever differ the transfer completes with no error bit set anywhere and
  ; the data is misaligned by however much they disagreed. There is no
  ; symptom to catch later. The only defence is to take the number from
  ; the card, and that costs two CMD52 reads once per attach.
  cyw43_f1Blk = 0
  cyw43_byteMax = 0
  cyw43_f2Blk = 0
  cyw43_f2ByteMax = 0
  cyw43_multiBlock = 0
  cyw43_useBlocks = 0
  blkLo = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, $110)
  blkHi = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, $111)
  If blkLo < 0 Or blkHi < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  blk = ((blkHi & $FF) << 8) | (blkLo & $FF)
  If blk <> #CYW43_F1_BLKSIZE
    ; The card did not take the size it was given. Refuse rather than
    ; adopt whatever it did take: a block size this file did not choose
    ; is a block size nothing here has reasoned about.
    cyw43_f1Blk = blk
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  cyw43_f1Blk = blk

  ; --- 4c, THE BYTE-MODE CEILING ---------------------------------------
  ; sdio_max_byte_size(), v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_io.c lines 189-202, taking
  ; the branch that cannot be too large. See THE BYTE-MODE CEILING above
  ; cyw43_XferOne for the whole argument and for the bench run that
  ; forced it. 512 is the protocol's ceiling and 64 is this function's.
  cyw43_byteMax = cyw43_f1Blk
  If cyw43_byteMax > 512
    cyw43_byteMax = 512
  EndIf

  ; AND FUNCTION 2'S, SEPARATELY, because the ceiling belongs to the
  ; FUNCTION. F1 is 64 and F2 is 512, and using one for the other is
  ; the 2026-08-26 bug pointing the other way - it would cap every
  ; frame transfer at 64 bytes, which is not wrong, merely eight times
  ; slower, and would therefore never be noticed.
  blkLo = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, $210)
  blkHi = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, $211)
  If blkLo < 0 Or blkHi < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  blk = ((blkHi & $FF) << 8) | (blkLo & $FF)
  If blk <> #CYW43_F2_BLKSIZE
    cyw43_f2Blk = blk
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  cyw43_f2Blk = blk
  cyw43_f2ByteMax = blk
  If cyw43_f2ByteMax > 512
    cyw43_f2ByteMax = 512
  EndIf

  ; --- 4d, CAN THE CARD DO MULTI-BLOCK AT ALL --------------------------
  ; CCCR CAPS bit SMB, v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h lines 108 and 111. The mmc
  ; core records it as card->cccr.multi_block (core_sdio.c line 171) and
  ; sdio_io_rw_ext_helper (sdio_io.c line 228) gates the ENTIRE
  ; block-mode branch on it:
  ;     if (func->card->cccr.multi_block && (size > sdio_max_byte_size(func)))
  ;
  ; Read here rather than assumed, because a card that cannot do
  ; multi-block would answer a block-mode CMD53 with an R5 refusal - and
  ; this file has now seen exactly what that costs when it is not
  ; expected. If the bit is clear, block mode stays off and the byte-mode
  ; path carries everything, slowly and correctly.
  cyw43_multiBlock = 0
  caps = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_CCCR, #CYW43_CCCR_CAPS)
  If caps < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  If (caps & #CYW43_CCCR_CAP_SMB) <> 0
    cyw43_multiBlock = 1
  EndIf

  ; --- 6 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  If Cyw43ClockPrepare() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- 7 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  If cyw43_ReadChipId() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- 8 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ; chip.c lines 998-1003 refuse an SB chip that is not a 4329. This
  ; refuses every SB chip, because the 4329's hard-coded core map is not
  ; something this file carries and a part that reports SB here is not
  ; the part on this board.
  If cyw43_socitype <> #CYW43_SOCI_AI
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_SOCI)
  EndIf

  ; --- 9 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  If Cyw43EnumerateCores() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  cyw43_attached = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - what was found.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i Cyw43Attached()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_attached
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43LastError()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_err
EndProcedure

; The whole 32-bit chipid register, exactly as read.
Procedure.i Cyw43ChipIdRaw()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_chipid
EndProcedure

; CID_ID. $4345 for the part on a Pi 4 B - NOT 43455, see THE PART.
Procedure.i Cyw43ChipId()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_chip
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ChipRev()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_chiprev
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ChipPackage()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_chippkg
EndProcedure

; 0 = SB, 1 = AXI. Anything else is a chip this file refused.
Procedure.i Cyw43SocType()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_socitype
EndProcedure

; The core-count FIELD of chipid, which is the chip's own claim.
; DELIBERATELY SEPARATE FROM Cyw43CoreCount(), which is what the EROM
; walk actually found. If those two disagree, the walk is wrong or the
; chip is - and a single number would have hidden that.
Procedure.i Cyw43ChipCoreCountField()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_ccount
EndProcedure

; CHIPCLKCSR as last read. Bit $40 is ALP available, $80 is HT.
Procedure.i Cyw43ClockCsr()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_clkcsr
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43CoreCount()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_ncores
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43CoreId(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_ncores
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_coreId[n]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43CoreRev(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_ncores
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_coreRev[n]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43CoreBase(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_ncores
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_coreBase[n]
EndProcedure

; 0 means no wrapper was seen for this core - which is a legitimate
; answer, not an error. See cyw43_EromRegAddr.
Procedure.i Cyw43CoreWrap(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_ncores
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_coreWrap[n]
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43IsCyw43455() - is this the part a Pi 4 Model B carries?
;
;  The rule is sdio.c line 652's and nothing more: chip id $4345 with a
;  revision inside the mask $FFFFFDC0. That mask is a bitmap over
;  revision numbers, so "revision r matches" means bit r of the mask is
;  set. Revisions 0-5 and 9 are excluded; 6, 7, 8 and 10 upward are the
;  43455.
;
;  A revision of 9 on a $4345 is the CYW43456 (sdio.c line 651, mask
;  $00000200 = bit 9), which is a different part with different
;  firmware. Returning "yes, 43455" for it would be exactly the quiet
;  wrong answer this project refuses.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43IsCyw43455()
  If cyw43_chip <> #CYW43_CHIP_4345
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If cyw43_chiprev < 0 Or cyw43_chiprev > 31
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If ((#CYW43_REVMASK_43455 >> cyw43_chiprev) & 1) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ChipName() - a short name for the log.
;
;  ONLY THE PART THIS BOARD HAS IS NAMED. Every other id gets "unknown",
;  and there is no table of the other twenty chips in the family,
;  because a name is a claim and this file can only support one of them.
;  brcmf_chip_name() builds a name by printing the id in hex or decimal
;  depending on its magnitude (chip.c), which is a nicety this does not
;  need - Cyw43ChipIdRaw() is right there.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ChipName()
  If cyw43_chip = #CYW43_CHIP_4345
    If Cyw43IsCyw43455() = 1
      ProcedureReturn "CYW43455"
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn "BCM4345 (a revision that is not the 43455)"
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn "unknown - see Cyw43ChipIdRaw"
EndProcedure



; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  THE BULK PATH - moving more than four bytes across the backplane.
;
;  Everything above this point moves ONE 32-bit register at a time,
;  which is all chip identification needs. A firmware image is 609,309
;  bytes. At four bytes a command that is 152,328 commands, and a
;  command costs a bus turnaround whether it carries four bytes or four
;  hundred, so the difference is not a percentage.
;
;  THREE LIMITS SHAPE EVERY TRANSFER HERE and they are unrelated to each
;  other, which is why the chunking loop applies all three separately
;  rather than folding them into one number:
;
;    1. THE BACKPLANE WINDOW IS 32 KB. The SDIO address field carries
;       fifteen bits of offset (#CYW43_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK); anything above
;       that is in the three window registers, and those are written by
;       three separate CMD52s. A transfer may not cross a window
;       boundary, because the card would wrap back to the bottom of the
;       same window rather than moving on - silently. This is the limit
;       brcmf_sdiod_ramrw() spends most of its body on, bcmsdh.c lines
;       663-735.
;
;    2. ONE CMD53 CARRIES AT MOST 511 BLOCKS. sdio.pi4's
;       #SDIO_BLOCKS_MAX, from the nine-bit count field. At function 1's
;       64-byte block that is 32,704 bytes - just under the window, and
;       the two limits are close enough that both bite on a real image.
;
;    3. BYTE MODE CARRIES AT MOST 512 BYTES, and it is still needed even
;       with block mode available, for the 1 to 511 bytes at the end of
;       a transfer that are not a whole block. Both modes go through the
;       same chunker for that reason.
;
;  WHY BLOCK MODE IS OPTIONAL AND SEPARATELY INSTALLED. Cyw43SetIo's
;  four primitives are all-or-nothing because none of them is optional -
;  without any one of them nothing works at all. The block-mode pair is
;  genuinely optional: this file will move a firmware image without it,
;  in 512-byte byte-mode commands, roughly eight times slower. Making
;  them part of the mandatory set would have broken every existing
;  caller of Cyw43SetIo to buy nothing. So they have their own installer
;  and their own refusal.
;
;  AND THE BYTE-MODE FALLBACK IS NOT DEAD CODE. It is the control arm.
;  The only way to know that a block-mode transfer moved the right bytes
;  is to compare it against a byte-mode transfer of the same region, and
;  Cyw43SetBlockMode() exists so a diagnostic can run both over the same
;  address and diff them. A fallback that is exercised on every bench
;  run is a fallback that will still work the day it is needed.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetBlockIo - install the optional block-mode primitives.
;
;  Pass both or neither. Passing 0 for either clears both and turns
;  block mode off, which is a legitimate call: it is how a caller says
;  "use byte mode only" without having to remember whether it installed
;  them earlier.
;
;  Installing them does NOT by itself switch the bulk path over -
;  Cyw43SetBlockMode(1) does that, and Cyw43Attach calls it once it has
;  set and READ BACK function 1's block size. The two steps are separate
;  because the block size is the thing that can be wrong, and a transfer
;  whose two ends disagree about the block size fails with no error bit
;  anywhere. See the warning on SdioCmd53WriteBlocks.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SetBlockIo(*rdb, *wrb)
  If *rdb = 0 Or *wrb = 0
    *cyw43_rdb = 0
    *cyw43_wrb = 0
    cyw43_useBlocks = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  *cyw43_rdb = *rdb
  *cyw43_wrb = *wrb
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43BlockIoReady()
  If *cyw43_rdb = 0 Or *cyw43_wrb = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Turn the bulk path's use of block mode on or off. Returns the state it
; ended in, which is NOT always what was asked: asking for block mode
; without the seam installed, or without a block size agreed, leaves it
; off. A caller that assumed its request was honoured would be timing a
; byte-mode transfer and calling it a block-mode one.
Procedure.i Cyw43SetBlockMode(on.i)
  If on = 0
    cyw43_useBlocks = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If Cyw43BlockIoReady() = 0
    cyw43_useBlocks = 0
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_BLOCK_IO)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If cyw43_f1Blk < 4 Or cyw43_f1Blk > 512
    cyw43_useBlocks = 0
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; AND THE CARD MUST SAY IT CAN. CCCR CAPS bit SMB, read back in
  ; Cyw43Attach. Linux gates its entire block-mode branch on the same
  ; bit - sdio_io.c line 228 - and a card without it would answer a
  ; block-mode CMD53 with an R5 refusal. This file has now seen on a
  ; bench what an unexpected R5 refusal costs: it wedges the data line
  ; and every command after it fails for a different, louder and
  ; completely unrelated-looking reason. Refusing here is cheaper than
  ; diagnosing that a second time.
  If cyw43_multiBlock = 0
    cyw43_useBlocks = 0
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_BLOCK_IO)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  cyw43_useBlocks = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43BlockMode()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_useBlocks
EndProcedure

; The longest byte-mode transfer function 1 will accept - 64 on this
; part, not 512. Exposed so a diagnostic can PRINT it, because it is the
; number that was wrong on 2026-08-26 and a log that shows it cannot be
; wrong about it silently again.
Procedure.i Cyw43ByteMax()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_byteMax
EndProcedure

; Whether the card claims CCCR CAPS bit SMB, multi-block CMD53.
Procedure.i Cyw43MultiBlockCapable()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_multiBlock
EndProcedure

; The block size function 1 is actually using, as READ BACK from the
; card in Cyw43Attach. Zero means it was never agreed and block mode is
; therefore refused.
Procedure.i Cyw43F1BlockSize()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_f1Blk
EndProcedure

; The EROM pointer the core walk started from. Exposed for one purpose:
; it names a large, static, read-only region that two transfer modes can
; be compared over. See the note on cyw43_eromptr.
Procedure.i Cyw43EromPtr()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_eromptr
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE BYTE-MODE CEILING, AND IT IS NOT 512.
;  MEASURED ON SILICON 2026-08-26, THE HARD WAY.
; ======================================================================
;  This code originally chunked byte-mode transfers at 512, on the
;  reasoning that 512 is what the nine-bit length field can express. The
;  board disagreed on the first run: a 256-byte byte-mode read of
;  function 1 came back with an R5 REFUSAL - the card understood the
;  command and declined it.
;
;  512 IS THE PROTOCOL'S LIMIT. THE CARD'S IS SMALLER, and Linux never
;  hits it because sdio_max_byte_size() takes the minimum of three
;  bounds - v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_io.c lines 189-202:
;
;      unsigned mval = func->card->host->max_blk_size;
;      if (mmc_blksz_for_byte_mode(func->card))
;              mval = min(mval, func->cur_blksize);
;      else
;              mval = min(mval, func->max_blksize);
;      return min(mval, 512u);   /* maximum size for byte mode */
;
;  and sdio_io_rw_ext_helper() (lines 219-260) then splits every
;  transfer into block-mode commands of cur_blksize plus a byte-mode
;  remainder of at most that value. The structure below is that
;  function's, and the bug was that the remainder used 512 where Linux
;  uses sdio_max_byte_size().
;
;  WHICH BRANCH THIS FILE TAKES, AND WHY IT IS THE SMALLER ONE.
;  func->max_blksize comes from the function's CIS, which nothing in
;  this project parses, so the else-branch is not available to be
;  computed honestly. cur_blksize IS available - Cyw43Attach reads it
;  back out of the card's FBR - and it is provably no larger, because a
;  function's current block size cannot exceed its maximum. So
;  cyw43_byteMax is min(cur_blksize, 512), which is 64 on function 1.
;
;  THE COST IS SPEED AND ONLY SPEED. A byte-mode-only upload of the
;  609 KB image is now roughly 9,500 commands rather than 1,200. That is
;  the control arm, not the fast path - block mode is the fast path and
;  it moves 511 blocks at a time - and a slow arm that is certainly
;  correct is worth more than a fast one that the card refuses.
;
;  WHY THE FOUR-BYTE ACCESSES ALWAYS WORKED: 4 is under 64. Every CMD53
;  this project had ever issued before today was a single backplane
;  register, so the ceiling had never been anywhere near.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_XferOne - ONE CMD53 inside ONE window, at one already-masked
;  offset. Everything above it has already guaranteed that the transfer
;  fits.
;
;  write  1 = host to chip, 0 = chip to host
;  off    the offset within the window, 0..$7FFF, ALREADY masked
;  *p     host buffer, 4-byte aligned
;  n      bytes, a multiple of 4
;
;  THE SPLIT BETWEEN BLOCK AND BYTE HAPPENS HERE, not in the caller, so
;  that the caller's arithmetic is the same in both modes and there is
;  one place where a mode decision is made. n is broken into a whole
;  number of blocks plus a remainder; each part is at most one command.
;
;  incr IS 1 ON EVERY CALL. This path walks a memory region, so the
;  card's address must advance. incr = 0 is for draining a FIFO at a
;  fixed address and this is not that; the parameter is not exposed
;  because there is no correct value of it here other than 1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_XferOne(write.i, off.i, *p, n.i)
  Define blocks.i
  Define whole.i
  Define rest.i
  Define addr.i
  Define q.i
  Define left.i

  addr = off | #CYW43_SB_ACCESS_2_4B
  q = *p
  left = n

  ; Whole blocks first, at most #CYW43_BLOCKS_PER_CMD to a command, and
  ; as many commands as it takes. The loop is here rather than in the
  ; caller because none of these commands crosses a window boundary -
  ; the caller has already guaranteed that - so the window registers do
  ; not move and there is nothing for the caller to redo between them.
  If cyw43_useBlocks <> 0
    While left >= cyw43_f1Blk
      blocks = left / cyw43_f1Blk
      If blocks > #CYW43_BLOCKS_PER_CMD
        blocks = #CYW43_BLOCKS_PER_CMD
      EndIf
      whole = blocks * cyw43_f1Blk
      If write <> 0
        If cyw43_wrb(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, addr, q, cyw43_f1Blk, blocks, 1) <> 1
          ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_XFER)
        EndIf
      Else
        If cyw43_rdb(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, addr, q, cyw43_f1Blk, blocks, 1) <> 1
          ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_XFER)
        EndIf
      EndIf
      q = q + whole
      addr = addr + whole
      left = left - whole
    Wend
  EndIf

  ; The remainder, in byte mode, in cyw43_byteMax-sized pieces. In block
  ; mode that remainder is whatever is left below one block; in byte
  ; mode it is the entire transfer.
  While left > 0
    rest = left
    If rest > cyw43_byteMax
      rest = cyw43_byteMax
    EndIf
    If write <> 0
      If cyw43_wr(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, addr, q, rest, 1) <> 1
        ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_XFER)
      EndIf
    Else
      If cyw43_rd(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, addr, q, rest, 1) <> 1
        ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_XFER)
      EndIf
    EndIf
    q = q + rest
    addr = addr + rest
    left = left - rest
  Wend

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_Xfer - the windowing loop. brcmf_sdiod_ramrw(), bcmsdh.c lines
;  663-735, with its one real subtlety kept and its scatter-gather
;  dropped.
;
;  THE SUBTLETY IS THE FIRST CHUNK. Every chunk after the first starts
;  at a window boundary and may run the full 32 KB; the first starts
;  wherever the caller's address happens to sit inside its window and
;  may therefore be much shorter. brcmfmac computes that first length
;  before the loop (lines 681-685) and then sets sdaddr to 0 at the
;  bottom of each pass (line 727). This does the same arithmetic each
;  time round instead, from the address itself, because a value carried
;  in a variable across a loop is a value that can be stale and this
;  one cannot.
;
;  addr is a full 32-bit BACKPLANE address, not an offset.
;  n must be a multiple of 4 and the buffer 4-byte aligned - the same
;  contract sdio.pi4's CMD53 imposes, restated here so the refusal
;  happens before any bus traffic rather than three commands in.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_Xfer(write.i, addr.i, *buf, n.i)
  Define off.i
  Define room.i
  Define chunk.i
  Define done.i

  If Cyw43IoReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
  EndIf
  ; AND THE BYTE-MODE CEILING MUST BE KNOWN, which it is not until
  ; Cyw43Attach has read function 1's block size back. This is a
  ; refusal rather than a default because the loop in cyw43_XferOne
  ; divides the remainder into cyw43_byteMax-sized pieces, and a zero
  ; there is not a slow transfer, it is a transfer of zero bytes
  ; repeated for ever with the bus untouched. A hang with no output is
  ; the worst failure this file could produce.
  If cyw43_byteMax < 4
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  If *buf = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf
  If (*buf & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_ALIGN)
  EndIf
  If (addr & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_ALIGN)
  EndIf
  If n < 0 Or (n & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf
  If n = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  done = 0
  While done < n
    If cyw43_SetWindow(addr + done) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf

    off = (addr + done) & #CYW43_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK

    ; How much of this window is left in front of us.
    room = #CYW43_SB_OFT_ADDR_LIMIT - off
    chunk = n - done
    If chunk > room
      chunk = room
    EndIf
    ; NO CAP BEYOND THE WINDOW, IN EITHER MODE. cyw43_XferOne issues as
    ; many commands as the chunk needs - block-mode commands of up to
    ; 511 blocks, then byte-mode commands of up to cyw43_byteMax - and
    ; none of them moves the window, so there is nothing for this loop
    ; to do between them. It used to cap byte mode at 512 here, which
    ; was both wrong (see THE BYTE-MODE CEILING) and redundant.

    If cyw43_XferOne(write, off, *buf + done, chunk) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    done = done + chunk
  Wend

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PUBLIC - the three bulk operations.
;
;  Cyw43WriteMem / Cyw43ReadMem move n bytes to or from a backplane
;  address. Cyw43VerifyMem reads a region back and compares it with what
;  was supposed to be there, returning the number of mismatched BYTES.
;
;  VERIFY IS NOT OPTIONAL ON A BUS THAT HAS NEVER MOVED BULK DATA, and
;  brcmfmac agrees more than it looks: brcmf_sdio_verifymemory is
;  compiled out unless DEBUG is set (sdio.c lines 3292-3343), which is a
;  choice a driver with fifteen years of field time is entitled to make
;  and this one is not. Every upload here is verified until a bench log
;  says it does not need to be.
;
;  IT NEEDS A SCRATCH BUFFER and takes one from the caller rather than
;  owning one, because the size is a policy decision: brcmfmac uses
;  2048 (#CYW43_MEMBLOCK) and there is no reason this file should insist
;  on that number for a caller with different memory. A buffer smaller
;  than 4 bytes, or not 4-aligned, is refused.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43WriteMem(addr.i, *src, n.i)
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_Xfer(1, addr, *src, n)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ReadMem(addr.i, *dst, n.i)
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_Xfer(0, addr, *dst, n)
EndProcedure

; Returns the number of mismatched bytes, or -1 if the read itself
; failed. ZERO AND -1 ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS and the caller must tell
; them apart: 0 means "checked and correct", -1 means "not checked".
Procedure.i Cyw43VerifyMem(addr.i, *src, n.i, *scratch, scratchLen.i)
  Define done.i
  Define chunk.i
  Define i.i
  Define got.i
  Define want.i
  Define bad.i

  If *scratch = 0 Or scratchLen < 4
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If (*scratch & 3) <> 0
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_ALIGN)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  bad = 0
  done = 0
  While done < n
    chunk = n - done
    If chunk > scratchLen
      chunk = scratchLen
    EndIf
    ; Down to a whole number of words - the bus cannot move anything
    ; else, and a scratch buffer of, say, 2050 bytes must not make the
    ; last read of every chunk illegal.
    chunk = chunk & (~3)
    If chunk = 0
      cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf

    If cyw43_Xfer(0, addr + done, *scratch, chunk) = 0
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf

    For i = 0 To chunk - 1
      got = PeekA(*scratch + i)
      want = PeekA(*src + done + i)
      If got <> want
        If cyw43_badFound = 0
          cyw43_badFound = 1
          cyw43_badAddr = addr + done + i
          cyw43_badGot = got
          cyw43_badWant = want
        EndIf
        bad = bad + 1
      EndIf
    Next

    done = done + chunk
  Wend

  ProcedureReturn bad
EndProcedure

; Clear the first-mismatch record. Called at the start of each upload so
; that a bad NVRAM readback names an NVRAM address and not whatever the
; firmware pass happened to leave behind.
Procedure Cyw43ClearVerifyRecord()
  cyw43_badFound = 0
  cyw43_badAddr = 0
  cyw43_badGot = 0
  cyw43_badWant = 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43BadAddr()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_badAddr
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43BadGot()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_badGot
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43BadWant()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_badWant
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  CORES - finding one, holding it in reset, letting it go.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43FindCore(id) - index into the walk's table, or -1.
;
;  Returns the FIRST match. A 4345 has one of each core this file cares
;  about; a part with two 802.11 cores exists (brcmf_chip_get_d11core
;  takes an index for exactly that reason, chip.c) and is not this part.
;  If one ever is, this returns the first and the second is invisible -
;  stated rather than guarded, because a guard for a case that cannot
;  arise here could not be tested.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43FindCore(id.i)
  Define i.i
  For i = 0 To cyw43_ncores - 1
    If cyw43_coreId[i] = id
      ProcedureReturn i
    EndIf
  Next
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_CoreDisable - brcmf_chip_ai_coredisable(), chip.c lines
;  349-381. Put a core into reset and leave it there, configured.
;
;  THE EARLY EXIT MATTERS. If RESET_CTL already reads RESET, the core is
;  already down and the "configure reset, assert reset, wait" half is
;  SKIPPED - but the in-reset configure at the bottom still runs. A
;  version that returned early from the whole procedure would leave the
;  IOCTL register holding whatever the last caller wanted, which for the
;  ARM is the difference between coming up halted and coming up running.
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico lines 2158-2164 records the same trap from the
;  other direction: the pico-sdk driver's disable_device_core FAILS when
;  the core is already in reset, and only works at all because a fresh
;  power cycle leaves it that way.
;
;  BCMA_IOCTL_FGC | BCMA_IOCTL_CLK is OR'd into both IOCTL writes, which
;  is what forces the core's clocks on while its reset is being moved.
;  Every write is followed by a read of the same register - that is
;  brcmfmac's line and it is a POSTED-WRITE FLUSH, not a check: the read
;  cannot complete until the write has landed, and on a bus with a
;  32 KB window and a three-register address latch that ordering is not
;  free.
;
;  idx is an index into the core table, not a core id.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_CoreDisable(idx.i, prereset.i, reset.i)
  Define wrap.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define v.i
  Define ok.l

  If idx < 0 Or idx >= cyw43_ncores
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE)
  EndIf
  wrap = cyw43_coreWrap[idx]
  If wrap = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_WRAPPER)
  EndIf

  v = cyw43_Read32Raw(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_CTL, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If (v & #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_ACTIVE) = 0
    ; Not in reset yet, so put it there.
    If Cyw43Write32(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL, prereset | #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_FGC | #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_CLK) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    Cyw43Read32(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL)

    If Cyw43Write32(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_CTL, #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_ACTIVE) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    ; usleep_range(10, 20) at chip.c line 371.
    cyw43_DelayUs(20)

    ; SPINWAIT for RESET_CTL to read exactly RESET, 300 us - chip.c
    ; lines 374-375. brcmfmac does NOT treat the timeout as an error and
    ; neither does this: it falls through to the in-reset configure
    ; either way, because the configure is what the next step depends on
    ; and a core that has not latched its reset bit yet will have by the
    ; time three more CMD52s have gone by.
    ;
    ; The budget is in TICKS and cyw43_hz is CNTFRQ, which is 54 MHz on
    ; this part, so 300 microseconds is (hz / 1000000) * 300. Written
    ; that way round rather than as hz * 300 / 1000000 because the first
    ; form cannot overflow and the second does not need to be checked.
    budget = (cyw43_hz / 1000000) * 300
    If budget < 1
      budget = 1
    EndIf
    t0 = cyw43_Ticks()
    While (cyw43_Ticks() - t0) < budget
      v = cyw43_Read32Raw(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_CTL, @ok)
      If ok = 0
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
      If v = #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_ACTIVE
        Break
      EndIf
    Wend
  EndIf

  ; The in-reset configure. Reached from both branches, deliberately.
  If Cyw43Write32(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL, reset | #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_FGC | #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_CLK) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  Cyw43Read32(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_CoreReset - brcmf_chip_ai_resetcore(), chip.c lines 434-495,
;  minus the two-D11-core special case this part does not have.
;
;  Disable first, unconditionally: "must disable first to work for
;  arbitrary current core state" is brcmfmac's comment at line 454 and
;  it is the whole reason the procedure takes three separate register
;  values rather than one. Then write 0 to RESET_CTL until the reset bit
;  reads clear, at most fifty times with a 40-60 us gap; then the
;  post-reset IOCTL with the clock left enabled but the force-gated-clock
;  bit NOT set.
;
;  FGC IS ABSENT FROM THE FINAL WRITE ON PURPOSE. chip.c line 484 is
;  "postreset | BCMA_IOCTL_CLK" - no FGC. Forcing the clock on for ever
;  is a bring-up measure, not a running state, and copying the earlier
;  line here would leave the core burning power with no symptom.
;
;  RETURNS 0 IF THE CORE NEVER LEAVES RESET. brcmfmac breaks out of the
;  loop and carries on regardless (line 470's "if (count > 50) break"),
;  which is defensible in a driver that has other error paths and is not
;  defensible here: the only reason this file releases a core is to run
;  firmware on it, and a core still in reset will look exactly like a
;  firmware that did not boot.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_CoreReset(idx.i, prereset.i, reset.i, postreset.i)
  Define wrap.i
  Define tries.i
  Define v.i
  Define ok.l

  If cyw43_CoreDisable(idx, prereset, reset) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  wrap = cyw43_coreWrap[idx]

  tries = 0
  Repeat
    v = cyw43_Read32Raw(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_CTL, @ok)
    If ok = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If (v & #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_ACTIVE) = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    If tries > 50
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CORE_RESET)
    EndIf
    If Cyw43Write32(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_CTL, 0) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    tries = tries + 1
    cyw43_DelayUs(60)
  ForEver

  If Cyw43Write32(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL, postreset | #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_CLK) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  Cyw43Read32(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43CoreIsUp - brcmf_chip_ai_iscoreup(), chip.c. A core is up when
;  its IOCTL has the clock bit set and the force-gated-clock bit clear,
;  AND its RESET_CTL has the reset bit clear. Both halves are required
;  and the second is the one people forget.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43CoreIsUp(idx.i)
  Define wrap.i
  Define v.i
  Define ok.l

  If idx < 0 Or idx >= cyw43_ncores
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE)
  EndIf
  wrap = cyw43_coreWrap[idx]
  If wrap = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_WRAPPER)
  EndIf

  v = cyw43_Read32Raw(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (v & (#CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_FGC | #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_CLK)) <> #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL_CLK
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  v = cyw43_Read32Raw(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_CTL, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (v & #CYW43_BCMA_RESET_ACTIVE) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  RAM - where the image goes and how much room there is.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ReadRamInfo - brcmf_chip_get_raminfo(), chip.c lines 757-810,
;  reduced to the branch this part takes.
;
;  THE BRANCH IS DECIDED BY WHICH CPU CORE EXISTS, and on this board the
;  EROM walk answers that question: an ARM CR4 at $18002000 and no
;  INTERNAL_MEM core at all. chip.c line 762 asks for the CR4 first, and
;  when it is there the size comes from the CR4's own bank registers and
;  the base from a per-chip table. The SOCSRAM path (line 791), which is
;  what a 43439 takes and what RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico implements, is NOT
;  reached here and is not written - a branch that cannot run on the one
;  part this file supports is a branch nobody can test.
;
;  THE SIZE IS WALKED, NOT LOOKED UP. ARMCR4_CAP says how many banks
;  there are, split into A-banks and B-banks; each bank is then selected
;  by writing its index to BANKIDX and its size read out of BANKINFO.
;  The unit is 8192 bytes, or 1024 if the bank's 1K flag is set, and the
;  field is a count MINUS ONE - chip.c line 704 is
;      memsize += ((bxinfo & ARMCR4_BSZ_MASK) + 1) * blksize;
;  and the "+ 1" is the part that is easy to drop.
;
;  THE BASE IS LOOKED UP, because it is not in any register: chip.c
;  lines 710-752 is a switch on the chip id and nothing else. $198000
;  for a $4345. This file refuses any other chip id rather than falling
;  through to a default, which is what INVALID_RAMBASE means at line 752.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with cyw43_err set.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ReadRamInfo()
  Define idx.i
  Define base.i
  Define cap.i
  Define nab.i
  Define nbb.i
  Define totb.i
  Define i.i
  Define bx.i
  Define blk.i
  Define total.i
  Define ok.l

  cyw43_rambase = 0
  cyw43_ramsize = 0

  If cyw43_chip <> #CYW43_CHIP_4345
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_RAMINFO)
  EndIf

  idx = Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_ARM_CR4)
  If idx < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE)
  EndIf
  ; The CAP and BANK registers are at the core's REGISTER base, unlike
  ; every reset register above, which is at the wrapper. chip.c reaches
  ; them through brcmf_chip_core_read32(), which adds core->pub.base.
  base = cyw43_coreBase[idx]
  If base = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE)
  EndIf

  cap = cyw43_Read32Raw(base + #CYW43_ARMCR4_CAP, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  nab = (cap & #CYW43_ARMCR4_TCBANB_MASK) >> #CYW43_ARMCR4_TCBANB_S
  nbb = (cap & #CYW43_ARMCR4_TCBBNB_MASK) >> #CYW43_ARMCR4_TCBBNB_S
  totb = nab + nbb

  total = 0
  For i = 0 To totb - 1
    If Cyw43Write32(base + #CYW43_ARMCR4_BANKIDX, i) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    bx = cyw43_Read32Raw(base + #CYW43_ARMCR4_BANKINFO, @ok)
    If ok = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    blk = #CYW43_ARMCR4_BSZ_MULT
    If (bx & #CYW43_ARMCR4_BLK_1K) <> 0
      blk = blk >> 3
    EndIf
    total = total + (((bx & #CYW43_ARMCR4_BSZ_MASK) + 1) * blk)
  Next

  ; chip.c lines 799-807: zero is fatal and so is anything above 4 MB.
  ; Both are kept because both are the shape a failed backplane read
  ; takes - all-zero descriptors give 0, all-ones give something absurd -
  ; and a RAM size that is wrong is a firmware upload into nowhere.
  If total <= 0 Or total > #CYW43_MAX_MEMSIZE
    cyw43_ramsize = total
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_RAMINFO)
  EndIf

  cyw43_rambase = #CYW43_RAMBASE_4345
  cyw43_ramsize = total
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43RamBase()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rambase
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43RamSize()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_ramsize
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  THE DOWNLOAD - what used to be THE WALL.
;
;  The sequence is brcmf_sdio_download_firmware(), sdio.c lines
;  3384-3421, with brcmf_chip_set_passive() and brcmf_chip_set_active()
;  from chip.c either side of it:
;
;     1. put the ARM CR4 down, HALTED, and the 802.11 cores with it
;        (brcmf_chip_cr4_set_passive, chip.c lines 1306-1321)
;     2. ask the chip how much RAM it has        Cyw43ReadRamInfo
;     3. take the reset vector from the image's first four bytes
;     4. write the image at the RAM base          Cyw43UploadFirmware
;     5. read it back and compare - NOT OPTIONAL here, see below
;     6. cook the NVRAM text and write it at the TOP of RAM, with the
;        token the firmware uses to find it      Cyw43UploadNvram
;     7. clear the SDIO core's interrupts, write the reset vector to
;        backplane address 0, and let the ARM go  Cyw43Start
;     8. force the HT clock on                    Cyw43HtClock
;     9. announce the host protocol version and enable function 2 -
;        F2 GOING READY IS THE FIRMWARE SAYING IT BOOTED
;
;  THE ORDER OF 1 AND 2 IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. chip.c line 1045 calls
;  the bus reset and brcmf_chip_set_passive() and only THEN line 1048
;  calls brcmf_chip_get_raminfo(). The CR4's bank registers are behind
;  the CR4, and reading them before the core has been brought out of
;  reset halted reads nothing useful. Doing it in the other order was
;  the obvious arrangement and it is the wrong one.
;
;  WHY VERIFY IS ON HERE WHEN LINUX COMPILES IT OUT. sdio.c lines
;  3292-3343 define brcmf_sdio_verifymemory twice: the real one under
;  #ifdef DEBUG and a "return true" for everybody else. A driver with
;  fifteen years of field time is entitled to that. This one has moved
;  bulk data across this bus for the first time today, using a CMD53
;  mode written this afternoon, and the failure it is looking for -
;  block mode moving the right number of bytes to the wrong offset -
;  produces a firmware that does not boot and says nothing about why.
;  Verification roughly doubles the upload time and buys the difference
;  between "the image is wrong at $0019A840" and "F2 never came ready".
;
;  THIS FILE DOES NOT OWN THE IMAGE and does not read it from anywhere.
;  Cyw43UploadFirmware takes a pointer and a length. Where they came
;  from - a FAT32 read through fat.pi4, a serial transfer into RAM, a
;  DataSection - is the caller's business, and every one of those is a
;  609 KB question that has nothing to do with the radio.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43PrepareDownload - step 1 and step 2 together, because doing
;  either alone is never useful.
;
;  brcmf_chip_cr4_set_passive(), chip.c lines 1306-1321:
;
;      brcmf_chip_disable_arm(chip, BCMA_CORE_ARM_CR4);
;      for (i = 0; (core = brcmf_chip_get_d11core(&chip->pub, i)); i++)
;              brcmf_chip_coredisable(core, D11_BCMA_IOCTL_PHYRESET |
;                                     D11_BCMA_IOCTL_PHYCLOCKEN,
;                                     D11_BCMA_IOCTL_PHYCLOCKEN);
;
;  and brcmf_chip_disable_arm's CR4 branch, chip.c lines 1065-1076:
;
;      val = read32(cpu->wrapbase + BCMA_IOCTL);
;      val &= ARMCR4_BCMA_IOCTL_CPUHALT;
;      brcmf_chip_resetcore(core, val, ARMCR4_BCMA_IOCTL_CPUHALT,
;                                      ARMCR4_BCMA_IOCTL_CPUHALT);
;
;  READ THE MASKING. The prereset value is the CURRENT IOCTL with every
;  bit but CPUHALT cleared, which is not the same as CPUHALT: if the
;  core is currently running, prereset is 0 and the core is configured
;  with its halt bit CLEAR while it is being put into reset. The reset
;  and post-reset values are both CPUHALT, so it comes out of reset
;  halted either way. Substituting CPUHALT for the masked read - the
;  obvious simplification - changes what the core sees during the
;  transition, and this file does not know what that costs.
;
;  THE D11 CORES ARE DISABLED, NOT RESET, and chip.c's comment at lines
;  1311-1313 says why in so many words: "Disable the cores only and let
;  the firmware enable them. Releasing reset ourselves breaks BCM4387 in
;  weird ways." That is a bug report standing in for a specification and
;  it is followed exactly.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with cyw43_err set.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43PrepareDownload()
  Define idx.i
  Define wrap.i
  Define val.i
  Define ok.l

  If cyw43_attached = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NOT_ATTACHED)
  EndIf

  idx = Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_ARM_CR4)
  If idx < 0
    ; No CR4 means this is not the part this file supports. A 43439 has
    ; a CM3 and a SOCSRAM core and takes a completely different path -
    ; see Cyw43ReadRamInfo - which is not written here.
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE)
  EndIf
  wrap = cyw43_coreWrap[idx]
  If wrap = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_WRAPPER)
  EndIf

  val = cyw43_Read32Raw(wrap + #CYW43_BCMA_IOCTL, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  val = val & #CYW43_ARMCR4_CPUHALT

  If cyw43_CoreReset(idx, val, #CYW43_ARMCR4_CPUHALT, #CYW43_ARMCR4_CPUHALT) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; The 802.11 core, if there is one. There is exactly one on this part
  ; and the walk found it at $18001000; a chip with two is handled by
  ; brcmf_chip_get_d11core's index loop and is not this chip, so this
  ; disables the first and only.
  idx = Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_80211)
  If idx >= 0
    If cyw43_CoreDisable(idx, #CYW43_D11_PHYRESET | #CYW43_D11_PHYCLOCKEN, #CYW43_D11_PHYCLOCKEN) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; And only now is the CR4 in a state where its bank registers mean
  ; anything. See the note at the top of this section about the order.
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43ReadRamInfo()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43UploadFirmware - steps 3, 4 and 5.
;
;  *image / length are the file exactly as it came off the disk. The
;  reset vector is its first four bytes, little-endian - sdio.c line
;  3394, get_unaligned_le32(fw->data) - and it is kept here rather than
;  returned, because Cyw43Start is the only thing that may use it and a
;  caller holding a reset vector is a caller that can pass the wrong one.
;
;  THE LENGTH IS ROUNDED UP TO A MULTIPLE OF FOUR AND THE PAD COMES OUT
;  OF THE CALLER'S BUFFER. brcmfmac hands its exact 609,309 bytes to the
;  MMC layer, which can move an odd tail in byte mode; this bus moves
;  whole 32-bit words through a 32-bit FIFO and cannot. So 609,309
;  becomes 609,312 and three bytes that are past the end of the image
;  are read from wherever the caller's buffer continues and written into
;  chip RAM immediately above the image.
;
;  THAT IS SAFE AND HERE IS WHY, because "three stray bytes into a radio"
;  deserves an argument rather than a shrug. The bytes land at
;  rambase + 609,309 .. + 609,311. The firmware's own image ends at
;  609,308. The NVRAM lives at the very TOP of RAM - rambase + ramsize -
;  1748 on this board - which is hundreds of kilobytes above. Nothing
;  reads the gap between them. The caller is still told to zero those
;  three bytes, because "harmless today" and "correct" are different
;  words and a future image whose length is a multiple of four would
;  make this note stale without making it wrong.
;
;  *scratch / scratchLen are the read-back compare buffer. 2048 is
;  brcmfmac's size (#CYW43_MEMBLOCK) and any 4-aligned buffer of 4 bytes
;  or more will do, more slowly.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with cyw43_err set. On a verify failure the error is
;  #CYW43_ERR_VERIFY and Cyw43BadAddr / Cyw43BadGot / Cyw43BadWant name
;  the first byte that disagreed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE UPLOAD READ-BACK VERIFY IS THE SLOW PART, not the write. The write
; is ~19 window chunks of block-mode CMD53; the full verify reads all
; 609 KB back AND compares it byte by byte with the MMU off - about 1.2
; million uncached reads, ten seconds of them. Mode 2 verifies only a
; head and tail window: the reset vector lives at offset 0 and a
; truncation shows at the end, and a mid-image SDIO glitch is caught
; downstream when the firmware fails to bring function 2 ready. Mode 0
; is off; mode 1 is the full check, still the default for a caller that
; asks for it.
Global cyw43_verifyMode.i = 1
Procedure Cyw43SetVerify(mode.i)
  cyw43_verifyMode = mode
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43UploadFirmware(*image, length.i, *scratch, scratchLen.i)
  Define padded.i
  Define bad.i
  Define hw.i
  Define toff.i

  cyw43_fwLoaded = 0
  cyw43_rstvec = 0
  Cyw43ClearVerifyRecord()

  If cyw43_attached = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NOT_ATTACHED)
  EndIf
  If cyw43_rambase = 0 Or cyw43_ramsize = 0
    ; Cyw43PrepareDownload has not run, so there is no RAM base to write
    ; to. Refusing is the only safe answer: $198000 is in a constant six
    ; lines away and using it without the bank walk would mean writing
    ; 609 KB into an address range nobody has confirmed exists.
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_RAMINFO)
  EndIf
  If *image = 0 Or length < 4
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf
  If (*image & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_ALIGN)
  EndIf

  padded = (length + 3) & (~3)
  If padded > cyw43_ramsize
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf

  ; PeekN, not PeekL and not four PeekAs: the image is 4-aligned by the
  ; refusal above, and PeekN is the unsigned 32-bit read, so a reset
  ; vector with bit 31 set - which this board's is, $B83EF198 - does not
  ; come back negative and sign-extended across a 64-bit register.
  cyw43_rstvec = PeekN(*image)

  If Cyw43WriteMem(cyw43_rambase, *image, padded) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  bad = 0
  If cyw43_verifyMode = 1
    bad = Cyw43VerifyMem(cyw43_rambase, *image, padded, *scratch, scratchLen)
  ElseIf cyw43_verifyMode = 2
    hw = 16384
    If (hw * 2) >= padded
      bad = Cyw43VerifyMem(cyw43_rambase, *image, padded, *scratch, scratchLen)
    Else
      bad = Cyw43VerifyMem(cyw43_rambase, *image, hw, *scratch, scratchLen)
      If bad = 0
        toff = (padded - hw) & (~3)
        bad = Cyw43VerifyMem(cyw43_rambase + toff, *image + toff, hw, *scratch, scratchLen)
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If bad < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If bad <> 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_VERIFY)
  EndIf

  cyw43_fwLoaded = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ResetVector()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rstvec
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_NvramCook - turn the board's .txt into the byte image the
;  firmware expects, in a caller-supplied buffer.
;
;  brcmf_fw_nvram_strip(), firmware.c lines 396-448, driving the state
;  machine at lines 87-185. What it does, in one paragraph: every
;  "key=value" line becomes a NUL-terminated "key=value" string with no
;  newline; comments (from '#' to end of line) and blank lines vanish;
;  the result is padded with NULs to a multiple of four - roundup(len+1,
;  4), so there is ALWAYS at least one trailing NUL - and a four-byte
;  token is appended.
;
;  THE TOKEN IS THE WHOLE POINT, firmware.c lines 441-446:
;      token = *new_length / 4;
;      token = (~token << 16) | (token & 0x0000FFFF);
;  A word count in the low half and its bitwise complement in the high
;  half. That pair, sitting at the very top of the radio's RAM, is how
;  the firmware finds the NVRAM at all. Get it wrong and the radio boots
;  with no calibration and does not complain.
;
;  WHAT THIS PARSER DOES NOT DO, stated so nobody assumes it does:
;
;    * no devpath / "pcie/" multi-device stripping (firmware.c lines
;      249-370). Those exist for boards carrying several radios in one
;      NVRAM file. This board's file has no devpath line - it is a
;      single-device file cloned from bcm94345wlpagb_p2xx.txt - so the
;      whole mechanism is unreachable here, and an implementation of it
;      could not be tested against anything on this disk.
;
;    * no boardrev default (brcmf_fw_add_defaults, firmware.c lines
;      371-378). That adds "boardrev=0x1100" only when the file did not
;      supply one, and this file supplies "boardrev=0x1304". The
;      condition is checked below anyway and the cook is REFUSED if it
;      is ever false, rather than silently producing an NVRAM image
;      missing a key the firmware wants: a refusal names the problem, a
;      default invents an answer for a board nobody here has.
;
;    * no platform MAC override (brcmf_fw_add_macaddr). There is no
;      platform MAC source on this machine to override it with.
;
;    * RAW1 is treated as a comment, which IS implemented, because it is
;      three lines and firmware.c line 118 does it.
;
;  outMax must be at least length + 8: the cooked form is never longer
;  than the input (it only ever deletes characters), plus up to three
;  pad NULs and four token bytes. The buffer must be 4-aligned because
;  the token is written as a 32-bit word and the whole thing is then
;  handed to the bulk path.
;
;  Returns the cooked length INCLUDING the token, or 0 with cyw43_err
;  set.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_NvramCook(*text, length.i, *out, outMax.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Define c.i
  Define start.i
  Define j.i
  Define state.i
  Define isPrint.i
  Define isWhite.i
  Define boardrev.i
  Define padded.i
  Define words.i
  Define token.i

  If *text = 0 Or length <= 0
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NVRAM)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If *out = 0 Or outMax < length + 8
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (*out & 3) <> 0
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_ALIGN)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; state 0 = IDLE, 1 = KEY, 2 = VALUE, 3 = COMMENT. The same four the
  ; kernel's nv_parser_states table has, minus END, which here is just
  ; running out of input.
  state = 0
  n = 0
  start = 0
  boardrev = 0
  i = 0

  While i < length
    c = PeekA(*text + i)

    ; is_nvram_char(), firmware.c lines 72-80.
    isPrint = 0
    If c >= #CYW43_CH_PRINT_LO And c <= #CYW43_CH_PRINT_HI
      If c <> #CYW43_CH_HASH
        isPrint = 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
    ; is_whitespace(), firmware.c lines 82-85.
    isWhite = 0
    If c = #CYW43_CH_SPACE Or c = #CYW43_CH_CR
      isWhite = 1
    EndIf
    If c = #CYW43_CH_LF Or c = #CYW43_CH_TAB
      isWhite = 1
    EndIf

    Select state

      Case 0
        ; IDLE, firmware.c lines 87-108.
        If c = #CYW43_CH_LF
          state = 3
        ElseIf isWhite = 1 Or c = #CYW43_CH_NUL
          i = i + 1
        ElseIf c = #CYW43_CH_HASH
          state = 3
        ElseIf isPrint = 1
          start = i
          state = 1
        Else
          ; An unprintable byte outside a comment. firmware.c line 102
          ; logs "ignoring invalid character" and steps over it.
          i = i + 1
        EndIf

      Case 1
        ; KEY, firmware.c lines 110-140.
        If c = #CYW43_CH_EQUALS
          state = 2
          ; RAW1 is treated as a comment - firmware.c lines 117-120.
          If (i - start) = 4
            If PeekA(*text + start) = 82 And PeekA(*text + start + 1) = 65
              If PeekA(*text + start + 2) = 87 And PeekA(*text + start + 3) = 49
                state = 3
              EndIf
            EndIf
          EndIf
          ; Note whether the file supplies its own boardrev. Eight
          ; characters, compared by code: b o a r d r e v.
          If (i - start) = 8
            If PeekA(*text + start) = 98 And PeekA(*text + start + 1) = 111
              If PeekA(*text + start + 2) = 97 And PeekA(*text + start + 3) = 114
                If PeekA(*text + start + 4) = 100 And PeekA(*text + start + 5) = 114
                  If PeekA(*text + start + 6) = 101 And PeekA(*text + start + 7) = 118
                    boardrev = 1
                  EndIf
                EndIf
              EndIf
            EndIf
          EndIf
          i = i + 1
        ElseIf isPrint = 0 Or c = #CYW43_CH_SPACE
          ; firmware.c lines 131-135: "'=' expected, skip invalid key
          ; entry". A space inside a key is not a key.
          state = 3
        Else
          i = i + 1
        EndIf

      Case 2
        ; VALUE, firmware.c lines 142-168. The entry ends at the first
        ; character that is not an nvram char, and the whole
        ; "key=value" span is copied out with a NUL after it.
        If isPrint = 0
          For j = start To i - 1
            PokeB(*out + n, PeekA(*text + j))
            n = n + 1
          Next
          PokeB(*out + n, #CYW43_CH_NUL)
          n = n + 1
          state = 0
        Else
          i = i + 1
        EndIf

      Case 3
        ; COMMENT, firmware.c lines 170-185: skip to the end of the
        ; line. A comment that runs to the end of the file ends the
        ; parse, which is what falling out of the loop does.
        If c = #CYW43_CH_LF
          state = 0
        EndIf
        i = i + 1

    EndSelect
  Wend

  ; A value still open when the input ran out. brcmfmac cannot hit this
  ; because its buffer is NUL-terminated by the firmware loader; a file
  ; read off a FAT32 volume into a plain buffer is not, so the last
  ; entry of a file with no trailing newline would otherwise be lost.
  If state = 2
    For j = start To length - 1
      PokeB(*out + n, PeekA(*text + j))
      n = n + 1
    Next
    PokeB(*out + n, #CYW43_CH_NUL)
    n = n + 1
  EndIf

  If n = 0
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NVRAM)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If boardrev = 0
    ; See the note above: refuse rather than default.
    cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NVRAM)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; roundup(n + 1, 4) - firmware.c line 435. The "+ 1" guarantees at
  ; least one NUL after the last entry even when n is already a multiple
  ; of four, which is what tells the firmware the list has ended.
  padded = ((n + 1) + 3) & (~3)
  For j = n To padded - 1
    PokeB(*out + j, #CYW43_CH_NUL)
  Next

  words = padded / 4
  token = (((~words) & $FFFF) << 16) | (words & $FFFF)
  PokeL(*out + padded, token)

  ProcedureReturn padded + 4
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43UploadNvram - step 6.
;
;  The destination is sdio.c line 3373 and it is measured DOWN from the
;  top of RAM, not up from the base:
;      address = bus->ci->ramsize - varsz + bus->ci->rambase;
;  with varsz being the cooked length INCLUDING the token, so the token
;  itself lands at exactly rambase + ramsize - 4.
;
;  *work is the cooking buffer and must hold length + 8 bytes, 4-aligned.
;  *scratch is the verify buffer, as for the image. They are separate
;  because the cooked NVRAM has to survive until the verify has compared
;  against it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43UploadNvram(*text, length.i, *work, workMax.i, *scratch, scratchLen.i)
  Define cooked.i
  Define dest.i
  Define bad.i
  Define rb.i
  Define token.i
  Define ok.l

  cyw43_nvLoaded = 0
  cyw43_nvBytes = 0
  Cyw43ClearVerifyRecord()

  If cyw43_attached = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NOT_ATTACHED)
  EndIf
  If cyw43_rambase = 0 Or cyw43_ramsize = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_RAMINFO)
  EndIf

  cooked = cyw43_NvramCook(*text, length, *work, workMax)
  If cooked = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If cooked > cyw43_ramsize
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf
  cyw43_nvBytes = cooked

  dest = cyw43_rambase + cyw43_ramsize - cooked

  If Cyw43WriteMem(dest, *work, cooked) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  bad = Cyw43VerifyMem(dest, *work, cooked, *scratch, scratchLen)
  If bad < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If bad <> 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_VERIFY)
  EndIf

  ; And read the token back on its own, as a single 32-bit register at
  ; the top of RAM. The bulk verify above has already compared it, so
  ; this is redundant - and it is kept, because the token is the one
  ; word in the whole upload whose corruption produces no symptom at all
  ; except a radio with no calibration, and a separate read through a
  ; separate code path is worth four bytes of bus traffic.
  token = PeekN(*work + cooked - 4)
  rb = cyw43_Read32Raw(cyw43_rambase + cyw43_ramsize - 4, @ok)
  If ok = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If rb <> token
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_VERIFY)
  EndIf

  cyw43_nvLoaded = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; The cooked NVRAM length, token included. Zero until an upload has
; succeeded. Worth printing on a bench run: on this board's
; brcmfmac43455-sdio.txt it should be 1748.
Procedure.i Cyw43NvramBytes()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_nvBytes
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43Start - step 7. THE FIRMWARE STARTS RUNNING ON THIS LINE.
;
;  brcmf_chip_cr4_set_active(), chip.c lines 1327-1336, which is two
;  things:
;
;      chip->ops->activate(chip->ctx, &chip->pub, rstvec);
;      brcmf_chip_resetcore(core, ARMCR4_BCMA_IOCTL_CPUHALT, 0, 0);
;
;  and activate for SDIO is brcmf_sdio_buscore_activate(), sdio.c lines
;  3888-3903:
;
;      reg_addr = core->base + SD_REG(intstatus);
;      brcmf_sdiod_writel(sdiodev, reg_addr, 0xFFFFFFFF, NULL);
;      if (rstvec)
;              brcmf_sdiod_ramrw(sdiodev, true, 0, &rstvec, sizeof(rstvec));
;
;  THE RESET VECTOR GOES TO BACKPLANE ADDRESS ZERO, not to the RAM base
;  and not to the CR4. Address 0 is where the ARM fetches its first
;  instruction from, and the word written there is the first word of the
;  image - which is already at the RAM base as part of the image, and is
;  now ALSO at 0. That duplication looks like a mistake and is not.
;
;  THE RESETCORE ARGUMENTS INVERT THE ONES IN Cyw43PrepareDownload:
;  prereset CPUHALT, reset 0, postreset 0. The core is configured halted
;  on the way into reset and NOT halted on the way out, which is the
;  single bit that decides whether the radio runs.
;
;  REFUSES WITHOUT BOTH UPLOADS. Releasing the ARM with no image in RAM
;  runs whatever the RAM happened to contain; releasing it with an image
;  but no NVRAM gives a radio with no crystal frequency - the .txt's
;  xtalfreq=37400 line is not decoration, and RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico
;  lines 2088-2090 records the same finding from the other family:
;  "NEITHER DRIVER EVER WRITES A PMU CRYSTAL REGISTER. The crystal comes
;  from the NVRAM line xtalfreq=37400, which is why the NVRAM is not
;  optional."
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43Start()
  Define idx.i
  Define sd.i

  If cyw43_attached = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NOT_ATTACHED)
  EndIf
  If cyw43_fwLoaded = 0 Or cyw43_nvLoaded = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NOT_LOADED)
  EndIf

  ; Clear every interrupt in the SDIO device core before the firmware
  ; can raise one.
  idx = Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_SDIO_DEV)
  If idx < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE)
  EndIf
  sd = cyw43_coreBase[idx]
  If Cyw43Write32(sd + #CYW43_SD_INTSTATUS_OFF, $FFFFFFFF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  If cyw43_rstvec <> 0
    PokeL(@cyw43_word[0], cyw43_rstvec)
    If Cyw43WriteMem(0, @cyw43_word[0], 4) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf

  idx = Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_ARM_CR4)
  If idx < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE)
  EndIf
  If cyw43_CoreReset(idx, #CYW43_ARMCR4_CPUHALT, 0, 0) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; And confirm, which brcmfmac does not bother to do here because its
  ; resetcore has already spun on the reset bit. Cyw43CoreIsUp checks
  ; the OTHER half - that IOCTL reads clock-on and force-gated-clock-off
  ; - and a core that fails this has been released into a state nothing
  ; here intended.
  If Cyw43CoreIsUp(idx) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CORE_RESET)
  EndIf

  Cyw43InvalidateWindow()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43HtClock - step 8. brcmf_sdio_clkctl(bus, CLK_AVAIL), which is
;  brcmf_sdio_htclk(), sdio.c lines 798-875.
;
;      clkreq = SBSDIO_HT_AVAIL_REQ;
;      writeb(SBSDIO_FUNC1_CHIPCLKCSR, clkreq);
;      while (!SBSDIO_CLKAV(clkctl, alp_only)) { read; sleep 5-10 ms; }
;
;  and SBSDIO_CLKAV with alp_only false is SBSDIO_HTAV, sdio.h line 195:
;      ((regval & SBSDIO_AVBITS) == SBSDIO_AVBITS)
;  BOTH availability bits, not just HT. That is not pedantry - a chip
;  that reports HT without ALP has answered something this file does not
;  understand, and accepting it would be accepting a value nobody has
;  reasoned about.
;
;  brcmf_sdio_bus_init then forces the clock rather than merely
;  requesting it, sdio.c lines 4247-4254: read CHIPCLKCSR, OR in
;  SBSDIO_FORCE_HT, write it back, with the comment "Force clocks on
;  backplane to be sure F2 interrupt propagates". That is done here too
;  and it is the reason this procedure is called before F2 and not after.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43HtClock()
  Define v.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i

  If cyw43_attached = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NOT_ATTACHED)
  EndIf

  If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR, #CYW43_CLK_HT_AVAIL_REQ) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE)
  EndIf

  budget = (cyw43_hz / 1000000) * #CYW43_HT_WAIT_US
  If budget < 1
    budget = 1
  EndIf
  t0 = cyw43_Ticks()
  Repeat
    v = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR)
    If v < 0
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE)
    EndIf
    cyw43_clkcsr = v
    If (v & #CYW43_CLK_AVBITS) = #CYW43_CLK_AVBITS
      Break
    EndIf
    If (cyw43_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_HT_TMO)
    EndIf
  ForEver

  ; Now FORCE it, so the F2 interrupt cannot be lost to a clock that
  ; gates itself off between the firmware raising it and the host
  ; noticing. sdio.c lines 4247-4254.
  v = Cyw43ReadByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR)
  If v < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE)
  EndIf
  If Cyw43WriteByte(#CYW43_FN_BACKPLANE, #CYW43_CHIPCLKCSR, v | #CYW43_CLK_FORCE_HT) = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE)
  EndIf
  cyw43_clkcsr = v | #CYW43_CLK_FORCE_HT
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43AnnounceHost - step 9's first half, and it is one register.
;
;  sdio.c lines 4260-4262, immediately before function 2 is enabled:
;      brcmf_sdiod_writel(sdiod, core->base + SD_REG(tosbmailboxdata),
;                         SDPCM_PROT_VERSION << SMB_DATA_VERSION_SHIFT,
;                         NULL);
;  The host tells the firmware which version of the SDPCM framing it
;  speaks, in the top half of a mailbox word, BEFORE F2 comes up. The
;  firmware reads it as part of coming ready.
;
;  The host interrupt mask is written too - sdio.c lines 4272-4275 -
;  even though nothing here fields an interrupt. It is what the firmware
;  is told the host wants, not what the host does with it, and the
;  firmware's start-up path reads it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43AnnounceHost()
  Define idx.i
  Define sd.i

  idx = Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_SDIO_DEV)
  If idx < 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE)
  EndIf
  sd = cyw43_coreBase[idx]

  If Cyw43Write32(sd + #CYW43_SD_TOSBMBDATA, #CYW43_SDPCM_PROT_VERSION << #CYW43_SMB_DATA_VERSION_S) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If Cyw43Write32(sd + #CYW43_SD_HOSTINTMASK, #CYW43_HOSTINTMASK) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure



; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  THE SDPCM TRANSPORT
;
;  Everything above this point talks to the CHIP. This section talks to
;  the FIRMWARE running on it, and the two have nothing in common: the
;  chip answers register reads, the firmware answers framed messages
;  with sequence numbers, credits and correlation ids.
;
;  THE SEAM IS FUNCTION 2. Below it, bus protocol - CMD52, CMD53, the
;  backplane window - which is this file and sdio.pi4 and is specific to
;  the Pi 4. Above it, chip protocol, which is identical on the Pico W's
;  43439 and is already bench-proven twice over in
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico and RP2350/Lib/cyw43.pico2 through WPA2, DHCP
;  and TLS 1.3. This section is the second of those ported downward.
;
;  WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM THE PICO, AND IT IS NOT THE FRAMING.
;  The Pico drives the radio over gSPI. Three things change:
;
;    1. THERE IS NO STATUS REGISTER SAYING A FRAME IS WAITING. On gSPI
;       the host reads F0 $0008, finds PKT_AVAIL and takes the frame
;       length straight out of bits 20:9. SDIO has no such register.
;       The frame's length lives in the frame's own header, so the host
;       reads 64 bytes first (#CYW43_FIRSTREAD) and finds out afterwards
;       - brcmf_sdio_readframes, sdio.c lines 1885-1893.
;
;    2. AN EMPTY FIFO ANSWERS WITH ZEROS RATHER THAN A CLEAR FLAG.
;       sdio.c lines 1382-1385: "All zero means no more to read". So the
;       poll IS the read, and a frame of length 0 is the normal answer
;       most of the time rather than an error.
;
;    3. F2 IS ADDRESSED THROUGH THE BACKPLANE WINDOW. Both directions go
;       to the CHIPCOMMON core's base with the 2/4-byte access flag -
;       brcmf_sdiod_recv_pkt and brcmf_sdiod_send_buf, bcmsdh.c lines
;       539-557 and 566-590, both of which do
;           set_backplane_window(cc_core->base);
;           addr &= SBSDIO_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK;
;           addr |= SBSDIO_SB_ACCESS_2_4B_FLAG;
;       which on this part is window $18000000 and SDIO address $8000.
;       That is not obvious and there is no way to guess it.
;
;  WHAT IS THE SAME: every byte of the framing. Both drivers build the
;  identical twelve-byte SDPCM header and sixteen-byte BCDC header,
;  because both are talking to the same firmware.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Transport state.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global Dim cyw43_tx.l[#CYW43_TXBUF / 4]
Global Dim cyw43_rx.l[#CYW43_RXBUF / 4]

; The iovar staging buffer. Declared .l for the same reason the others
; are: a Dim array lands 8-byte aligned on this backend, which is more
; than CMD53's 4-byte contract asks for.
Global Dim cyw43_arg.l[#CYW43_ARGBUF / 4]

; The CLM chunk staging buffer, and it is DELIBERATELY NOT cyw43_arg AND
; DELIBERATELY NOT cyw43_rx.
;
; The twin assembles its CLM payload in cyw43_rx (RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico
; line 3644) on the argument that the receive buffer is idle until a
; reply arrives. That argument is TRUE and this file does not take it,
; for two reasons. First, it is true only for as long as nothing polls
; the bus between building the payload and sending it, and Cyw43Ioctl
; does exactly that when it drains for a credit - cyw43_Poll writes into
; cyw43_rx, so a stalled clmload would send a chunk with an SDPCM frame
; laid over the middle of it and no error anywhere. The twin gets away
; with it because its credit shortcut is the same and it has never
; stalled; "has never happened" is not the same as "cannot". Second, the
; blob here is 2676 bytes and needs three chunks, so this buffer is read
; three times and a subtle aliasing bug would show as two good chunks
; and one wrong one - the hardest possible shape to diagnose.
;
; 1056 bytes of BSS to make the question not arise.
Global Dim cyw43_clm.l[#CYW43_CLMBUF / 4]

; What the CLM path did. -1 in either means the step never ran, which is
; a different answer from 0 and prints as different words: see the
; probe_Count convention in pi4WifiScan.
Global cyw43_clmStatus.i = -1     ; the clmload_status word, raw
Global cyw43_clmChunks.i = 0      ; how many chunks the firmware accepted

; What "scan_ver" answered. See Cyw43ScanVerProbe for what the two
; fields mean and why the STATUS is the interesting one.
Global cyw43_scanVer.i     = -1   ; the u32 the firmware returned
Global cyw43_scanVerRc.i   = 0    ; the transport's verdict on the GET
Global cyw43_scanVerStat.i = 0    ; the firmware's own status word

; What "sup_wpa" answered - THE QUESTION THAT DECIDES WHO RUNS THE
; SUPPLICANT, and therefore the shape of every line above the
; transport. Same three fields as scan_ver and for the same reason: the
; STATUS is the answer, the value is a curiosity. Cyw43SupWpaProbe.
Global cyw43_supWpa.i      = -1   ; the u32 the firmware returned
Global cyw43_supWpaRc.i    = 0    ; the transport's verdict on the GET
Global cyw43_supWpaStat.i  = 0    ; the firmware's own status word

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE EVENT RING AND THE JOIN STATE MACHINE.
;
;  Four parallel arrays rather than one array of records, for the same
;  reason settings.pi4 gives for its two: a record would need a
;  Structure and the arrays are addressed by one index anyway.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global Dim cyw43_evrType.i[#CYW43_EVRING]
Global Dim cyw43_evrStatus.i[#CYW43_EVRING]
Global Dim cyw43_evrReason.i[#CYW43_EVRING]
Global Dim cyw43_evrFlags.i[#CYW43_EVRING]
Global cyw43_evrHead.i    = 0     ; where the next push goes
Global cyw43_evrTail.i    = 0     ; where the next pop comes from
Global cyw43_evrCount.i   = 0     ; how many are waiting
Global cyw43_evrDropped.i = 0     ; pushes that found the ring full
Global cyw43_evrTotal.i   = 0     ; every event ever pushed, for the log

; The four fields of the event most recently POPPED. Separate from the
; cyw43_ev* set, which holds the most recently DECODED - the two differ
; whenever the ring is more than one deep, and a caller printing "the
; event I just acted on" must read these.
Global cyw43_jevType.i   = 0
Global cyw43_jevStatus.i = 0
Global cyw43_jevReason.i = 0
Global cyw43_jevFlags.i  = 0
Global cyw43_jevNew.i    = 0      ; 1 when this poll drained one

; The most recently decoded event's flags. Read at decode time because
; the island is only addressable while the frame is still in cyw43_rx.
Global cyw43_evFlags.i  = 0
Global cyw43_evReason.i = 0

Global cyw43_joinState.i   = 0
Global cyw43_joinFail.i    = 0
Global cyw43_joinPhase.i   = 0
Global cyw43_joinDone.i    = 0
Global cyw43_joinSecured.i = 0
Global cyw43_joinSsidOk.i  = 0    ; SET_SSID status 0 arrived. LATCHED
                                  ; SEPARATELY and not folded into the
                                  ; accumulator - see cyw43_JoinClassify
Global cyw43_linkLost.i    = 0
Global cyw43_joinT0.i      = 0    ; ticks at the SET_SSID reply
Global cyw43_joinTlink.i   = 0    ; ticks at the first LINK-up
Global cyw43_icvCount.i    = 0
Global cyw43_mfpRc.i       = 0    ; `mfp`'s verdict, TOLERATED
Global cyw43_failReg.i     = -1   ; which security item refused

; The link, as the firmware reports it back. SIX BYTES AND A LENGTH,
; not a string - a BSSID is not text.
Global Dim cyw43_linkBssid.a[6]
Global cyw43_linkRssi.i    = 0
Global cyw43_linkChannel.i = 0
Global Dim cyw43_linkSsid.a[33]
Global cyw43_linkSsidLen.i = 0

; Where the last received 802.3 frame sits inside cyw43_rx, and how long
; it is. ZERO LENGTH MEANS NOTHING IS THERE - the pointer is only valid
; until the next F2 read, which is the one rule a caller of
; Cyw43Receive has to keep.
Global cyw43_dataP.i   = 0
Global cyw43_dataLen.i = 0
Global cyw43_txFrames.i = 0       ; data frames handed to F2
Global cyw43_rxData.i   = 0       ; data frames handed to a caller

; The SDPCM sequence number the host puts on its next frame, and the
; highest sequence the firmware has said it will accept. A send is
; supposed to stall while they are equal.
;
; CREDIT STARTS AT 1 AND SEQUENCE AT 0, AND THAT PAIR IS LOAD-BEARING.
; It is what RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico line 3452 records from the pico-sdk
; driver, whose comment is "we get an immediate stall if this isn't
; done". A credit of 0 would stall the very first frame for ever.
Global cyw43_seq.i    = 0
Global cyw43_credit.i = 1
Global cyw43_stalls.i = 0

; The BCDC correlation id. Never 0 - see the BCDC notes above.
Global cyw43_reqid.i = 0

; Where the last control reply's payload starts and how long it is, plus
; the firmware's own status word from that reply.
Global cyw43_replyOff.i = 0
Global cyw43_replyLen.i = 0
Global cyw43_ioctlStatus.i = 0

; Frame accounting. Every one of these is a COUNT OF SOMETHING THAT WENT
; WRONG, kept separately, because "the scan found nothing" and "the scan
; found nine networks and threw them all away for a bad header" are
; different bugs that produce the same empty list.
Global cyw43_rxFrames.i  = 0   ; frames read whole
Global cyw43_rxBadHdr.i  = 0   ; length/complement disagreed
Global cyw43_rxTooBig.i  = 0   ; claimed more than #CYW43_MAX_FRAME
Global cyw43_rxShort.i   = 0   ; too short to hold what it claimed
Global cyw43_rxOther.i   = 0   ; a channel this file does not serve
Global cyw43_evOther.i   = 0   ; an event that was not ESCAN_RESULT
Global cyw43_evBadIsland.i = 0 ; event too short for its own 72-byte prefix
Global cyw43_evEmpty.i = 0     ; event frame with NO payload at all

; WHERE THE ETHERNET FRAME STARTS inside cyw43_rx, for the event that
; was just decoded. It is NOT a constant: it is
;     header_length + 4 + (BDC data_offset << 2)
; and the BDC data_offset is a field the firmware sets, observed as 1 on
; event frames. Computed by cyw43_RxFrame and read by cyw43_ParseBss,
; which is a seam worth naming - passing 0 here instead of the computed
; value parses the SDPCM header as if it were a bss_info and produces a
; network with a garbage name rather than an error.
Global cyw43_evP.i       = 0

; The last event decoded, whether or not it was one we wanted.
Global cyw43_evType.i    = -1
Global cyw43_evStatus.i  = -1
Global cyw43_evDatalen.i = 0

; The scan table.
Global cyw43_bssCount.i = 0
Global cyw43_bssOverflow.i = 0
; HOW MANY RECORDS FOLDED INTO A ROW THAT ALREADY EXISTED. An escan
; reports one PARTIAL event per beacon or probe response heard, so the
; same access point arrives several times; cyw43_ParseBss merges them by
; BSSID and this counts the merges. It is EXPECTED TO BE NON-ZERO on any
; real scan - the first bench run that showed this behaviour delivered
; five records for two networks, which is three folds - and it is kept
; rather than discarded because "five beacons from one AP and one from
; another" and "three and three" are different rooms. Zero with several
; networks in the list and a long scan is the reading to be suspicious
; of: it means either the fold never fires or the environment is
; unusually quiet. See cyw43_ParseBss.
Global cyw43_bssDup.i = 0
Global Dim cyw43_bssSsid.b[#CYW43_MAX_BSS * 33]
Global Dim cyw43_bssSsidLen.i[#CYW43_MAX_BSS]
Global Dim cyw43_bssChan.i[#CYW43_MAX_BSS]
; The chanspec's own channel byte, kept BESIDE the reported channel and
; not instead of it. On a wide AP the two differ - 52 and 58 for an
; 80 MHz block - and a bench log that shows only one of them cannot tell
; a wide channel from a wrong offset. See cyw43_ParseBss.
Global Dim cyw43_bssCspec.i[#CYW43_MAX_BSS]
Global Dim cyw43_bssRssi.i[#CYW43_MAX_BSS]
Global Dim cyw43_bssPriv.i[#CYW43_MAX_BSS]
Global Dim cyw43_bssBssid.b[#CYW43_MAX_BSS * 6]

; Cross-checks that cost nothing and catch a wrong layout. If the
; chanspec's low byte and ctl_ch ever disagree, one of the two offsets
; is wrong and the channel column is fiction.
Global cyw43_chMismatch.i = 0
Global cyw43_syncBad.i = 0
Global cyw43_bssVersion.i = 0
Global cyw43_bssLength.i = 0

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Little-endian and big-endian readers over the RX buffer.
;
;  TWO SETS OF NAMES, ON PURPOSE. See THE BIG-ENDIAN ISLAND above. The
;  cost of getting this wrong is not a crash: event_type 69 read the
;  wrong way round is $45000000, which looks like a plausible unknown
;  event rather than like a mistake. Separate names mean the choice is
;  made at every call site instead of remembered once.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_Rx8(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekA(@cyw43_rx[0] + off)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cyw43_RxLE16(off.i)
  Define p.i
  p = @cyw43_rx[0] + off
  ProcedureReturn PeekA(p) | (PeekA(p + 1) << 8)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cyw43_RxLE32(off.i)
  Define p.i
  p = @cyw43_rx[0] + off
  ProcedureReturn PeekA(p) | (PeekA(p + 1) << 8) | (PeekA(p + 2) << 16) | (PeekA(p + 3) << 24)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i cyw43_RxBE32(off.i)
  Define p.i
  Define v.i
  p = @cyw43_rx[0] + off
  v = PeekA(p)
  v = (v << 8) | PeekA(p + 1)
  v = (v << 8) | PeekA(p + 2)
  v = (v << 8) | PeekA(p + 3)
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  A SIXTEEN-BIT BIG-ENDIAN READ, and it exists for exactly one field.
;
;  The event message's `flags` is a u16 at P+26 while every other field
;  in the island is a u32, so there was no BE16 reader here until the
;  join needed one. Reading flags with cyw43_RxBE32 instead would take
;  the version field beside it into the top half of the word and push
;  the up/down bit sixteen places out of the `& 1` test - which does not
;  fail, it just reports every link as down for ever.
;
;  A SEPARATE NAMED READER RATHER THAN A SHIFT AT THE CALL SITE, which
;  is this file's rule for the whole island: the coastline is exact and
;  every crossing of it is a named procedure, because the one time it
;  was a remembered rule the answer came back as $45000000.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_RxBE16(off.i)
  Define p.i
  p = @cyw43_rx[0] + off
  ProcedureReturn (PeekA(p) << 8) | PeekA(p + 1)
EndProcedure

; A SIGNED sixteen-bit little-endian read, for RSSI. It arrives as
; $FFD3 for -45 dBm and a scan list that prints 65491 dBm has not failed
; loudly enough for anybody to notice it failed.
Procedure.i cyw43_RxI16(off.i)
  Define v.i
  v = cyw43_RxLE16(off)
  If v >= $8000
    v = v - $10000
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_PutLE32 - a 32-bit little-endian store that does not care where
;  it lands.
;
;  THIS EXISTS BECAUSE PokeL WEDGED THE BOARD DEAD, AND THE REASON IS
;  WORTH THE PARAGRAPH BECAUSE THE OBVIOUS REASONING IS WRONG.
;
;  An iovar payload is a NUL-terminated NAME followed by a struct, so
;  every field in the struct sits at (name length + 1 + offset). For
;  "bsscfg:event_msgs" that is 18, and for "escan" it is 6 - NEITHER IS
;  A MULTIPLE OF FOUR. `PokeL` lowers to a single `str w`, so
;      PokeL(@cyw43_arg[0] + 18, 0)
;  is a word store to an address ending in 2.
;
;  THE TRAP: SCTLR_EL2.A - strict alignment checking - is CLEAR on this
;  board. armstub8.S writes $30C50830 and every set bit in it is RES1
;  (Datasheets/pi4/armstub8.S lines 136-141), which was confirmed by
;  reading the register on silicon. So the natural conclusion is that
;  unaligned access is permitted, and that conclusion is FALSE, because
;  SCTLR_EL2.M is clear too. With the MMU off, ARMv8-A makes every data
;  access Device-nGnRnE, and an unaligned access to Device memory is an
;  Alignment fault whatever SCTLR.A says.
;
;  WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE ON THE BENCH: nothing. No vector is installed, so
;  the fault is a runaway rather than a message. On 2026-08-27 the scan
;  payload printed "-- transport --" and then never printed again; the
;  dead line was this store, the first unaligned word access anywhere in
;  the whole program, three instructions into Cyw43SetEventMask.
;
;  THE GATE AGREED WITH THE BUG. tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py modelled
;  memory as a Python dict of bytes, which does not care about
;  alignment, so the scan gate passed a payload that could not survive
;  ten milliseconds on the part. That gate now refuses an unaligned
;  access exactly where the silicon would fault.
;
;  REJECTED: turning the MMU on so that DRAM is Normal memory. It would
;  work - that is stage 1 of the MMU plan and DRAM is mapped Normal-NC
;  there - but it makes a two-line protocol-packing bug depend on the
;  whole address-translation apparatus, and this file must keep working
;  before that lands. REJECTED ALSO: padding the name to a multiple of
;  four. The name length is the wire format; the firmware parses up to
;  the NUL and expects the struct immediately after it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure cyw43_PutLE32(p.i, v.i)
  PokeB(p,     v & $FF)
  PokeB(p + 1, (v >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeB(p + 2, (v >> 16) & $FF)
  PokeB(p + 3, (v >> 24) & $FF)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE TRACE SEAM - how a hang in here names itself.
;
;  A HANG IS ONLY EVER DIAGNOSED BY WHAT WAS PRINTED BEFORE IT. The
;  alignment fault above was found by reading source, and that worked
;  only because the fault happened to be the first unaligned access in
;  the whole program. The next one will not be so obliging, and the
;  transport is the part of this file with the most places to stop:
;  three ioctls, a poll loop, and two chunking loops under that.
;
;  THIS FILE CANNOT PRINT. A library never includes a library, so
;  cyw43.pi4 may not reach uart.pi4 - the same rule that produced
;  Cyw43SetIo. So the instrument is a SEAM, exactly like the I/O one:
;  the caller installs a procedure, and this file calls it with a small
;  integer naming the step it is ABOUT TO ATTEMPT. With nothing
;  installed it is one load and one branch per step and prints nothing.
;
;  THE ANNOUNCEMENT MUST COME BEFORE THE DANGEROUS CALL, AND THE
;  HANDLER MUST DRAIN. That second half is the caller's job and it is
;  not optional: without a UartDrain() inside the handler the marker
;  sits in the PL011 transmit FIFO while the call blocks, the CPU never
;  reaches the code that would push it out, and the one line that was
;  supposed to survive the hang dies with everything else. This was
;  learned twice in one day on 2026-08-26 and again on 2026-08-27.
;  See Examples/Diagnostics/pi4WifiScan.pi4 for a handler that does it.
;
;  TWO LEVELS, AND THE REASON IS THE SCAN. The coarse points are a
;  dozen lines for a whole bring-up and cost nothing. The FINE points
;  sit inside cyw43_Poll and cyw43_F2Xfer, which run thousands of times
;  per second; printing and draining at every one does not merely make
;  a slow log, it changes the timing enough that the F2 FIFO overruns
;  and the scan loses results. So fine tracing is off unless asked for,
;  and it is for hunting inside one call that is already known to hang.
;
;  THE CODES ARE NUMBERS, NOT STRINGS, so this file carries no text for
;  the caller to have to print and no character literals.
; ======================================================================
#CYW43_TR_WIFIUP       = 1    ; Cyw43WifiUp entered
#CYW43_TR_RESET_DONE   = 2    ; transport bookkeeping reset
#CYW43_TR_EVMASK_SEND  = 3    ; about to send bsscfg:event_msgs
#CYW43_TR_EVMASK_DONE  = 4    ; it came back
#CYW43_TR_UP_SEND      = 5    ; about to send WLC_UP
#CYW43_TR_UP_DONE      = 6    ; it came back
#CYW43_TR_SCAN_SEND    = 7    ; about to send the escan request
#CYW43_TR_SCAN_DONE    = 8    ; it came back
#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_STALL  = 9    ; no credit; draining to earn one
#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_TX     = 10   ; about to push the request frame at F2
#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_WAIT   = 11   ; sent; about to wait for the reply
#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_REPLY  = 12   ; the wait returned
; The CLM steps. 13 announces EVERY chunk, not just the first, because
; the whole point of a chunked upload is that it can die on the second
; one - and on this board the blob is three chunks, so a stop after two
; "13"s and no "14" names the failing chunk by counting.
#CYW43_TR_CLM_CHUNK    = 13   ; about to send one clmload chunk
#CYW43_TR_CLM_DONE     = 14   ; every chunk was accepted
#CYW43_TR_CLM_STATUS   = 15   ; about to ask clmload_status
#CYW43_TR_CLM_VERDICT  = 16   ; clmload_status answered

; FINE - inside the loops. See the two-levels note above.
#CYW43_TR_POLL_ENTER   = 20   ; cyw43_Poll starting a wait
#CYW43_TR_POLL_TIMEOUT = 21   ; cyw43_Poll gave up on its budget
#CYW43_TR_F2_FIRST     = 22   ; about to read the fixed first 64 bytes
#CYW43_TR_F2_REST      = 23   ; about to read the rest of a frame
#CYW43_TR_F2_BLOCK     = 24   ; about to move one block-mode chunk
#CYW43_TR_F2_BYTE      = 25   ; about to move one byte-mode chunk

; The handler, or 0. DECLARED WITH '*' for the same reason the four I/O
; primitives are.
Global *cyw43_traceFn = 0
Global cyw43_trFine.i = 0

; The last step announced, kept whether or not a handler is installed.
; A payload that survives the hang - because a watchdog reset it, say -
; can still be asked what the last thing attempted was, and one integer
; costs nothing. It is NOT a substitute for the printed line: a board
; that is wedged cannot answer this either.
Global cyw43_trStep.i = 0

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetTrace - install the instrument, or remove it with 0.
;
;  fine <> 0 turns on the points inside the poll and chunking loops.
;  Leave it 0 unless a call is already known to hang, and read the
;  two-levels note above before turning it on during a scan.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Cyw43SetTrace(*fn, fine.i)
  *cyw43_traceFn = *fn
  cyw43_trFine = fine
EndProcedure

; The last step announced. See cyw43_trStep.
Procedure.i Cyw43TraceStep()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_trStep
EndProcedure

Procedure cyw43_Trace(step.i)
  cyw43_trStep = step
  If *cyw43_traceFn = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  cyw43_traceFn(step)
EndProcedure

Procedure cyw43_TraceIo(step.i)
  If cyw43_trFine = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  cyw43_Trace(step)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_F2Xfer - move a whole frame to or from function 2.
;
;  The address is the ChipCommon base seen through the backplane window,
;  with the 2/4-byte flag - see point 3 in this section's header. The
;  window is programmed on every call rather than cached, because F1
;  traffic between two frames may have moved it and a frame read through
;  the wrong window returns the wrong memory silently.
;
;  CHUNKING USES F2'S OWN BLOCK SIZE, NOT F1'S. F2's is 512 and F1's is
;  64; the byte-mode ceiling follows the function, and mixing them is
;  exactly the fault that cost a bench run on 2026-08-26.
;
;  n is rounded UP to a whole number of words by the caller. The command
;  carries the rounded length because the bus cannot move a partial
;  word; the SDPCM header inside carries the true length, and that is
;  the one the firmware checks.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_F2Xfer(write.i, *buf, n.i)
  Define addr.i
  Define left.i
  Define blocks.i
  Define whole.i
  Define rest.i
  Define q.i

  If Cyw43IoReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_NO_IO)
  EndIf
  If cyw43_f2ByteMax < 4
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE)
  EndIf
  If n <= 0 Or (n & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf
  If n > #CYW43_MAX_FRAME
    ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf

  If cyw43_SetWindow(#CYW43_ENUM_BASE) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  addr = (#CYW43_ENUM_BASE & #CYW43_SB_OFT_ADDR_MASK) | #CYW43_SB_ACCESS_2_4B

  q = *buf
  left = n

  If cyw43_useBlocks <> 0
    While left >= cyw43_f2Blk
      blocks = left / cyw43_f2Blk
      If blocks > #CYW43_BLOCKS_PER_CMD
        blocks = #CYW43_BLOCKS_PER_CMD
      EndIf
      whole = blocks * cyw43_f2Blk
      cyw43_TraceIo(#CYW43_TR_F2_BLOCK)
      If write <> 0
        If cyw43_wrb(#CYW43_FN_WLAN, addr, q, cyw43_f2Blk, blocks, 1) <> 1
          ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_XFER)
        EndIf
      Else
        If cyw43_rdb(#CYW43_FN_WLAN, addr, q, cyw43_f2Blk, blocks, 1) <> 1
          ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_XFER)
        EndIf
      EndIf
      q = q + whole
      addr = addr + whole
      left = left - whole
    Wend
  EndIf

  While left > 0
    rest = left
    If rest > cyw43_f2ByteMax
      rest = cyw43_f2ByteMax
    EndIf
    cyw43_TraceIo(#CYW43_TR_F2_BYTE)
    If write <> 0
      If cyw43_wr(#CYW43_FN_WLAN, addr, q, rest, 1) <> 1
        ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_XFER)
      EndIf
    Else
      If cyw43_rd(#CYW43_FN_WLAN, addr, q, rest, 1) <> 1
        ProcedureReturn cyw43_Fail(#CYW43_ERR_XFER)
      EndIf
    EndIf
    q = q + rest
    addr = addr + rest
    left = left - rest
  Wend

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE EVENT RING.
;
;  Three procedures over the four arrays declared with the join state.
;  They sit HERE, above cyw43_RxFrame, because that is where the push
;  happens and this language wants a procedure defined before it is
;  called - there is no forward Declare anywhere in this file and adding
;  one for three ten-line procedures would be a second place to keep a
;  signature correct.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_EvrPush - one decoded event into the ring.
;
;  OVERFLOW DROPS THE NEW EVENT, NOT THE OLDEST, and that is a choice
;  with a reason. The alternative - overwrite the tail - keeps the ring
;  full of the most RECENT events, which sounds better and is wrong for
;  a join: the events that decide a join are AUTH and SET_SSID, they
;  arrive FIRST, and a burst of late DEAUTH churn would evict exactly
;  the two records the verdict depends on. Dropping the newest loses
;  information the caller has already fallen behind on; dropping the
;  oldest loses the answer.
;
;  EITHER WAY IT IS COUNTED. cyw43_evrDropped non-zero means the caller
;  is not draining fast enough and every verdict from that run is
;  suspect - which is a thing an instrument must be able to say, and
;  cannot if the loss is silent.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure cyw43_EvrPush(t.i, s.i, rr.i, f.i)
  cyw43_evrTotal = cyw43_evrTotal + 1
  If cyw43_evrCount >= #CYW43_EVRING
    cyw43_evrDropped = cyw43_evrDropped + 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  cyw43_evrType[cyw43_evrHead]   = t
  cyw43_evrStatus[cyw43_evrHead] = s
  cyw43_evrReason[cyw43_evrHead] = rr
  cyw43_evrFlags[cyw43_evrHead]  = f
  cyw43_evrHead = (cyw43_evrHead + 1) % #CYW43_EVRING
  cyw43_evrCount = cyw43_evrCount + 1
EndProcedure

; Pop one into the cyw43_jev* set. Returns 1 if there was one, 0 if the
; ring was empty. The FIELDS ARE SEPARATE FROM cyw43_ev* on purpose: a
; caller printing "the event I just acted on" must not read the fields
; of whatever frame happened to arrive during the print.
Procedure.i cyw43_EvrPop()
  If cyw43_evrCount <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  cyw43_jevType   = cyw43_evrType[cyw43_evrTail]
  cyw43_jevStatus = cyw43_evrStatus[cyw43_evrTail]
  cyw43_jevReason = cyw43_evrReason[cyw43_evrTail]
  cyw43_jevFlags  = cyw43_evrFlags[cyw43_evrTail]
  cyw43_evrTail = (cyw43_evrTail + 1) % #CYW43_EVRING
  cyw43_evrCount = cyw43_evrCount - 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; Empty it. Called at the start of every join attempt, because events
; from a PREVIOUS attempt are not evidence about this one - a stale
; DEAUTH sitting in the ring would fail attempt 2 before attempt 2 had
; transmitted anything, which is a measured failure on the Pico bench
; (cyw43.pico:5488-5495) and not a hypothetical.
;
; THE DROP AND TOTAL COUNTS SURVIVE A CLEAR. They are the run's
; evidence, not the attempt's, and zeroing them here would mean a
; retried join could never report that it had lost events.
Procedure cyw43_EvrClear()
  cyw43_evrHead = 0
  cyw43_evrTail = 0
  cyw43_evrCount = 0
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43TransportReset - the bookkeeping, once, before the first ioctl.
;
;  Sequence 0 and credit 1. See the declarations for why the 1 matters.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Cyw43TransportReset()
  cyw43_seq = 0
  cyw43_credit = 1
  cyw43_reqid = 0
  cyw43_stalls = 0
  cyw43_replyOff = 0
  cyw43_replyLen = 0
  cyw43_ioctlStatus = 0
  cyw43_rxFrames = 0
  cyw43_rxBadHdr = 0
  cyw43_rxTooBig = 0
  cyw43_rxShort = 0
  cyw43_rxOther = 0
  cyw43_evOther = 0
  cyw43_evBadIsland = 0
  cyw43_evEmpty = 0
  cyw43_evP = 0
  cyw43_evType = -1
  cyw43_evStatus = -1
  cyw43_evDatalen = 0
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_BssFind - which table row already holds this record's BSSID,
;  or -1.
;
;  b is the start of the bss_info, so the address the six BSSID bytes
;  live at is b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_BSSID (the offset is declared at line
;  1038 of this file and is derived in the gate from the vendor struct,
;  not copied from here).
;
;  LINEAR, AND THAT IS THE RIGHT SHAPE. #CYW43_MAX_BSS is 32 (line
;  1090), so the worst case is 32 six-byte comparisons per record, on a
;  path that has just finished a 64-byte SDIO burst. A hash or a sorted
;  index would be more code, more state to reset, and more to get wrong
;  for a saving nothing here can measure.
;
;  THE COMPARISON IS MASKED because the table is a .b array - signed -
;  and cyw43_Rx8 returns 0..255 out of PeekA. Unmasked, a BSSID byte of
;  $C4 reads back as -60 and never matches itself, which would fold
;  nothing and look exactly like the old behaviour. Cyw43ScanBssidByte
;  masks for the same reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_BssFind(b.i)
  Define r.i
  Define i.i
  Define same.i

  For r = 0 To cyw43_bssCount - 1
    same = 1
    For i = 0 To 5
      If (cyw43_bssBssid[r * 6 + i] & $FF) <> cyw43_Rx8(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_BSSID + i)
        same = 0
        Break
      EndIf
    Next
    If same = 1
      ProcedureReturn r
    EndIf
  Next

  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_ParseBss - one bss_info out of a validated ESCAN_RESULT.
;
;  p is P, the start of the Ethernet frame on the event channel. The
;  escan header sits at P+72 and the bss_info at P+84.
;
;  EVERY LENGTH HERE IS UNTRUSTED. datalen comes from the firmware,
;  SSID_len comes off the air, and both are used as loop bounds. The
;  clamp on SSID_len is not tidiness: one byte of 255 walks 223 bytes
;  past the SSID and prints whatever the RX buffer happens to hold.
;
;  Returns 2 if the record was FOLDED INTO an existing row, 1 if a new
;  network was appended, 0 if the record was too short or the table is
;  full.
;
;  ONE BSSID IS ONE ROW, AND UNTIL 2026-08-27 IT WAS NOT.
;
;  An escan emits one PARTIAL event for every beacon and every probe
;  response the radio hears, and a scan hears the same access point
;  several times. This file used to append each one, so the first real
;  bench run reported FIVE networks for TWO - three rows for the 2.4 GHz
;  AP and two for the 5 GHz one, same BSSID, same name, RSSI differing
;  by a dBm. That is not a firmware fault and it is not a parsing fault;
;  it is the escan contract, and the merge is the host's job.
;
;  WHERE THE VENDOR PUTS THE MERGE, AND WHY IT IS HERE INSTEAD.
;  brcmfmac does not deduplicate in the driver at all:
;  brcmf_inform_single_bss (cfg80211.c:3322) hands every single record
;  to cfg80211_inform_bss_data (cfg80211.c:3371), and cfg80211's own BSS
;  list is what collapses them by BSSID upstream. There is no cfg80211
;  under this file - the table below IS the list a caller sees - so the
;  merge has to happen at the only place that exists. The behaviour is
;  the vendor's; only the address changes.
;
;  WHAT A HIT UPDATES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.
;
;    RSSI  - the BEST of the readings, not the newest. Each beacon is
;            one sample of a noisy quantity; the strongest is the one
;            taken when the antenna pattern and the air agreed, and it
;            is the figure a human uses to decide whether an AP is
;            reachable. Taking the last instead would make the column
;            depend on the order the firmware happened to emit, which
;            is a number that changes between two runs of an unchanged
;            program - the worst kind of measurement.
;    chan  - refreshed from the newer record, along with the chanspec
;            byte beside it. A record with ctl_ch filled in is better
;            evidence than an older one without it, and an AP that has
;            genuinely moved channel mid-scan should report where it is
;            now rather than where it was.
;
;    SSID and privacy are NOT touched, and that is a decision rather
;    than an omission. The tempting case is a hidden AP whose beacon
;    carries SSID_len 0 and whose probe response carries the name, where
;    filling the name in on the second record looks like an improvement.
;    It is rejected here for two reasons. It is not what the reference
;    does - cfg80211 keeps hidden and named BSSes as separate entries
;    and reconciles them in its own hidden-SSID machinery, which is a
;    body of code this file has no equivalent of. And it would make the
;    "<hidden>" row in the scan list depend on which frame arrived
;    first, so a network would appear named on one run and hidden on the
;    next with nothing in the log to say why. If a bench ever shows a
;    hidden row that should have had a name, THAT is the evidence to
;    change this on, and the change belongs beside this paragraph.
;
;  THE COUNT IS KEPT. cyw43_bssDup is how many records folded, and it is
;  real information about the room - three beacons from one AP and one
;  from another is a different environment from two and two. Dropping it
;  silently would leave a control that cannot be shown to have done
;  anything, which is the exact shape the header of this file warns
;  about.
;
;  AND A DUPLICATE DOES NOT CONSUME A TABLE SLOT: the lookup runs BEFORE
;  the overflow test, so thirty-three beacons from one AP fill one row
;  and raise no overflow, and a duplicate arriving when the table is
;  genuinely full still folds instead of being counted as a network that
;  was thrown away. cyw43_bssOverflow keeps meaning "distinct networks
;  this table could not hold".
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_ParseBss(p.i)
  Define b.i
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define cap.i
  Define ctl.i
  Define low.i
  Define chan.i
  Define rssi.i
  Define hit.i
  Define idx.i
  Define syncid.i

  b = p + #CYW43_EV_PKTLEN + #CYW43_ESCAN_HDRLEN

  ; TWO INDEPENDENT BOUNDS AND BOTH ARE WANTED. The firmware's own
  ; datalen must be big enough to hold an escan header plus a bss_info,
  ; AND the buffer must physically contain it. A parser that trusts one
  ; untrusted number is a parser walking off the end of cyw43_rx.
  If cyw43_evDatalen < (#CYW43_ESCAN_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BSSINFO_MIN)
    cyw43_rxShort = cyw43_rxShort + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (b + #CYW43_BSSINFO_MIN) > #CYW43_RXBUF
    cyw43_rxShort = cyw43_rxShort + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; The sync id we asked for, echoed back. COUNTED, NOT ENFORCED: that
  ; the firmware echoes it is a claim from another driver and nobody
  ; here has watched it happen, so discarding every network on the
  ; strength of an unobserved field would be the worse failure. A
  ; non-zero count on the bench beside a sane network list means the
  ; field is not what we think it is - and that is worth knowing.
  syncid = cyw43_RxLE16(p + #CYW43_EV_PKTLEN + 8)
  If syncid <> #CYW43_ESCAN_SYNC_ID
    cyw43_syncBad = cyw43_syncBad + 1
  EndIf

  cyw43_bssVersion = cyw43_RxLE32(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_VERSION)
  cyw43_bssLength  = cyw43_RxLE32(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_LENGTH)

  ; CHANNEL, TWO WAYS, AND THE ANSWER IS ctl_ch. THIS WAS WRONG UNTIL
  ; 2026-08-27 AND THE FIRST REAL SCAN IS WHAT SHOWED IT.
  ;
  ; chanspec's low byte is the vendor's CHSPEC_CHANNEL (u:97) and ctl_ch
  ; is a separate byte 16 bytes further on. This code used to report the
  ; chanspec's byte and treat any disagreement as a layout error. On the
  ; bench the very first scan printed
  ;      ch 58   -45 dBm  enc  example_5G
  ; and raised two "channel mismatch"es - and 58 is not a channel any
  ; access point sits on. It is the CENTRE of an 80 MHz block whose
  ; control channel is 52. The two 5 GHz records in the list were the
  ; two mismatches, exactly.
  ;
  ; brcmf_inform_single_bss settles it, cfg80211.c:3343-3348:
  ;      if (!bi->ctl_ch) { decode the chanspec into ctl_ch }
  ;      channel = bi->ctl_ch;
  ; The control channel IS the channel; the chanspec is only consulted
  ; when ctl_ch is zero. So that is what this does, fallback included.
  ;
  ; THE CROSS-CHECK IS KEPT AND ITS MEANING HAS CHANGED, which is worth
  ; being explicit about because a counter whose meaning drifts silently
  ; is worse than no counter. cyw43_chMismatch is no longer "one of
  ; these two offsets is wrong". It now counts WIDE-CHANNEL records, and
  ; it is expected to be non-zero anywhere a 40 or 80 MHz AP is in
  ; range. It is still an alarm in one specific case: non-zero in an
  ; environment with no 5 GHz networks in the list means the offsets
  ; really are wrong. Read it next to the channel column, not alone.
  low = cyw43_RxLE16(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_CHANSPEC) & #CYW43_CHANSPEC_CHAN_MASK
  ctl = cyw43_Rx8(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_CTLCH)
  If ctl <> 0
    chan = ctl
  Else
    ; ctl_ch zero means the firmware did not fill it in. brcmfmac
    ; decodes the chanspec properly here - centre channel minus the
    ; sideband offset - and this file does not, because it has no
    ; brcmu_d11 decoder and inventing one from the bit layout without
    ; the header on disk would be a guess. The chanspec's own channel
    ; byte is the honest approximation, it is what this file reported
    ; for every record until today, and the counter below says when it
    ; is being leaned on.
    chan = low
  EndIf

  ; COUNTED PER RECORD, NOT PER ROW, and that is on purpose now that
  ; records fold. cyw43_chMismatch is an observation about the frames
  ; that arrived - "three of the beacons I heard described a wide
  ; channel" - and folding them into one row does not un-hear them.
  ; Counting it per row instead would make the number depend on how
  ; many times an AP happened to beacon, which is the reading nobody
  ; wants.
  If ctl <> low
    cyw43_chMismatch = cyw43_chMismatch + 1
  EndIf

  rssi = cyw43_RxI16(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_RSSI)

  ; THE FOLD. Before the overflow test, for the reason in the header.
  hit = cyw43_BssFind(b)
  If hit >= 0
    cyw43_bssDup = cyw43_bssDup + 1
    ; STRICTLY GREATER. Equal readings are the common case - two
    ; beacons a hundred milliseconds apart usually agree - and writing
    ; the same number back would be a store for nothing.
    If rssi > cyw43_bssRssi[hit]
      cyw43_bssRssi[hit] = rssi
    EndIf
    cyw43_bssChan[hit] = chan
    cyw43_bssCspec[hit] = low
    ProcedureReturn 2
  EndIf

  If cyw43_bssCount >= #CYW43_MAX_BSS
    ; REFUSE AND COUNT, do not wrap. A wrapped table shows the last 32
    ; networks and looks exactly like a complete list of 32.
    cyw43_bssOverflow = cyw43_bssOverflow + 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  idx = cyw43_bssCount

  For i = 0 To 5
    cyw43_bssBssid[idx * 6 + i] = cyw43_Rx8(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_BSSID + i)
  Next

  cap = cyw43_RxLE16(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_CAP)
  cyw43_bssPriv[idx] = 0
  If (cap & #CYW43_DOT11_CAP_PRIVACY) <> 0
    cyw43_bssPriv[idx] = 1
  EndIf

  n = cyw43_Rx8(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_SSIDLEN)
  If n > 32
    n = 32
  EndIf
  cyw43_bssSsidLen[idx] = n
  For i = 0 To n - 1
    cyw43_bssSsid[idx * 33 + i] = cyw43_Rx8(b + #CYW43_BSS_OFF_SSID + i)
  Next
  ; The wire copy is NOT terminated. This one is, so a printer can treat
  ; it as a string without carrying the length everywhere.
  cyw43_bssSsid[idx * 33 + n] = 0

  cyw43_bssCspec[idx] = low
  cyw43_bssChan[idx] = chan
  cyw43_bssRssi[idx] = rssi

  cyw43_bssCount = cyw43_bssCount + 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_RxFrame - read at most one frame and dispatch it.
;
;  Returns:
;     0  nothing was there. The FIFO answered with an all-zero header,
;        which sdio.c line 1383 documents as "no more to read". This is
;        the normal answer and is NOT an error.
;     1  a control reply arrived whose BCDC id matched wantId
;     2  a validated ESCAN_RESULT event was decoded
;     3  a frame arrived and was consumed but was none of the above
;    -1  the bus failed
;
;  wantId of -1 matches no reply, which is how a caller drains the FIFO
;  without expecting an answer.
;
;  A BAD FRAME IS COUNTED AND DROPPED, NEVER RE-READ. It has already
;  been taken out of the FIFO by the read below; anything else would
;  punish the next frame for this one's sins.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_RxFrame(wantId.i)
  Define size.i
  Define comp.i
  Define chan.i
  Define hlen.i
  Define rest.i
  Define total.i
  Define flags.i
  Define gotId.i
  Define p.i
  Define doff.i

  ; The first read. Its length is fixed because the frame's own length
  ; is not known yet.
  cyw43_TraceIo(#CYW43_TR_F2_FIRST)
  If cyw43_F2Xfer(0, @cyw43_rx[0], #CYW43_FIRSTREAD) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  size = cyw43_RxLE16(#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_LEN)
  comp = cyw43_RxLE16(#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_COMP)

  ; sdio.c lines 1382-1385, and the order of these two tests is the
  ; whole point: all-zero is EMPTY, not corrupt.
  If size = 0 And comp = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (size ! comp) <> $FFFF
    cyw43_rxBadHdr = cyw43_rxBadHdr + 1
    ProcedureReturn 3
  EndIf
  If size < #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN
    cyw43_rxShort = cyw43_rxShort + 1
    ProcedureReturn 3
  EndIf
  If size > #CYW43_MAX_FRAME
    ; REFUSED, NOT TRUNCATED. A truncated scan result parses into a
    ; plausible network with a wrong name, which is worse than no
    ; network at all.
    cyw43_rxTooBig = cyw43_rxTooBig + 1
    ProcedureReturn 3
  EndIf

  ; The rest of the frame, if there is any. Rounded up to a word for the
  ; bus; the slack lands in the buffer and is ignored.
  If size > #CYW43_FIRSTREAD
    rest = size - #CYW43_FIRSTREAD
    total = (rest + 3) & (~3)
    If (#CYW43_FIRSTREAD + total) > #CYW43_RXBUF
      cyw43_rxTooBig = cyw43_rxTooBig + 1
      ProcedureReturn 3
    EndIf
    cyw43_TraceIo(#CYW43_TR_F2_REST)
    If cyw43_F2Xfer(0, @cyw43_rx[0] + #CYW43_FIRSTREAD, total) = 0
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf
  EndIf

  cyw43_rxFrames = cyw43_rxFrames + 1

  chan = cyw43_Rx8(#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_CHAN) & #CYW43_SDPCM_CHAN_MASK
  hlen = cyw43_Rx8(#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_DOFF)

  ; CREDITS REFRESH FROM EVERY FRAME, whatever channel it came on, which
  ; is why this sits before the channel test and not inside it.
  cyw43_credit = cyw43_Rx8(#CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_WIN)

  ; The data offset is where the payload starts, measured from the start
  ; of the SDPCM header. It must be inside the frame.
  If hlen < #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN Or hlen > size
    cyw43_rxShort = cyw43_rxShort + 1
    ProcedureReturn 3
  EndIf

  If chan = #CYW43_CHAN_CONTROL
    If size < (hlen + #CYW43_BCDC_HDRLEN)
      cyw43_rxShort = cyw43_rxShort + 1
      ProcedureReturn 3
    EndIf
    flags = cyw43_RxLE32(hlen + #CYW43_BCDC_OFF_FLAGS)
    gotId = (flags >> #CYW43_BCDC_ID_SHIFT) & $FFFF
    If wantId >= 0 And gotId = wantId
      cyw43_replyOff = hlen + #CYW43_BCDC_HDRLEN
      cyw43_replyLen = size - cyw43_replyOff
      cyw43_ioctlStatus = cyw43_RxLE32(hlen + #CYW43_BCDC_OFF_STATUS)
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    ; A reply to somebody else's request, or to one that has already
    ; timed out. d:152 does exactly this - reads and discards until the
    ; id matches.
    ProcedureReturn 3
  EndIf

  If chan = #CYW43_CHAN_EVENT
    ; ==================================================================
    ; A FRAME THAT IS NOTHING BUT ITS OWN HEADER, AND IT IS NORMAL.
    ; RESOLVED ON THE BENCH 2026-08-27 - WHAT FOLLOWS IS MEASURED, NOT
    ; REASONED.
    ;
    ; Every successful scan on this board reported exactly one anomaly,
    ; `bad event prefix 1`, and everything else zero. The arithmetic
    ; said 17 rx frames = 10 ioctl replies + 5 escan PARTIALs + 1
    ; SUCCESS + one thing nobody asked for, and the standing guess was
    ; an unsolicited event. THE GUESS WAS WRONG. Temporary
    ; instrumentation captured the frame and it is not an event at all:
    ;
    ;   frame length 12   sdpcm channel 1   sdpcm doff 12
    ;   bdc data_offset 0 (read from a byte that is not in the frame)
    ;   0C 00 F3 FF 00 01 00 0C 00 15 00 00
    ;
    ; Read against the SDPCM offsets declared at lines 805-813: length
    ; 12, its complement, sequence 0, channel 1 (EVENT), next-length 0,
    ; DATA OFFSET 12 - and 12 is #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN, so the payload is
    ; zero bytes long and the header IS the whole frame. The two bytes
    ; that carry information are the flow-control byte, $00, and the
    ; credit window, $15 = 21.
    ;
    ; So it is none of the three things it was suspected of being. It
    ; is not a short event this driver should decode - there is no
    ; event in it. It is not an event the mask should have left off -
    ; Cyw43SetEventMask enables exactly one bit, ESCAN_RESULT, and this
    ; frame carries no event type to mask. And it is not a framing
    ; miscalculation: p came out as 16 from data_offset 0, which is
    ; correct arithmetic over a BDC header that was never sent, and
    ; 16 + 72 > 12 whatever p had been. It is the firmware giving the
    ; host a credit update with no packet attached.
    ;
    ; THE VENDOR DRIVER DOES EXACTLY THIS AND SAYS NOTHING ABOUT IT.
    ; brcmf_sdio_hdparse accepts the frame - dat_offset 12 passes
    ; `< SDPCM_HDRLEN || > rd->len` at sdio.c:1438 - the credit and the
    ; flow-control byte are taken at sdio.c:1461-1470, and then
    ; readframes does `skb_pull(pkt, rd->dat_offset)` and
    ; `if (pkt->len == 0) brcmu_pkt_buf_free_skb(pkt);` at
    ; sdio.c:2044-2047. Freed, uncounted, unremarked. The frame is part
    ; of the protocol.
    ;
    ; WHAT CHANGED HERE IS THE COUNTER IT LANDS IN, AND NOTHING ELSE.
    ; The frame was already being dropped correctly; it was being
    ; dropped into cyw43_evBadIsland, which means "an event arrived too
    ; malformed to hold its own prefix" and is an alarm. An alarm that
    ; fires once on every healthy scan is an alarm nobody reads, and
    ; this one had already trained a reader to describe a clean run as
    ; having one unexplained frame. cyw43_evEmpty is the benign count
    ; and cyw43_evBadIsland goes back to meaning what it says.
    ;
    ; THE CREDIT IS ALREADY TAKEN, twenty lines up, before the channel
    ; is even looked at - which is the whole reason this frame exists
    ; and is why it must be counted and dropped rather than refused
    ; earlier. Moving the credit read inside the channel tests would
    ; throw away the one useful byte in it.
    ;
    ; SCOPED TO THE EVENT CHANNEL because that is where it was seen. A
    ; payload-less frame on the data or glom channel would land in
    ; cyw43_rxOther, which is already a count-and-ignore, and widening
    ; this test to cover a frame nobody has observed would be the same
    ; kind of guess this paragraph exists to replace.
    ; ==================================================================
    If size = hlen
      cyw43_evEmpty = cyw43_evEmpty + 1
      ProcedureReturn 3
    EndIf

    ; The BDC header sits between the SDPCM header and the Ethernet
    ; frame, and its data_offset is in units of four bytes. G and
    ; Linux's bcdc.c both honour it; RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico lines
    ; 3222-3238 records that our own bench has seen data_offset = 1 on
    ; event frames, so hard-coding it would shift every field below by
    ; four bytes.
    doff = cyw43_Rx8(hlen + 3)
    p = hlen + 4 + (doff << 2)
    If (p + #CYW43_EV_PKTLEN) > size
      cyw43_evBadIsland = cyw43_evBadIsland + 1
      ProcedureReturn 3
    EndIf
    If (p + #CYW43_EV_PKTLEN) > #CYW43_RXBUF
      cyw43_evBadIsland = cyw43_evBadIsland + 1
      ProcedureReturn 3
    EndIf

    ; THE BIG-ENDIAN ISLAND. FIVE fields since the join went in, network
    ; order, named readers.
    ;
    ; FLAGS AND REASON WERE ADDED 2026-08-27 AND THEY ARE NOT OPTIONAL
    ; EXTRAS. `flags & 1` on event 16 is the entire link-up test, and
    ; reason is what separates "the supplicant timed out" from "the AP
    ; refused the key" - two failures that look identical without it and
    ; want opposite responses. A scan needed neither, which is why they
    ; were not here before; a join cannot be written without both.
    cyw43_evP       = p
    cyw43_evFlags   = cyw43_RxBE16(p + #CYW43_EV_OFF_FLAGS)
    cyw43_evType    = cyw43_RxBE32(p + #CYW43_EV_OFF_TYPE)
    cyw43_evStatus  = cyw43_RxBE32(p + #CYW43_EV_OFF_STATUS)
    cyw43_evReason  = cyw43_RxBE32(p + #CYW43_EV_OFF_REASON)
    cyw43_evDatalen = cyw43_RxBE32(p + #CYW43_EV_OFF_DATALEN)

    If cyw43_evType <> #CYW43_E_ESCAN_RESULT
      ; INTO THE RING, THEN RETURN 4. Two separate facts and they used
      ; to be one: before the join this arm was `cyw43_evOther + 1;
      ; return 3`, which means "a frame nobody wanted, keep polling".
      ; Every join event would have been counted and thrown away.
      ;
      ; THE COUNTER STAYS ALONGSIDE THE RING rather than being replaced
      ; by it. A non-zero cyw43_evOther with an empty ring means the
      ; caller has stopped draining, which is a different bug from a
      ; quiet radio and is invisible if the two facts share one number.
      cyw43_evOther = cyw43_evOther + 1
      cyw43_EvrPush(cyw43_evType, cyw43_evStatus, cyw43_evReason, cyw43_evFlags)
      ProcedureReturn 4
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 2
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; THE DATA CHANNEL. An 802.3 frame inside a BDC header inside SDPCM.
  ;
  ; UNTIL 2026-08-27 THIS WAS A COUNTER AND NOTHING ELSE, and the
  ; comment said so honestly: "nothing in this file yet has anywhere to
  ; put an Ethernet frame". net.pi4 is where it goes now.
  ;
  ; THE BDC HEADER IS FOUR BYTES AND ITS data_offset IS IN WORDS, the
  ; same shape as the event channel's above and read the same way. It
  ; is NOT assumed to be zero: the Pico bench has seen data_offset = 1
  ; on real frames (cyw43.pico:3222-3238), and hard-coding it would
  ; shift every byte of the Ethernet frame four places.
  ;
  ; THE COUNTER STAYS. It was the only evidence the radio was receiving
  ; at all before this arm existed, and a frame that fails the bounds
  ; check below still has to be counted somewhere.
  ; ====================================================================
  If chan = #CYW43_CHAN_DATA
    cyw43_rxOther = cyw43_rxOther + 1
    If size < (hlen + 4)
      cyw43_rxShort = cyw43_rxShort + 1
      ProcedureReturn 3
    EndIf
    doff = cyw43_Rx8(hlen + 3)
    p = hlen + 4 + (doff << 2)
    If p >= size
      cyw43_rxShort = cyw43_rxShort + 1
      ProcedureReturn 3
    EndIf
    cyw43_dataP = p
    cyw43_dataLen = size - p
    ProcedureReturn 5
  EndIf

  ; Glom frames and anything else. Counted so that a non-zero total is
  ; available as evidence the radio is receiving something, and
  ; otherwise ignored - this file does not de-glom.
  cyw43_rxOther = cyw43_rxOther + 1
  ProcedureReturn 3
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_Poll - drain frames for up to timeoutMs, stopping early on the
;  thing the caller is waiting for.
;
;  wantId >= 0   stop on a control reply carrying that BCDC id
;  wantEvent = 1 stop on a validated ESCAN_RESULT
;
;  Both may be set: an ioctl's reply and an event can be outstanding at
;  the same time and the loop must not throw away whichever arrives
;  second.
;
;  NO DELAY IN THE LOOP. Each pass is one 64-byte F2 read; a scan
;  delivers results in bursts and the F2 FIFO is finite, so a sleep here
;  loses networks. The Pico driver's note at line 4395 is the same
;  finding from the other bus.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_Poll(wantId.i, wantEvent.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define r.i

  budget = (cyw43_hz / 1000) * timeoutMs
  If budget < 1
    budget = 1
  EndIf
  t0 = cyw43_Ticks()

  cyw43_TraceIo(#CYW43_TR_POLL_ENTER)

  Repeat
    r = cyw43_RxFrame(wantId)
    If r = -1
      ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_BADFRAME
    EndIf
    If r = 1
      ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
    EndIf
    If r = 2 And wantEvent <> 0
      ProcedureReturn 2
    EndIf
    If (cyw43_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      cyw43_TraceIo(#CYW43_TR_POLL_TIMEOUT)
      ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TIMEOUT
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_PollEvent - drain frames until the ring has something, or the
;  budget expires. Returns 1 or 0.
;
;  IT DOES NOT POP. Filling the ring and consuming from it are separate
;  acts because a single poll can deliver several events - a join
;  routinely gets AUTH and LINK inside one 20 ms slice - and a poller
;  that popped would hand back one and leave the caller with no way of
;  knowing there were more. The caller loops on cyw43_EvrPop until it
;  returns 0, then polls again.
;
;  wantId = -1 MATCHES NO REPLY, which is what makes this legal to call
;  with no ioctl outstanding. A control frame that does arrive is still
;  read and dropped by cyw43_RxFrame, exactly as before.
;
;  IT RETURNS THE MOMENT THE RING IS NON-EMPTY EVEN IF THE RING WAS
;  ALREADY NON-EMPTY ON ENTRY, and that is deliberate rather than lazy:
;  a caller that has not drained does not want to spend its slice
;  waiting for a further event it has no room to notice.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_PollEvent(timeoutMs.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define r.i

  If cyw43_evrCount > 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  budget = (cyw43_hz / 1000) * timeoutMs
  If budget < 1
    budget = 1
  EndIf
  t0 = cyw43_Ticks()

  Repeat
    r = cyw43_RxFrame(-1)
    If r = -1
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If cyw43_evrCount > 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    If (cyw43_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43Ioctl - one command to the firmware, and the reply.
;
;  kindSet  1 for a SET, 0 for a GET
;  cmd      one of the #CYW43_C_* numbers
;  *src     the payload, or 0
;  len      payload length
;
;  FOR A GET, len IS DOING TWO JOBS AT ONCE and this trips people up.
;  It is the number of bytes sent AND the size of the answer the
;  firmware is told it may return. Ask for "ver\0" as 4 bytes and the
;  answer may be 4 bytes long. The rule is: PASS THE SIZE OF THE ANSWER
;  YOU WANT, with the name at the front and zeros behind it.
;
;  Returns #CYW43_IO_OK, or a negative code. A well-framed reply
;  carrying a non-zero firmware status is #CYW43_IO_STATUS and NOT OK -
;  every ioctl below WLC_UP can come back perfectly framed and refused.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43Ioctl(kindSet.i, cmd.i, *src, len.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define total.i
  Define padded.i
  Define i.i
  Define id.i
  Define flags.i
  Define r.i

  If len < 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  total = #CYW43_CTRL_HDRLEN + len
  padded = (total + 3) & (~3)
  If padded > #CYW43_TXBUF Or total > #CYW43_MAX_FRAME
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf

  ; FLOW CONTROL, AND THIS FILE DOES NOT BLOCK FOR EVER ON IT. A send is
  ; meant to stall while the last credit seen equals the sequence about
  ; to go out. Draining for a moment usually refreshes it. If it does
  ; not, the frame goes anyway and cyw43_stalls counts it - a hard block
  ; would turn one lost credit into a board that stops printing, and at
  ; this stage there is exactly one ioctl outstanding at a time. A
  ; non-zero cyw43_stalls on the bench is the thing to look at: it means
  ; this shortcut is being leaned on rather than merely present.
  If cyw43_credit = cyw43_seq
    cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_STALL)
    cyw43_Poll(-1, 0, 100)
    If cyw43_credit = cyw43_seq
      cyw43_stalls = cyw43_stalls + 1
    EndIf
  EndIf

  cyw43_reqid = (cyw43_reqid + 1) & $FFFF
  If cyw43_reqid = 0
    cyw43_reqid = 1
  EndIf
  id = cyw43_reqid

  flags = (id << #CYW43_BCDC_ID_SHIFT) | (#CYW43_IFACE_STA << #CYW43_BCDC_IF_SHIFT)
  If kindSet <> 0
    flags = flags | #CYW43_BCDC_SET
  EndIf

  For i = 0 To padded - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + i, 0)
  Next

  ; The SDPCM header. The length field carries the UNPADDED total, which
  ; is what the firmware's own size/complement check is computed over.
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_LEN, total & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_LEN + 1, (total >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_COMP, (~total) & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_COMP + 1, ((~total) >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_SEQ, cyw43_seq & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_CHAN, #CYW43_CHAN_CONTROL)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_NEXT, 0)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_DOFF, #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN)

  ; The BCDC header.
  ;
  ; PokeL IS SAFE HERE AND cyw43_PutLE32 IS NOT NEEDED, which is worth
  ; stating because the two iovar builders below had to stop using it.
  ; cyw43_tx is a Dim .l array, so its base is at least 4-aligned, and
  ; the four offsets are 12+0, 12+4, 12+8 and 12+12 - every one a
  ; multiple of four. That is the invariant: if #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN or
  ; any #CYW43_BCDC_OFF_* ever stops being a multiple of four, these
  ; four lines must become cyw43_PutLE32 the same day.
  PokeL(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BCDC_OFF_CMD, cmd)
  PokeL(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BCDC_OFF_LEN, len & $FFFF)
  PokeL(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BCDC_OFF_FLAGS, flags)
  PokeL(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BCDC_OFF_STATUS, 0)

  For i = 0 To len - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_CTRL_HDRLEN + i, PeekA(*src + i))
  Next

  cyw43_ioctlStatus = 0
  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_TX)
  If cyw43_F2Xfer(1, @cyw43_tx[0], padded) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_BADFRAME
  EndIf
  cyw43_seq = (cyw43_seq + 1) & $FF

  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_WAIT)
  r = cyw43_Poll(id, 0, timeoutMs)
  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_REPLY)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  ; A WELL-FORMED REPLY IS NOT A CALL THAT WORKED. The raw signed status
  ; is all this file offers: the BCME_* codes are small negative
  ; integers and no reference driver on this disk enumerates them, so a
  ; table here would be invented. Diagnose by POSITION instead - which
  ; call returned it is nearly always the answer.
  If cyw43_ioctlStatus <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_STATUS
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43IoctlSet(cmd.i, *src, len.i, timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43Ioctl(1, cmd, *src, len, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43IoctlGet(cmd.i, *src, len.i, timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43Ioctl(0, cmd, *src, len, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; A SET of one 32-bit value, which is most of them.
Procedure.i Cyw43IoctlSetU32(cmd.i, v.i, timeoutMs.i)
  PokeL(@cyw43_word[0], v)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSet(cmd, @cyw43_word[0], 4, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_PutName - an iovar name into a buffer, and the offset after it.
;
;  This is the third copy of a loop that Cyw43SetEventMask and
;  Cyw43ScanStart each wrote out by hand, and the fifth caller made it
;  worth naming. It returns the length INCLUDING the NUL, which is where
;  the value goes - so every caller reads
;      n = cyw43_PutName(...)   ; and the struct starts at n
;  rather than remembering to add one.
;
;  PeekA OVER A `?` LABEL, NEVER Read/Restore. "Read strides 2 bytes
;  whatever the element width - Data.a returns every OTHER byte" is an
;  open bug on every target, Bugs/Open bugs.md line 309, open since
;  2026-08-08. A name half-read is not an error the firmware reports; it
;  simply does not recognise it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_PutName(dst.i, src.i, namelen.i)
  Define i.i
  For i = 0 To namelen - 1
    PokeB(dst + i, PeekA(src + i))
  Next
  PokeB(dst + namelen, 0)
  ProcedureReturn namelen + 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  THE CLM - THE REGULATORY BLOB, AND WHY THE SCAN DOES NOT WORK
;  WITHOUT IT.
;
;  WHAT WENT WRONG, IN ONE PARAGRAPH, BECAUSE THIS FILE SPENT A DAY
;  LOOKING LIKE IT WORKED. On 2026-08-27 this driver got all the way
;  through bring-up on real silicon, sent bsscfg:event_msgs and WLC_UP,
;  and had BOTH accepted with BCDC status zero - and Cyw43Ioctl checks
;  that status, so those were not silent successes. Then the escan came
;  back refused: Cyw43ScanStart returned #CYW43_IO_STATUS with the
;  firmware's own status word at -4. -4 is BCME_NOTUP in the standard
;  Broadcom error table, which Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_feature.c:20
;  pins by defining BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED as 23. WLC_UP had succeeded and
;  the radio was still not up.
;
;  THE CAUSE WAS THIS SECTION NOT EXISTING. The CLM carries the channel
;  and power limits for every regulatory domain the part knows. Without
;  it the PHY has no legal channel list, so it does not come up, so
;  every scan and every channel ioctl is refused with NOTUP - while
;  WLC_UP itself, which only brings the MAC up, still returns 0. Two
;  different meanings of "up", and only one of them was being asked
;  about.
;
;  WHY IT WAS MISSED. brcmfmac marks the blob BRCMF_FW_REQF_OPTIONAL and
;  Reference/firmware/README.md repeats that word. "Optional" there
;  means Linux will still probe the device without the file present - it
;  does not mean the radio will scan.
;
;  THE ORDER IS clmload BEFORE WLC_UP and that is not a preference:
;  brcmf_c_preinit_dcmds runs brcmf_c_process_clm_blob during attach,
;  and WLC_UP is not sent until brcmf_cfg80211_up, which is a network
;  interface being opened much later. pico-sdk puts clmload first too.
;  Cyw43WifiUp therefore does the CLM before the event mask.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_Signed32 - a firmware status word as the small negative number
;  it actually is.
;
;  THE BCME_* CODES ARE SIGNED 32-BIT AND EVERY READER IN THIS FILE IS
;  64-BIT. cyw43_RxLE32 assembles four bytes into an .i, which is 64
;  bits wide here, so $FFFFFFFC is $00000000FFFFFFFC unless something
;  says otherwise - and a bench log that prints "ioctl status
;  4294967292" instead of "-4" is a log nobody can look up in an error
;  table. The same trap as cyw43_RxI16 and RSSI, one field wider.
;
;  IT IS IDEMPOTENT ON PURPOSE. Passing an already-negative -4 masks to
;  $FFFFFFFC and comes back as -4, so it is safe to apply to a value
;  whose width somebody else has already settled - which matters,
;  because whether the arithmetic above sign-extends is a property of
;  the backend and not of this file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_Signed32(v.i)
  Define u.i
  u = v & $FFFFFFFF
  If u >= $80000000
    u = u - $100000000
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn u
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ClmLoad - push the blob at the firmware, one chunk per ioctl.
;
;  Each chunk is one WLC_SET_VAR of "clmload" whose value is a
;  brcmf_dload_data_le followed by that many bytes of blob:
;
;    +0   "clmload\0"                             8
;    +8   u16 flag  = $1000 | BEGIN? | END?
;    +10  u16 dload_type = DL_TYPE_CLM (2)
;    +12  u32 len   = the bytes IN THIS CHUNK, not the whole blob
;    +16  u32 crc   = 0, always - see the constants block
;    +20  the chunk
;
;  THE FLAGS ARE THE PART THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG AND THIS BOARD IS
;  THE FIRST TO EXERCISE THEM. DL_BEGIN goes on the FIRST chunk only and
;  DL_END on the LAST chunk only, and a blob that fits in one chunk
;  carries both - which is the only case the twin has ever run. Getting
;  END onto the wrong chunk does not produce a refused ioctl: the
;  firmware assembles whatever it was given and clmload_status reports
;  the verdict afterwards, which is exactly why the status query below
;  is not optional.
;
;  brcmf_c_process_clm_blob's loop sets DL_END BEFORE sending the last
;  chunk, not after, and clears DL_BEGIN AFTER the first send. This does
;  the same thing arithmetically - `off = 0` is the first and
;  `off + len >= length` is the last - which is the same test written
;  where it can be read rather than tracked through a mutated variable.
;
;  THE PADDING TO 8 IS THE TWIN'S AND NOT brcmfmac's, deliberately.
;  brcmf_c_download sends exactly struct_size(buf, data, len), i.e.
;  12 + len with no rounding. pico-sdk and RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:3676
;  round the whole payload up to a multiple of 8, so the twin has been
;  sending four zero bytes past its declared length to this same
;  firmware family for months without complaint. Both are proven; the
;  padded one is proven by us. The bytes are past the `len` field the
;  firmware parses, so they are ignored either way - and if a future
;  bench run ever suspects them, deleting the While loop is the whole
;  change.
;
;  Returns #CYW43_IO_OK, or the transport code from the first chunk that
;  was refused. cyw43_clmChunks says how many got through, which turns
;  "the CLM failed" into "the CLM failed on chunk 2 of 3".
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ClmLoad(*blob, length.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define off.i
  Define len.i
  Define flag.i
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define p.i

  ; TWO FULL COMPARISONS EITHER SIDE OF THE Or. The house rule exists
  ; because a bare pointer next to a comparison is a different
  ; expression in this language than it reads as.
  If *blob = 0 Or length <= 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf

  cyw43_clmChunks = 0
  p = @cyw43_clm[0]
  off = 0

  While off < length
    len = length - off
    If len > #CYW43_CLM_CHUNK
      len = #CYW43_CLM_CHUNK
    EndIf

    flag = #CYW43_DLOAD_VER
    If off = 0
      flag = flag | #CYW43_DL_BEGIN
    EndIf
    If (off + len) >= length
      flag = flag | #CYW43_DL_END
    EndIf

    n = cyw43_PutName(p, ?cyw43_ClmLoadName, #CYW43_CLMLOAD_NAMELEN)

    ; The two u16s go in as bytes because they are not word-sized and
    ; the two u32s go through cyw43_PutLE32 because this file does not
    ; hand-check alignment at every store any more. Here the name is
    ; eight bytes so `len` lands at +12 and `crc` at +16 - both already
    ; word aligned, so PokeL would in fact be legal. IT IS STILL NOT
    ; USED. The rule this file settled on after the alignment fault of
    ; 2026-08-27 is that an iovar STRUCT FIELD is written with
    ; cyw43_PutLE32 whatever the arithmetic says today, because the
    ; arithmetic changes the moment somebody renames the iovar, and a
    ; store that is legal only because the name happens to be seven
    ; characters long is a trap with a fuse in it.
    PokeB(p + n,     flag & $FF)
    PokeB(p + n + 1, (flag >> 8) & $FF)
    PokeB(p + n + 2, #CYW43_DL_TYPE_CLM)
    PokeB(p + n + 3, 0)
    cyw43_PutLE32(p + n + 4, len)
    cyw43_PutLE32(p + n + 8, 0)

    For i = 0 To len - 1
      PokeB(p + n + #CYW43_CLM_HDR + i, PeekA(*blob + off + i))
    Next
    n = n + #CYW43_CLM_HDR + len

    While (n & 7) <> 0
      PokeB(p + n, 0)
      n = n + 1
    Wend

    cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_CLM_CHUNK)
    r = Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_VAR, p, n, timeoutMs)
    If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
      ProcedureReturn r
    EndIf

    cyw43_clmChunks = cyw43_clmChunks + 1
    off = off + len
  Wend

  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_CLM_DONE)
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_ClmStatusQuery - ask the firmware whether the blob it assembled
;  is any good, and DO NOT SKIP THIS.
;
;  Lower case because it is internal: Cyw43ClmLoadAndVerify is the name
;  a caller uses, and Cyw43ClmStatus() further down is the ACCESSOR that
;  reports what this found. Three near-identical names is one more than
;  is comfortable, and the alternative - folding the query into the
;  loader - would have made "the upload was refused" and "the upload
;  completed and the blob is bad" the same return value.
;
;  Every chunk above came back with BCDC status 0, and that means only
;  that the ioctl was well formed and the firmware took the bytes. It
;  says nothing about whether the assembled blob parsed, whether the
;  chunks arrived in order, or whether the first/last flags made sense.
;  A truncated or misordered CLM is accepted chunk by chunk and then
;  quietly does nothing, and the symptom of that is the symptom of not
;  loading it at all: NOTUP on the next scan, three hundred lines away
;  from the cause.
;
;  "clmload_status\0" is fifteen bytes and the answer is a u32, so the
;  GET asks for 19 - which is the twin's number and follows this file's
;  own rule that for a GET, `len` is the size of the answer you will
;  accept. Asking for 15 would leave the firmware no room to reply.
;
;  Returns #CYW43_IO_OK only when the status word is zero.
;  #CYW43_IO_CLMBAD means the upload completed and the firmware rejected
;  what it assembled; the raw word is in Cyw43ClmStatus().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i cyw43_ClmStatusQuery(timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define p.i

  p = @cyw43_arg[0]
  n = cyw43_PutName(p, ?cyw43_ClmStatusName, #CYW43_CLMSTAT_NAMELEN)
  For i = n To 18
    PokeB(p + i, 0)
  Next

  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_CLM_STATUS)
  r = Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_VAR, p, 19, timeoutMs)
  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_CLM_VERDICT)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  ; A reply too short to hold the answer is a framing fault, not a
  ; regulatory one, so it gets the framing code. Reporting it as
  ; CLMBAD would send the next person to look at the blob.
  If cyw43_replyLen < 4
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_BADFRAME
  EndIf

  cyw43_clmStatus = cyw43_Signed32(cyw43_RxLE32(cyw43_replyOff))
  If cyw43_clmStatus <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_CLMBAD
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ClmLoadAndVerify - the whole contract in one name.
;
;  The loader AND the status query, and the status MUST be 0. There is
;  no point continuing to a scan whose every channel decision comes out
;  of a blob the firmware has just said it could not use.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ClmLoadAndVerify(*blob, length.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define r.i
  cyw43_clmStatus = -1
  r = Cyw43ClmLoad(*blob, length, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_ClmStatusQuery(timeoutMs)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43GetIovarStr - a GET whose answer is text, into cyw43_arg.
;
;  `buflen` is the SIZE OF THE ANSWER WE WILL ACCEPT, not the size of
;  the name - see the contract at Cyw43Ioctl. 128 is what both reference
;  drivers ask for and it is what #CYW43_ARGBUF was sized around.
;
;  The reply is a NUL-terminated ASCII string at Cyw43ReplyPtr() and is
;  NOT parsed here: a library that parsed it would have to carry a
;  vocabulary of firmware version strings, and printing it raw is what
;  turns it into a bench fact.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43GetIovarStr(src.i, namelen.i, buflen.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define p.i
  If buflen > #CYW43_ARGBUF
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  p = @cyw43_arg[0]
  n = cyw43_PutName(p, src, namelen)
  If n > buflen
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  For i = n To buflen - 1
    PokeB(p + i, 0)
  Next
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_VAR, p, buflen, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43GetClmVersion - "clmver", and THE FIRST HUMAN-READABLE PROOF
;  THAT THE CLM WENT IN.
;
;  clmload_status says the transfer completed. This says what the
;  firmware thinks it is now holding: several \n-separated Key: value
;  lines - API, Data, Compiler, ClmImport, Creation. A sane string here
;  and a zero status together are the checkpoint; either alone is not.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43GetClmVersion(timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43GetIovarStr(?cyw43_ClmVerName, #CYW43_CLMVER_NAMELEN, 128, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43GetCountry - read the regulatory domain the firmware is using.
;
;  struct brcmf_fil_country_le, Reference/v6.12_brcmfmac_fwil_types.h
;  lines 764-768, twelve bytes:
;
;    +0  char country_abbrev[4]   BRCMF_COUNTRY_BUF_SZ is 4, t:146
;    +4  le32 rev
;    +8  char ccode[4]
;
;  The GET therefore asks for 8 (name and NUL) + 12 = 20 bytes back.
;
;  WHY THIS FILE READS THE COUNTRY AND DOES NOT SET IT - and this was a
;  decision, taken from the two reference drivers rather than by
;  analogy with the twin.
;
;  THE TWIN SETS IT. RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:3936 sends the `country`
;  iovar with "XX" and rev -1 before WLC_UP, because that is what
;  pico-sdk does, and pico-sdk does it because the Pico W's NVRAM says
;  `ccode=ALL` - a board declaring itself calibrated for everywhere and
;  therefore committed to nothing, which leaves the firmware with no
;  domain to pick until the host names one.
;
;  brcmfmac DOES NOT SET IT AT BRING-UP. brcmf_c_preinit_dcmds loads the
;  CLM and never touches `country`; the only place the iovar is written
;  is brcmf_cfg80211_reg_notifier (cfg80211.c:8180-8221), which runs
;  when userspace or CRDA asks for a specific domain, and its first act
;  is to GET the current value. Its comment at :8190 - "The country code
;  gets set to 00 by default at boot, ignore" - says plainly that the
;  firmware already has a domain before anyone sets one. A Raspberry Pi
;  scans out of the box, before any regulatory daemon has run.
;
;  AND THIS BOARD'S NVRAM HAS NO ccode LINE AT ALL. Reference/firmware/
;  brcmfmac43455-sdio.txt does not mention ccode, regrev or country -
;  checked, not assumed. So the "ccode=ALL leaves it undecided" argument
;  that motivates the twin's SET does not even apply here: there is
;  nothing in the NVRAM to leave it undecided.
;
;  SO: NO SET. Sending `country` before WLC_UP would be copying the
;  twin's answer to a question this board does not ask, and it would
;  overwrite whatever the CLM's own default is with a guess. If the
;  escan is still refused after the CLM loads, a SET is the next thing
;  to try and it is ten lines - but it is not sent speculatively, and
;  this GET is here so that a bench run can PRINT what the firmware
;  actually chose instead of anybody having to reason about it again.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43GetCountry(timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43GetIovarStr(?cyw43_CountryName, #CYW43_COUNTRY_NAMELEN, 20, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ScanVerProbe - does this firmware want escan v1 or v2?
;
;  brcmfmac decides by asking whether the iovar EXISTS and by nothing
;  else. brcmf_feat_iovar_int_get (feature.c:184-201) does the GET with
;  fwil_fwerr set so the firmware's own error reaches it, and then:
;
;      if (err != -BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED)   -> enable the feature
;
;  and BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED is 23 (feature.c:20). scan_ver is wired to
;  BRCMF_FEAT_SCAN_V2 at feature.c:341, and cfg80211.c:1451-1466 shows
;  what the flag buys: v2 sends brcmf_escan_params_v2_le with version
;  BRCMF_ESCAN_REQ_VERSION_V2, and v1 converts it back down.
;
;  SO THE ANSWER IS IN THE STATUS WORD, NOT IN THE VALUE. A firmware
;  that does not know the name replies -23; anything else - including a
;  reply carrying a version number - means v2. That is why this returns
;  the transport code and stashes BOTH the status and the value rather
;  than collapsing them into a verdict: -23 and "some other refusal" are
;  different answers and this file will not guess which one it got.
;
;  This driver's escan request is v1 and is byte-for-byte the twin's,
;  which has been scanning for months, so v1 is very probably right for
;  this build too. THIS COSTS ONE IOCTL AND SETTLES IT, which is worth
;  more than the probability.
;
;  "scan_ver\0" is nine bytes and an int GET wants a u32 back, so 13.
;  brcmf_fil_iovar_int_get sends exactly that shape.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanVerProbe(timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define p.i

  cyw43_scanVer = -1
  cyw43_scanVerStat = 0

  p = @cyw43_arg[0]
  n = cyw43_PutName(p, ?cyw43_ScanVerName, #CYW43_SCANVER_NAMELEN)
  For i = n To 12
    PokeB(p + i, 0)
  Next

  r = Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_VAR, p, 13, timeoutMs)
  cyw43_scanVerRc = r

  ; cyw43_ioctlStatus IS READ WHETHER OR NOT THE CALL SUCCEEDED, and
  ; that is the whole point of this procedure. #CYW43_IO_STATUS means a
  ; perfectly framed reply carrying a refusal, and the refusal is the
  ; answer we came for.
  cyw43_scanVerStat = cyw43_Signed32(cyw43_ioctlStatus)

  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  If cyw43_replyLen >= 4
    cyw43_scanVer = cyw43_RxLE32(cyw43_replyOff)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SupWpaProbe - CAN THIS FIRMWARE RUN THE SUPPLICANT ITSELF?
;
;  THIS IS THE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL SINGLE IOCTL IN THE FILE, because the
;  answer decides how much code the WPA2 path needs:
;
;    PRESENT - the host sets `bsscfg:sup_wpa` = 1, hands the firmware
;              the ASCII passphrase through WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK, and the
;              FIRMWARE does PBKDF2 and the whole EAPOL four-way
;              handshake. The host writes about two hundred lines and
;              touches no crypto at all.
;
;    ABSENT  - the HOST must derive the PMK with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1 over
;              4096 iterations and then run the four-way handshake by
;              hand over the data channel: EAPOL framing, key
;              descriptors, nonce handling, a KCK/KEK split, an AES key
;              wrap and a MIC on every frame. hmac.pi4 and sha256.pi4
;              exist here and the RP2040 tree has a TLS stack, so the
;              pieces are on the disk - but SHA-1 is not among them and
;              PBKDF2-SHA1 4096 on this part is a whole task on its own.
;
;  SO IT IS ASKED, NOT ASSUMED, AND IT IS ASKED THE WAY brcmfmac ASKS.
;  brcmf_feat_iovar_int_get (feature.c:184-201) does a plain integer GET
;  with fwil_fwerr set so the firmware's own error reaches the caller,
;  and then:
;
;      if (err != -BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED)   -> enable BRCMF_FEAT_FWSUP
;
;  BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED is 23 (feature.c:20) and the wiring is
;  feature.c:341, `brcmf_feat_iovar_int_get(ifp, BRCMF_FEAT_FWSUP,
;  "sup_wpa")`. So -23 means ABSENT and EVERYTHING ELSE - including a
;  clean 0, and including some other refusal - means PRESENT.
;
;  THAT LAST CLAUSE IS UNCOMFORTABLE AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT
;  SOFTENED. "Any error except one specific error means yes" is a
;  strange test to inherit, but it is the test the Linux driver has
;  shipped for a decade against this exact silicon, and inventing a
;  narrower one here would mean this file and brcmfmac could disagree
;  about the same firmware. What this procedure does instead is keep
;  the three facts apart - transport verdict, firmware status, returned
;  value - so a reader can see WHICH answer arrived rather than a
;  verdict computed from it. Cyw43SupWpaPresent() applies the rule; the
;  three accessors let an instrument print the evidence beside it.
;
;  THE SAME SHAPE AS Cyw43ScanVerProbe, deliberately - that probe
;  settled the escan version question on this bench on 2026-08-27 and
;  this is the same question about a different name. "sup_wpa\0" is
;  eight bytes and an int GET wants a u32 back, so twelve.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SupWpaProbe(timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define p.i

  cyw43_supWpa = -1
  cyw43_supWpaStat = 0

  p = @cyw43_arg[0]
  n = cyw43_PutName(p, ?cyw43_SupWpaName, #CYW43_SUPWPA_NAMELEN)
  For i = n To 11
    PokeB(p + i, 0)
  Next

  r = Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_VAR, p, 12, timeoutMs)
  cyw43_supWpaRc = r

  ; READ WHETHER OR NOT THE CALL SUCCEEDED. #CYW43_IO_STATUS is a
  ; perfectly framed reply carrying a refusal, and the refusal is the
  ; answer we came for - exactly as in Cyw43ScanVerProbe.
  cyw43_supWpaStat = cyw43_Signed32(cyw43_ioctlStatus)

  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  If cyw43_replyLen >= 4
    cyw43_supWpa = cyw43_RxLE32(cyw43_replyOff)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

; brcmfmac's rule, in one line, applied to whatever the probe last saw.
;
; RETURNS -1 IF THE PROBE HAS NOT RUN, and that is a third answer rather
; than a defensive 0. A caller that has not asked must not be told "no
; firmware supplicant" - that is the probe_Count lesson from
; pi4WifiScan, where a control printed a confident 0 for a comparison
; that had never happened.
Procedure.i Cyw43SupWpaPresent()
  If cyw43_supWpaRc = 0 And cyw43_supWpaStat = 0 And cyw43_supWpa = -1
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If cyw43_supWpaStat = -23
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetEventMask - tell the firmware which events to send.
;
;  The iovar is "bsscfg:event_msgs": a NUL-terminated name, then a u32
;  bsscfg index, then the mask. Event n lives in mask[n / 8] at bit
;  n % 8, so ESCAN_RESULT (69) is mask[8] bit 5 = $20.
;
;  THE MASK LENGTH IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM ANY HEADER ON THIS DISK.
;  brcmfmac uses BRCMF_EVENTING_MASK_LEN, which v6.12's fweh.h does not
;  define - the enum it was computed from has no BRCMF_E_LAST any more.
;  19 is what RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico line 4227 sends and is bench-proven
;  against real firmware; 19 bytes covers events 0 to 151, which is
;  every code fweh.h names. It is written as a named constant rather
;  than a literal precisely because its provenance is weaker than
;  everything else in this section.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;
;  THE MODE ARGUMENT WAS ADDED 2026-08-27 AND THE ALTERNATIVE WAS
;  REJECTED DELIBERATELY. The tempting shape is one mask with every bit
;  this file might ever want, set once, for ever - "the bit arithmetic
;  lives in exactly one place" is the argument the Pico driver makes
;  (cyw43.pico:4975-4985) and it is a good one. It is rejected HERE for
;  a reason that does not apply there: the scan path on this part is
;  gated by tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py --scan, which cross-checks
;  transport counters against the number of frames a clean scan
;  produces. Enabling eleven more event types would let unsolicited
;  traffic into a run whose whole value is that every counter
;  reconciles, and a gate that has to tolerate noise is a gate that
;  stops catching things. One argument, two masks, and the bit
;  arithmetic still lives in exactly one procedure.
;
;    mode 0   ESCAN_RESULT only. What the scan has always sent.
;    mode 1   that, plus the ten events a join is decided by.
;
;  A mode outside the range is treated as 0 rather than refused: an
;  event mask is not a place to invent a new failure path, and a caller
;  that passes nonsense gets the behaviour this file has shipped all
;  along rather than a radio that reports nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_EVMASK_LEN = 19

#CYW43_EVMASK_SCAN = 0
#CYW43_EVMASK_JOIN = 1

Procedure cyw43_MaskBit(base.i, ev.i)
  Define byteIdx.i
  Define bitIdx.i
  Define was.i
  byteIdx = ev / 8
  bitIdx  = ev % 8
  was = PeekA(base + byteIdx)
  PokeB(base + byteIdx, was | (1 << bitIdx))
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43SetEventMask(mode.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Define byteIdx.i
  Define bitIdx.i

  ; "bsscfg:event_msgs", seventeen characters and a NUL.
  ;
  ; REACHED WITH ? AND PeekA, NEVER WITH Read/Restore, and that is not a
  ; style choice: "Read strides 2 bytes whatever the element width -
  ; Data.a returns every OTHER byte" is an open bug on every target,
  ; filed in the vault at Bugs/Open bugs.md line 309 and open since
  ; 2026-08-08. display.pi4 reaches its font the same way for the same
  ; reason. An iovar name half-read is not an error the firmware
  ; reports - it simply does not recognise the name.
  n = 0
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_EVMSGS_NAMELEN - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, PeekA(?cyw43_EventMsgsName + i))
    n = n + 1
  Next
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, 0)
  n = n + 1

  ; The bsscfg index, u32 0.
  ;
  ; cyw43_PutLE32 AND NOT PokeL. n is 18 here - the name is seventeen
  ; characters and a NUL - so a `str w` lands two bytes off a word
  ; boundary and takes an Alignment fault on Device memory. The whole
  ; argument is at cyw43_PutLE32. This one line cost a bench session.
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, 0)
  n = n + 4

  ; The mask, all zero except the one event this file wants.
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_EVMASK_LEN - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + n + i, 0)
  Next
  byteIdx = #CYW43_E_ESCAN_RESULT / 8
  bitIdx  = #CYW43_E_ESCAN_RESULT % 8
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + n + byteIdx, 1 << bitIdx)

  ; THE JOIN'S TEN. Each one is decoded by cyw43_JoinClassify and none
  ; is here speculatively - a bit set for an event nothing reads is a
  ; frame the transport has to fetch and throw away, and on this bus
  ; that is real microseconds and one more thing to explain in a log.
  ;
  ; ICV_ERROR (49) IS THE ONE THAT LOOKS LIKE PADDING AND IS NOT. It is
  ; a MIC failure, it arrives unsolicited and often from a NEIGHBOUR's
  ; traffic, and it is enabled so that it can be COUNTED - two within
  ; sixty seconds is the 802.11i countermeasure condition, which neither
  ; reference driver implements, so the threshold is unverified and the
  ; count is what gets printed.
  If mode = #CYW43_EVMASK_JOIN
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_SET_SSID)
    ; JOIN (1) AND ASSOC (7) ARE DIAGNOSTIC AND THEY EARNED THEIR PLACE
    ; ON THE BENCH. On 2026-08-27 a join to a 5 GHz access point
    ; produced AUTH status 0 and then SET_SSID status 1 - "the
    ; association failed" and not one word about why, because SET_SSID's
    ; status is a summary. Event 7 carries the association's OWN status
    ; and reason, which is where an 802.11 status code such as "the AP
    ; refused the channel" or "capability mismatch" actually lives. Two
    ; more bits and two more frames per join, against a whole class of
    ; failure that is otherwise unnameable.
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_JOIN)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_ASSOC)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_AUTH)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_DEAUTH)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_DEAUTH_IND)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_DISASSOC)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_DISASSOC_IND)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_LINK)
    ; ROAM IS SUBSCRIBED SO THAT A ROAM CANNOT HAPPEN UNNOTICED. The
    ; bring-up asks the firmware not to roam; this bit is how the host
    ; finds out if it did anyway, and a roam with a host supplicant is a
    ; link whose keys no longer match the access point they are aimed at.
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_ROAM)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_PRUNE)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_PSK_SUP)
    cyw43_MaskBit(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, #CYW43_EV_ICV_ERROR)
  EndIf

  n = n + #CYW43_EVMASK_LEN

  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_VAR, @cyw43_arg[0], n, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43WifiUp - the CLM, the event mask, and WLC_UP, in that order.
;
;  ORDER MATTERS AND IT IS NOT OBVIOUS. Three things happen here and
;  each is where it is for a reason:
;
;    1. THE CLM FIRST. brcmf_c_preinit_dcmds downloads the blob during
;       attach and WLC_UP does not happen until an interface is opened,
;       much later; pico-sdk sends clmload first as well. Without the
;       blob the PHY has no channel list, WLC_UP still returns 0, and
;       the escan is refused with BCME_NOTUP - which is exactly what
;       this board did on 2026-08-27. The long version is at THE CLM.
;
;    2. THE EVENT MASK BEFORE WLC_UP, because a mask written after the
;       interface is up is not applied to a scan already in flight, and
;       because both reference drivers do it in that order.
;
;    3. WLC_UP LAST, then a settle.
;
;  IT ALL LIVES IN ONE PROCEDURE BECAUSE Cyw43TransportReset IS HERE.
;  The reset zeroes the SDPCM sequence and the BCDC request id, so
;  anything sent before it is sent with numbers the firmware will then
;  see go backwards. A caller cannot do the CLM "before Cyw43WifiUp"
;  without either reaching past this file for the reset or corrupting
;  the sequence - so the CLM comes in as an argument instead, and the
;  order becomes this file's to keep rather than every caller's to
;  remember.
;
;  *clm = 0 IS ALLOWED AND MEANS "no blob available". It is not an
;  error and it is not silent: cyw43_clmChunks stays 0 and
;  Cyw43ClmStatus() stays -1, which a caller prints as "never ran".
;  Bring-up still completes; the scan will very likely be refused.
;
;  WLC_UP TAKES NO PAYLOAD and returns nothing. Its status word is the
;  whole answer - though note that on this part it can be 0 while the
;  radio is still not up, which is the trap this file fell into.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;
;  THE evMask ARGUMENT WAS ADDED 2026-08-27, AND IT IS A BREAKING CHANGE
;  TO THIS SIGNATURE RATHER THAN A GLOBAL SET BEFOREHAND. The
;  alternative - a `Cyw43SetEventMaskMode(m)` call that a caller must
;  remember to make BEFORE Cyw43WifiUp - is a second thing to get in the
;  right order, and the entire reason the CLM comes in as an argument
;  rather than as a call of its own is that this file keeps the ordering
;  instead of every caller remembering it. Adding a mode that had to be
;  set at the right moment would undo that in the same procedure that
;  argues for it. One more argument, no new ordering rule.
;
;  Pass #CYW43_EVMASK_SCAN to get exactly the behaviour this file has
;  shipped since the transport landed, or #CYW43_EVMASK_JOIN to also
;  receive the ten events an association is decided by.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43WifiUp(*clm, clmLen.i, evMask.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define r.i

  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_WIFIUP)
  Cyw43TransportReset()
  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_RESET_DONE)

  ; A full comparison on each side of the And, per the house rule that
  ; a bare pointer is never a truth value in this language.
  If *clm <> 0 And clmLen > 0
    r = Cyw43ClmLoadAndVerify(*clm, clmLen, timeoutMs)
    If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
      ProcedureReturn r
    EndIf
  EndIf

  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_EVMASK_SEND)
  r = Cyw43SetEventMask(evMask, timeoutMs)
  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_EVMASK_DONE)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_UP_SEND)
  r = Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_UP, 0, 0, timeoutMs)
  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_UP_DONE)

  ; THE SETTLE AFTER UP IS NOT POLITENESS, AND LEAVING IT OUT COST A RUN.
  ;
  ; WLC_UP's reply IS the completion - neither reference driver polls or
  ; waits on an event here - but the PHY is not ready the instant the
  ; reply lands. The escan sent immediately afterwards came back rejected
  ; with BCDC status -4 on this board, while WLC_UP itself had reported
  ; success, so the firmware was up enough to answer and not up enough to
  ; scan.
  ;
  ; RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:4283-4287 already had this written down, from
  ; the two reference drivers: "Post-UP delay: G says 50 ms, E says
  ; 100 ms, and E wins because this happens once per boot and there is no
  ; reason to hurry", and immediately after it, "BEFORE UP, escan and
  ; every channel/scan ioctl are REJECTED". The Pico W has been scanning
  ; for months with that delay in place. This file was written without
  ; it.
  ;
  ; 100 ms, matching the twin, and it is once per boot.
  If r = #CYW43_IO_OK
    cyw43_DelayUs(100000)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure



; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ScanStart - ask for an escan.
;
;  The iovar is "escan" and its payload is the escan params laid out at
;  the offsets in the constants block above. Everything not written here
;  is zero, and the zeros are meaningful: a zero SSID length is a
;  broadcast scan, a zero channel_num means every channel the firmware
;  is allowed to use.
;
;  -1 IN THE FOUR TIMING FIELDS ASKS FOR THE FIRMWARE'S OWN DEFAULTS.
;  Those defaults are firmware constants nobody here can read out. The
;  commonly quoted figures - about 40 ms active dwell, 110 ms passive,
;  45 ms home - are general Broadcom numbers and are UNVERIFIED for this
;  build, which is why the diagnostic TIMES the scan instead of
;  predicting it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanStart(passive.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define i.i
  Define b.i
  Define n.i
  Define r.i
  Define scanType.i

  cyw43_bssCount = 0
  cyw43_bssOverflow = 0
  cyw43_bssDup = 0
  cyw43_chMismatch = 0
  cyw43_syncBad = 0

  ; "escan", then the NUL. PeekA over a ? label, not Read/Restore - see
  ; Cyw43SetEventMask for the open bug that rules that out.
  n = 0
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_ESCAN_NAMELEN - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, PeekA(?cyw43_EscanName + i))
    n = n + 1
  Next
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, 0)
  n = n + 1
  b = n

  For i = 0 To #CYW43_ESCAN_PARAMS_LEN - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + b + i, 0)
  Next

  ; cyw43_PutLE32, not PokeL - see Cyw43SetEventMask and the argument at
  ; cyw43_PutLE32. b is 6 here ("escan" plus a NUL), so this field is
  ; two bytes off a word boundary and a `str w` faults on Device memory.
  ; It is the SECOND of the two sites that had this defect, and it would
  ; have killed the very next payload after the first one was fixed.
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_arg[0] + b + #CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_VERSION, #CYW43_ESCAN_VERSION)
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + b + #CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_ACTION, #CYW43_ESCAN_ACTION_START)
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + b + #CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_SYNCID, #CYW43_ESCAN_SYNC_ID)

  ; bssid = broadcast. A zero BSSID would ask for one specific network
  ; whose address is 00:00:00:00:00:00, which is not what "scan" means.
  For i = 0 To 5
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + b + #CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_BSSID + i, $FF)
  Next

  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + b + #CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_BSSTYPE, #CYW43_BSSTYPE_ANY)
  scanType = #CYW43_SCANTYPE_ACTIVE
  If passive <> 0
    scanType = #CYW43_SCANTYPE_PASSIVE
  EndIf
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + b + #CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_SCANTYPE, scanType)

  ; nprobes, active_time, passive_time, home_time - four signed 32-bit
  ; -1s, sixteen bytes of $FF.
  For i = 0 To 15
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + b + #CYW43_ESCAN_OFF_NPROBES + i, $FF)
  Next

  ; channel_num 0 and channel_list 0 are already zero.

  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_SCAN_SEND)
  r = Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_VAR, @cyw43_arg[0], b + #CYW43_ESCAN_PARAMS_LEN, timeoutMs)
  cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_SCAN_DONE)
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ScanPoll - one slice of a running scan.
;
;  Returns:
;    #CYW43_SCAN_RUNNING  nothing NEW arrived this slice
;    #CYW43_SCAN_RESULT   one more network is in the table
;    #CYW43_SCAN_DONE     the firmware says the scan finished cleanly
;    #CYW43_SCAN_FAILED   it finished for some other reason, which is in
;                         Cyw43EventStatus()
;
;  FOUR STATES, STILL, NOW THAT DUPLICATES FOLD. A record that merged
;  into an existing row returns RUNNING, because the alternative
;  readings are both wrong: RESULT says "one more network is in the
;  table" and the table did not grow, and a fifth state would ripple
;  into every caller's Select to carry a fact Cyw43ScanDuplicates()
;  already carries with more detail. The word "nothing" above is now
;  "nothing NEW" and that is the whole change to this contract.
;
;  THE CALLER OWNS THE GUARD TIMER, deliberately. A scan ends on an
;  EVENT, never on a delay, and "no completion event in ten seconds" is
;  a DIFFERENT FAILURE from "completed, zero networks" (status 3). They
;  have different causes and a poll that conflated them would hide one.
;
;  STATUS 8 IS PARTIAL - another result follows. Status 0 is complete
;  and successful. ANYTHING ELSE IS ALSO COMPLETE and its code is
;  reported: the pico-sdk driver tests 8 then 0 and ignores every other
;  value, which leaves a FAIL, NO_NETWORKS or ABORT hanging until the
;  caller's timeout and turns a clean answer into a mystery.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanPoll(sliceMs.i)
  Define r.i

  r = cyw43_Poll(-1, 1, sliceMs)
  If r <> 2
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_SCAN_RUNNING
  EndIf

  If cyw43_evStatus = #CYW43_E_STATUS_PARTIAL
    ; TESTED AGAINST 1, NOT AGAINST 0, and the difference matters now
    ; that the parser has three answers. 0 is "nothing was recorded"
    ; and 2 is "folded into a row that already existed"; only 1 grew
    ; the table. Both of the others are counted inside the parser and
    ; neither is a reason to end the scan.
    If cyw43_ParseBss(cyw43_evP) = 1
      ProcedureReturn #CYW43_SCAN_RESULT
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_SCAN_RUNNING
  EndIf

  If cyw43_evStatus = #CYW43_E_STATUS_SUCCESS
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_SCAN_DONE
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_SCAN_FAILED
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - what the scan found, and what the transport saw.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i Cyw43ScanCount()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssCount
EndProcedure

; A NUL-terminated pointer to network n's SSID. The buffer is 33 bytes
; per entry and the terminator is written by the parser, so this may be
; handed straight to a string printer.
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanSsid(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_bssCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn @cyw43_bssSsid[0] + (n * 33)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ScanSsidLen(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_bssCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssSsidLen[n]
EndProcedure

; THE CONTROL CHANNEL - the one an AP is described as being on, and the
; one brcmf_inform_single_bss uses (cfg80211.c:3348).
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanChannel(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_bssCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssChan[n]
EndProcedure

; The chanspec's own channel byte, which on a 40 or 80 MHz AP is the
; CENTRE of the block and not a channel anything sits on. Here so that a
; diagnostic can show both and a "channel mismatch" can be read as the
; wide channel it usually is rather than as a layout fault.
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanChanspecChannel(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_bssCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssCspec[n]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ScanRssi(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_bssCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssRssi[n]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ScanPrivate(n.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_bssCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssPriv[n]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ScanBssidByte(n.i, i.i)
  If n < 0 Or n >= cyw43_bssCount
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If i < 0 Or i > 5
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssBssid[n * 6 + i] & $FF
EndProcedure

; DISTINCT NETWORKS THAT DID NOT FIT. Duplicates are folded before this
; is ever reached, so a table full of one AP's beacons is impossible and
; a non-zero count here really does mean #CYW43_MAX_BSS was not enough.
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanOverflow()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssOverflow
EndProcedure

; HOW MANY RECORDS WERE FOLDED INTO A ROW THAT ALREADY EXISTED, by
; BSSID. Non-zero is normal and is the point of the counter - it is the
; difference between "this room has two access points" and "this room
; has two access points and they are chatty". Read it next to
; Cyw43ScanCount(): count plus duplicates is the number of PARTIAL
; results the firmware actually delivered.
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanDuplicates()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssDup
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43EventType()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_evType
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43EventStatus()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_evStatus
EndProcedure

; SIGN-EXTENDED ON THE WAY OUT, not on the way in. The BCME_* codes are
; small negative 32-bit integers and the raw word is 64 bits wide here;
; a status printed as 4294967292 is a number nobody can look up, while
; -4 is BCME_NOTUP and is one search away. Cyw43Ioctl's own `<> 0` test
; is unaffected either way, which is why the conversion is here and not
; at the assignment - the transport's decision must not depend on a
; width question. See cyw43_Signed32.
Procedure.i Cyw43IoctlStatus()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_Signed32(cyw43_ioctlStatus)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ReplyLen()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_replyLen
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ReplyPtr()
  ProcedureReturn @cyw43_rx[0] + cyw43_replyOff
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43RxFrames()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rxFrames
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43RxBadHdr()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rxBadHdr
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43RxTooBig()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rxTooBig
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43RxShort()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rxShort
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43RxOther()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rxOther
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43EvOther()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_evOther
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43EvBadIsland()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_evBadIsland
EndProcedure

; PAYLOAD-LESS EVENT FRAMES - the header and nothing else, carrying a
; credit update. ONE PER SCAN IS THE OBSERVED NORMAL on this board and
; is not a fault; the long note is in cyw43_RxFrame. It is counted
; rather than ignored because the frame is evidence the firmware is
; managing the transmit window, and because it used to be counted as a
; malformed event, which made every healthy scan report an anomaly.
Procedure.i Cyw43EvEmpty()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_evEmpty
EndProcedure


Procedure.i Cyw43Stalls()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_stalls
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ChanMismatch()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_chMismatch
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43SyncBad()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_syncBad
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43BssLength()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_bssLength
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43F2BlockSize()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_f2Blk
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  What the CLM path did, for the instrument to print.
;
;  Cyw43ClmStatus() IS -1 UNTIL THE QUERY HAS ACTUALLY RUN, and that is
;  a third value and not a spare one. 0 means the firmware accepted the
;  blob; a positive value means it rejected it and says why; -1 means
;  nobody asked. A file that reported "0" for all three would let a CLM
;  that was never uploaded read as a CLM that loaded perfectly, which is
;  the same vacuous-control failure the probe_Count convention in
;  pi4WifiScan was invented for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ClmStatus()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_clmStatus
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ClmChunks()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_clmChunks
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  What "scan_ver" answered - all three parts of it, because the answer
;  is the STATUS and not the value. See Cyw43ScanVerProbe.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ScanVer()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_scanVer
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ScanVerRc()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_scanVerRc
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ScanVerStatus()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_scanVerStat
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  What "sup_wpa" answered - the same three fields, and for the same
;  reason. Cyw43SupWpaPresent() is beside Cyw43SupWpaProbe rather than
;  here, because it is a RULING over these three and not a field.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SupWpa()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_supWpa
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43SupWpaRc()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_supWpaRc
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43SupWpaStatus()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_supWpaStat
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The iovar names, as byte codes.
;
;  SPELLED IN DECIMAL because this project does not use character
;  literals, with the characters written above each row so the spelling
;  can be checked by eye. A mistyped iovar name is not an error the
;  firmware reports: it answers with a status this file would show as
;  "the firmware refused", and the cause would be a typo three hundred
;  lines away from the message.
;
;  EACH ROW'S LENGTH IS A #CYW43_*_NAMELEN CONSTANT up in the constants
;  block, and the two are checked against each other by eye and by
;  nothing else - a loop bound one short sends "clmloa" and gets the
;  same refusal a real failure would.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
DataSection
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    e    v    e    n    t    _    m    s    g    s
  cyw43_EventMsgsName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58, 101, 118, 101, 110, 116,  95, 109, 115, 103, 115
  ;        e    s    c    a    n
  cyw43_EscanName:
    Data.b 101, 115,  99,  97, 110
  ;        c    l    m    l    o    a    d
  cyw43_ClmLoadName:
    Data.b  99, 108, 109, 108, 111,  97, 100
  ;        c    l    m    l    o    a    d    _    s    t    a    t    u    s
  cyw43_ClmStatusName:
    Data.b  99, 108, 109, 108, 111,  97, 100,  95, 115, 116,  97, 116, 117, 115
  ;        c    l    m    v    e    r
  cyw43_ClmVerName:
    Data.b  99, 108, 109, 118, 101, 114
  ;        c    o    u    n    t    r    y
  cyw43_CountryName:
    Data.b  99, 111, 117, 110, 116, 114, 121
  ;        s    c    a    n    _    v    e    r
  cyw43_ScanVerName:
    Data.b 115,  99,  97, 110,  95, 118, 101, 114
  ;        s    u    p    _    w    p    a
  ;  NOT "bsscfg:sup_wpa". THE EXISTENCE PROBE AND THE SETTING ARE TWO
  ;  DIFFERENT NAMES and reading the probe off the setting is the way to
  ;  get a confident wrong answer. feature.c:341 asks for the BARE name
  ;  through brcmf_feat_iovar_int_get; the bsscfg-prefixed form is what
  ;  a driver WRITES afterwards to turn the supplicant on, per interface
  ;  (RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico:4627 idx 2). Ask with the prefix and a
  ;  firmware that does have the feature can still answer -23, because
  ;  it is being asked about a per-bsscfg variable in a context that has
  ;  no bsscfg attached to the question.
  cyw43_SupWpaName:
    Data.b 115, 117, 112,  95, 119, 112,  97
  ;        a    m    p    d    u    _    b    a    _    w    s    i    z    e
  cyw43_AmpduName:
    Data.b  97, 109, 112, 100, 117,  95,  98,  97,  95, 119, 115, 105, 122, 101
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    s    u    p    _    w    p    a
  cyw43_BsscfgSupWpaName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58, 115, 117, 112,  95, 119, 112,  97
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    s    u    p    _    w    p    a    2    _    e    a    p    v    e    r
  cyw43_BsscfgEapverName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58, 115, 117, 112,  95, 119, 112,  97,  50,  95, 101,  97, 112, 118, 101, 114
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    s    u    p    _    w    p    a    _    t    m    o
  cyw43_BsscfgTmoName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58, 115, 117, 112,  95, 119, 112,  97,  95, 116, 109, 111
  ;        r    o    a    m    _    o    f    f
  cyw43_RoamOffName:
    Data.b 114, 111,  97, 109,  95, 111, 102, 102
  ;        m    f    p
  cyw43_MfpName:
    Data.b 109, 102, 112
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    a    u    t    h
  cyw43_BsscfgAuthName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58,  97, 117, 116, 104
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    w    s    e    c
  cyw43_BsscfgWsecName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58, 119, 115, 101,  99
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    m    f    p
  cyw43_BsscfgMfpName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58, 109, 102, 112
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    w    p    a    _    a    u    t    h
  cyw43_BsscfgWpaAuthName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58, 119, 112,  97,  95,  97, 117, 116, 104
EndDataSection


; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT IS STILL REFUSED, AND IT IS A SHORTER LIST THAN IT WAS.
;
;  Until 2026-08-26 everything from Cyw43UploadFirmware upward returned
;  #CYW43_ERR_NO_FIRMWARE, for two stated reasons: the image was not on
;  this disk, and the licence question had not been settled. BOTH HAVE
;  BEEN ANSWERED and the answers are recorded where they belong -
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/firmware/ holds the three files and the
;  licence text they must travel with, and its README quotes the grant
;  line by line. The short version, because the old refusal here said
;  the opposite and somebody will read this file before that one:
;  "non-free" and "non-distributable" are different things. Debian's
;  non-free-firmware component means no SOURCE. The Cypress grant at
;  LICENCE-firmware-nonfree.txt:1974-1977 permits reproduction and
;  distribution in object code form for use with Cypress silicon, which
;  is what a Pi 4 is, and the NVRAM .txt is not Cypress's at all - it is
;  GPL-2, Linaro and Raspberry Pi (Trading) Ltd, at :602-606.
;
;  NOTHING IS REFUSED HERE ANY MORE, AND THIS BANNER IS KEPT RATHER
;  THAN DELETED because two sessions read it as the current state of the
;  file after it had stopped being true. What it used to say, and when
;  each part stopped being true:
;
;    "the SDPCM transport is not written"   -  UNTRUE since 2026-08-27
;       morning. Section 7 is the transport and the scan runs on it.
;    "scan, join and frames are not"        -  the scan landed the same
;       morning; JOIN AND FRAMES LANDED THE SAME EVENING, in sections 8
;       and 9 below.
;
;  WHAT IS STILL NOT HERE, stated positively so it cannot rot into a
;  refusal that is no longer accurate:
;
;    * THE `join` IOVAR. WLC_SET_SSID joins by name; pinning a scanned
;      BSSID and chanspec is a 70-byte iovar this file does not build.
;      The argument is at the head of section 8.
;    * DE-GLOMMING. Channel 3 frames are counted and dropped.
;    * A HOST SUPPLICANT. If `bsscfg:sup_wpa` is refused by this
;      firmware then WPA2 needs PBKDF2-SHA1 and EAPOL in the host, and
;      none of that is here. The bench log says whether it is refused.
;
;  The reference implementation remains on this disk twice over:
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico and RP2350/Lib/cyw43.pico2, roughly 8,000 lines
;  each, bench-proven through WPA2, DHCP and TLS 1.3.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43FirmwareRequired() - always 1, on this part, always.
;
;  Here so that a caller can ASK rather than discover. There is no
;  configuration, no fuse and no OTP option that makes a CYW43455 run
;  without a downloaded image - the ARM comes out of reset with empty
;  RAM and stays in reset until something writes to it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43FirmwareRequired()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The names of the three files, by the names brcmfmac uses. From
;  sdio.c lines 630-633 (the pattern) and line 652 (this part's
;  basename); the CLM blob's suffix is sdio.c line 4225's.
;
;  NAMED RATHER THAN DESCRIBED so that a caller reading them off a FAT32
;  volume does not have to carry its own copy of the strings.
;
;  THESE ARE NOT THE NAMES ON THIS BOARD'S STICK, and that is a trap
;  worth naming here rather than leaving to be discovered. fat.pi4
;  writes SHORT directory entries only - it does not generate VFAT
;  long-name chains - so none of these three can be a filename on the
;  boot volume. The mapping tools/anvil_putfile.py uses is:
;
;      brcmfmac43455-sdio.bin        ->  BRCMFW.BIN
;      brcmfmac43455-sdio.txt        ->  BRCMNV.TXT
;      brcmfmac43455-sdio.clm_blob   ->  BRCMCLM.BLB
;
;  What these procedures return is what brcmfmac calls the files, which
;  is what somebody comparing against Linux needs; the 8.3 names are
;  what Anvil's `load` needs. Both are true and they are different.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43FirmwareName()
  ProcedureReturn "brcmfmac43455-sdio.bin"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43NvramName()
  ProcedureReturn "brcmfmac43455-sdio.txt"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ClmName()
  ProcedureReturn "brcmfmac43455-sdio.clm_blob"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ScanStart / Cyw43Join / Cyw43Leave / Cyw43LinkUp / Cyw43Send /
;  Cyw43Receive / Cyw43MacAddress - ALL STILL REFUSED, all for the one
;  reason in the banner above: there is no SDPCM transport in this file.
;
;  These are not stubs in the bad sense. Each returns 0 and sets
;  #CYW43_ERR_NO_FIRMWARE, and Cyw43ErrorText() explains what is missing
;  in one sentence a person can act on. They exist so that a program
;  written against this file has names to call and gets a specific
;  answer, and so that when the transport lands the shape of the API
;  does not change underneath everything written in the meantime.
;
;  Cyw43MacAddress is worth its own line because it is the one people
;  assume must be free. It is not in a register: it is in the NVRAM text
;  this file now uploads - "macaddr=b8:27:eb:74:f2:6c" is line 10 of
;  this board's file - and the firmware reports it back through an
;  ioctl once running. Reading it out of the .txt before upload would
;  give the right six bytes and would be a lie about where they came
;  from, because the firmware is entitled to override them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE LIST IS EMPTY. Cyw43ScanStart and Cyw43ScanPoll left it on the
; morning of 2026-08-27 when the transport landed; Cyw43Join,
; Cyw43Leave, Cyw43LinkUp, Cyw43Send, Cyw43Receive and Cyw43MacAddress
; left it the same evening, in sections 8 and 9. The paragraph above is
; kept because it explains what each of them needed, and a reader who
; arrives here from an old comment elsewhere in the tree needs to be
; told plainly that they are real now.
;
; THEIR SIGNATURES CHANGED ON THE WAY OUT OF THIS LIST and that is worth
; one line, because a stub's signature is written to be plausible rather
; than to be right. Cyw43Send takes a length and no pointer - the frame
; is built in place at Cyw43DataPtr(). Cyw43Receive takes a timeout and
; returns a length. Cyw43MacAddress takes a timeout, because it is an
; ioctl and every ioctl in this file is bounded.

; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 8 - THE ASSOCIATION.
;
;  Everything below this line was written on 2026-08-27 against
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico lines 4538-5710, which has been associating
;  with WPA2 access points and carrying TLS 1.3 for months. WHERE THIS
;  FILE DIFFERS FROM THAT ONE, THE DIFFERENCE IS NAMED AND ARGUED ON THE
;  LINE. The two parts are a CYW43439 and a CYW43455 running different
;  firmware images, and today alone the CLM handling and the country
;  handling both turned out to differ, so "the twin does it this way" is
;  a starting point and never a conclusion.
;
;  ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHO RUNS THE SUPPLICANT, AND THE ANSWER IS NOT WHAT THE PROBE SAID
;  ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  brcmfmac's existence test - GET the bare iovar "sup_wpa" and treat
;  any status other than -23 as "the feature is there" (feature.c:341,
;  and the rule at feature.c:184-201) - was run on this board on
;  2026-08-27 and answered:
;
;      sup_wpa rc -3   status -23   value -1
;
;  -23 IS BRCMF_FW_UNSUPPORTED, so by brcmfmac's own rule this firmware
;  does NOT advertise BRCMF_FEAT_FWSUP. That is consistent with how a
;  Raspberry Pi actually runs: Linux on a Pi 4 does not offload the
;  handshake, it runs wpa_supplicant in userspace over nl80211.
;
;  THAT IS NOT THE SAME QUESTION AS "CAN THE FIRMWARE DO IT", AND THIS
;  FILE ASKS THE SECOND ONE TOO. The existence probe asks about the
;  BARE name; the thing a driver actually WRITES to turn the in-firmware
;  supplicant on is the per-interface `bsscfg:sup_wpa`. They are
;  different iovars and a firmware can know the second without
;  answering to the first. The direct test costs one ioctl and it is
;  item 2 of the security block below: if `bsscfg:sup_wpa = 1` is
;  ACCEPTED then the supplicant is there whatever the probe said, and if
;  it is REFUSED then the probe was right and the host has to do
;  PBKDF2-SHA1 and EAPOL itself.
;
;  SO THE BLOCK BELOW IS ALSO THE EXPERIMENT. Every item reports its own
;  verdict and cyw43_failReg names the one that refused, exactly so that
;  this question is answered by a bench log and not by an argument.
;
;  ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND IT IS A DELIBERATE OMISSION
;  ----------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE `join` IOVAR. Pinning a scanned BSSID and chanspec is done with
;  a 70-byte `join` iovar rather than the 36-byte WLC_SET_SSID this file
;  sends, and it would cut the association time by skipping the
;  full-channel walk. It is not here, for two reasons and neither is
;  laziness. First, brcmf_assoc_params_le (fwil_types.h:482-490) is a
;  kernel struct with NO packing attribute, so the offset of
;  chanspec_num after a six-byte array is a compiler alignment question
;  this file cannot answer by reading the header - and a join payload
;  that is two bytes out is refused with a code that names nothing.
;  Second, the twin declined it for the same milestone and said why
;  (cyw43.pico:5029-5034): it is an optimisation, not the thing itself.
;
;  SELECTION BY BSSID IS THEREFORE THE CALLER'S JOB AND THE PROOF IS
;  THE READ-BACK. Cyw43LinkBssid() returns the six bytes the firmware
;  says it associated with; comparing them against the BSSID the scan
;  reported for the chosen row is what turns "we joined something called
;  example" into "we joined THAT access point".
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetIovarU32 - NAME + NUL, then one u32 little-endian.
;
;  cyw43_PutLE32 AND NOT PokeL, and the reason is the one that cost a
;  bench session on 2026-08-27: an iovar value sits at (name length + 1)
;  and no interesting name has a length that makes that a multiple of
;  four. "mfp\0" is 4 and happens to be safe; "ampdu_ba_wsize\0" is 15
;  and is not. Using the safe store for both is one line and removes the
;  whole class.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SetIovarU32(src.i, namelen.i, v.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  n = cyw43_PutName(@cyw43_arg[0], src, namelen)
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, v)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_VAR, @cyw43_arg[0], n + 4, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetIovarU32x2 - the `bsscfg:` payload shape.
;
;      +0  name + NUL      +n  u32 LE interface index      +n+4  u32 LE
;
;  Cyw43SetIovarU32 CANNOT SEND THESE. It writes one u32 after the name,
;  so the index would be read as the value and the value would fall off
;  the end of the payload - and the firmware would accept the frame,
;  because it is a perfectly well-formed iovar set of the wrong thing.
;  Exactly the shape Cyw43SetEventMask already needs for
;  "bsscfg:event_msgs", which is why that procedure builds its payload
;  by hand rather than calling the plain setter.
;
;  THE INDEX IS 0 FOR THE STATION INTERFACE at every call site here.
;  It is still an argument rather than a constant, because the number
;  means something - it is which bsscfg the setting applies to - and a
;  procedure that hard-coded it would be a procedure whose name lies.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(src.i, namelen.i, idx.i, v.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  n = cyw43_PutName(@cyw43_arg[0], src, namelen)
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, idx)
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_arg[0] + n + 4, v)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_VAR, @cyw43_arg[0], n + 8, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  STAY JOINED - the two firmware policies a board that must never drop
;  a link has to state out loud.
;
;  Neither of these was ever sent. The radio ran on whatever the firmware
;  defaults to, and both defaults are wrong for this board:
;
;  1. ROAMING. The 43455 blob carries a roam engine (`roamprof` is in its
;     build string) and the reference driver leaves it ENABLED unless
;     told otherwise - its own comment on the iovar is that turning it
;     off is what lets the supplicant take care of roaming. On this part
;     the supplicant IS the host: the pairwise key was derived with the
;     BSSID of the access point the host handshook with, and
;     brcmf_bss_roaming_done does no keying at all - it copies the new
;     BSSID into the profile and hands the roam upward. So a firmware
;     roam on this board is an association whose keys are aimed at the
;     wrong radio, and it presents as a link that is up and decrypts
;     nothing. The host owns the association, so the firmware is asked
;     not to move it.
;
;  2. POWER MANAGEMENT. The reference driver's boot default is PM_FAST,
;     which lets the radio doze between beacons. That is right for a
;     laptop and wrong for a board whose whole console is on this link:
;     "a board that is joined but cannot be reached is not joined". This
;     board is mains-powered and always awake, so PM is set OFF and the
;     receiver stays on.
;
;  NEITHER IS FATAL IF REFUSED. Both return their own status so the
;  bring-up can print what the firmware said rather than assume; a part
;  that does not implement one of them is a fact to report, not a reason
;  to fail a join.
; ======================================================================

#CYW43_C_SET_PM      = 86     ; l:86  WLC_SET_PM. brcmf_cfg80211 line 3296
#CYW43_PM_OFF        = 0      ; the receiver stays on
#CYW43_PM_MAX        = 1
#CYW43_PM_FAST       = 2      ; the reference driver's own boot default
#CYW43_ROAMOFF_NAMELEN = 8    ; "roam_off", the NUL is added by the sender

; Ask the firmware not to roam. 1 = the host owns the association.
Procedure.i Cyw43SetRoamOff(off.i, timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32(?cyw43_RoamOffName, #CYW43_ROAMOFF_NAMELEN, off, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; Power management, as an ioctl and not an iovar - the reference driver
; uses BRCMF_C_SET_PM (86) and there is no "PM" iovar in it.
Procedure.i Cyw43SetPowerMode(mode.i, timeoutMs.i)
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_arg[0], mode)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_PM, @cyw43_arg[0], 4, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE SECURITY BLOCK, ONE KNOB PER CALL.
;
;  ======================================================================
;  ALL OF IT GOES OUT AFTER WLC_UP. THERE IS NO PRE-UP SECURITY SETUP.
;  ======================================================================
;  Both reference drivers put the entire block inside their join
;  function and both join long after the interface is up. The Pico bench
;  measured what happens otherwise: the PRE-UP bundle was refused with
;  -5 BCME_NOTDOWN on 2026-08-06.
;
;  ======================================================================
;  THE SPELLINGS CHANGED ON 2026-08-27 AND THE BENCH IS WHY. READ THIS
;  BEFORE PUTTING THE IOCTLS BACK.
;  ======================================================================
;  The first version of this block was the twin's, item for item: ioctl
;  134 WLC_SET_WSEC, ioctl 22 WLC_SET_AUTH, ioctl 165 WLC_SET_WPA_AUTH
;  and the plain `mfp` iovar. On this part EVERY ONE OF THEM RETURNED
;  STATUS 0 - accepted, no complaint - and the association was then
;  REFUSED:
;
;      event 3  AUTH      status 0     <- 802.11 authentication PASSED
;      event 0  SET_SSID  status 1     <- WLC_E_STATUS_FAIL, 7 ms later
;
;  Open-system authentication always succeeds; it carries no security
;  information at all. The ASSOCIATION REQUEST is where the RSN
;  information element goes, and the firmware builds that from its
;  PER-INTERFACE security state. An association request with no RSN IE
;  offered to a WPA2 access point is refused exactly like this.
;
;  SO THE IOCTLS SET SOMETHING THE ASSOCIATION REQUEST IS NOT BUILT
;  FROM. brcmfmac - which is the driver that works with THIS firmware
;  image every day on every Raspberry Pi - does not use them. It sets
;  `bsscfg:auth` (cfg80211.c:1995), `bsscfg:wsec` (cfg80211.c:2069),
;  `bsscfg:mfp` (cfg80211.c:2217) and `bsscfg:wpa_auth`
;  (cfg80211.c:2221), all through brcmf_fil_bsscfg_int_set, which is the
;  name + NUL + u32 index + u32 value shape Cyw43SetIovarU32x2 sends.
;
;  THE TWIN IS NOT WRONG, IT IS A DIFFERENT CHIP. The CYW43439's
;  firmware honours the ioctls and has been associating on them for
;  months. This is the third thing today where the two parts differed -
;  the CLM and the country handling were the first two - and the rule
;  that keeps being confirmed is: where brcmfmac and pico-sdk disagree
;  about a CYW43455, brcmfmac wins.
;
;  THE ORDER IS brcmfmac's AND wpa_auth IS LAST. brcmf_cfg80211_connect
;  calls set_auth_type, then set_wsec_mode, then set_key_mgmt - which
;  sets mfp and then wpa_auth as its final act (cfg80211.c:2441-2453).
;  Nothing here reorders it on the grounds that it should not matter.
;
;    idx 0  iovar  ampdu_ba_wsize          = 8
;    idx 1  ioctl   20 WLC_SET_INFRA       = 1     infrastructure BSS
;    idx 2  iovar  bsscfg:auth             = 0     802.11 OPEN SYSTEM
;    idx 3  iovar  bsscfg:wsec             = $04   AES
;    idx 4  iovar  bsscfg:sup_wpa          = 1     <- THE EXPERIMENT
;    idx 5  iovar  bsscfg:sup_wpa2_eapver  = -1
;    idx 6  iovar  bsscfg:sup_wpa_tmo      = 5000
;    idx 7  iovar  bsscfg:mfp              = 1     TOLERATED IF REFUSED
;    ------  100 ms, THE PMK (ioctl 268), 3 ms  ------
;    idx 8  iovar  bsscfg:wpa_auth         = $0080 WPA2_PSK. LAST.
;
;  ONE KNOB PER CALL RATHER THAN ONE PROCEDURE THAT SENDS NINE, because
;  the instrument wants a printed verdict per item. A single call that
;  returned one number for nine ioctls would answer "the security block
;  was refused" and send somebody to read this file to find out which
;  line - which is the whole failure mode the per-item shape prevents.
;  Cyw43SecurityWpa2 below is the convenience wrapper for a caller that
;  does not narrate.
;
;  AN idx OUTSIDE THE RANGE RETURNS OK AND SENDS NOTHING, so a caller's
;  loop bound cannot turn into a bus transaction.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_SEC_PRE   = 8    ; items 0..7 go BEFORE the PMK
#CYW43_SEC_ITEMS = 9    ; item 8 goes after it

; The indices a caller has to know about by number, named so that a
; payload's `If i = 4` stops being a number nobody can check.
#CYW43_SEC_SUPWPA   = 4   ; bsscfg:sup_wpa - THE EXPERIMENT
#CYW43_SEC_EAPVER   = 5
#CYW43_SEC_SUPTMO   = 6
#CYW43_SEC_MFP      = 7   ; TOLERATED IF REFUSED

Procedure.i Cyw43SecItem(idx.i, timeoutMs.i)
  If idx = 0
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32(?cyw43_AmpduName, #CYW43_AMPDU_NAMELEN, 8, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  If idx = 1
    ; STILL AN IOCTL, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT STAYS ONE. `infra` is not
    ; a security setting and brcmfmac reaches it through
    ; BRCMF_C_SET_INFRA as well (brcmf_cfg80211_change_iface); there is
    ; no bsscfg spelling to prefer.
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSetU32(#CYW43_C_SET_INFRA, 1, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  If idx = 2
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(?cyw43_BsscfgAuthName, #CYW43_BSSAUTH_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, #CYW43_AUTH_OPEN, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  If idx = 3
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(?cyw43_BsscfgWsecName, #CYW43_BSSWSEC_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, #CYW43_WSEC_AES, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  If idx = #CYW43_SEC_SUPWPA
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(?cyw43_BsscfgSupWpaName, #CYW43_BSSSUPWPA_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, 1, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  If idx = #CYW43_SEC_EAPVER
    ; -1, NOT the literal $FFFFFFFF. The same four bytes reach the wire
    ; and -1 is what the reference driver writes; a 32-bit hex literal
    ; here is where an argument about signed constant ranges with this
    ; compiler would start, for no gain at all.
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(?cyw43_BsscfgEapverName, #CYW43_EAPVER_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, -1, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  If idx = #CYW43_SEC_SUPTMO
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(?cyw43_BsscfgTmoName, #CYW43_SUPTMO_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, #CYW43_SUP_WPA_TMO, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  If idx = #CYW43_SEC_MFP
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(?cyw43_BsscfgMfpName, #CYW43_BSSMFP_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, #CYW43_MFP_CAPABLE, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  If idx = 8
    ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(?cyw43_BsscfgWpaAuthName, #CYW43_BSSWPAAUTH_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, #CYW43_WPA_AUTH_WPA2_PSK, timeoutMs)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

; The English name of one security item, so an instrument can print a
; row a person can act on instead of an index. HERE rather than in the
; instrument because the index-to-name mapping is this file's and a
; second copy in a payload would drift the first time an item moves.
Procedure.i Cyw43SecItemName(idx.i)
  Select idx
    Case 0
      ProcedureReturn "ampdu_ba_wsize = 8"
    Case 1
      ProcedureReturn "WLC_SET_INFRA = 1 (ioctl 20)"
    Case 2
      ProcedureReturn "bsscfg:auth = 0 (802.11 OPEN SYSTEM)"
    Case 3
      ProcedureReturn "bsscfg:wsec = AES"
    Case 4
      ProcedureReturn "bsscfg:sup_wpa = 1  <- THE FIRMWARE SUPPLICANT"
    Case 5
      ProcedureReturn "bsscfg:sup_wpa2_eapver = -1"
    Case 6
      ProcedureReturn "bsscfg:sup_wpa_tmo = 5000"
    Case 7
      ProcedureReturn "bsscfg:mfp = 1 (CAPABLE) - tolerated if refused"
    Case 8
      ProcedureReturn "bsscfg:wpa_auth = WPA2_PSK (LAST, as brcmfmac does)"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "no such security item"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetPmk - ioctl 268, 68 BYTES, ALWAYS 68.
;
;    off  sz  field    order  value
;     0    2  key_len   LE    the number of ASCII bytes. NOT 64
;     2    2  flags     LE    1 = BRCMF_WSEC_PASSPHRASE
;     4   64  key        -    the RAW ASCII passphrase, zero-padded
;
;  Triple-sourced: brcmfmac's struct brcmf_wsec_pmk_le, the Pico
;  driver's 68 (cyw43.pico:553), and E's PassphraseInfo.
;
;  THE PASSPHRASE GOES IN RAW - not hashed, not hex, not terminated
;  beyond the padding. flags = 1 is what tells the firmware these bytes
;  are a passphrase and that IT must run PBKDF2 over them.
;
;  64 IS REFUSED AND THAT IS NOT AN OFF-BY-ONE. A 64-character key is a
;  hex-encoded PMK, which wants flags = 0 and key_len = 32 and a
;  completely different meaning for the same 64 bytes. Neither
;  reference driver ever sets flags = 0, so that path is unverified and
;  is not implemented here - and letting a 64-character key take the
;  passphrase path silently would be a wrong answer with no error
;  attached to it.
;
;  ======================================================================
;  THE WIPE IS PART OF THE PROCEDURE, NOT AN OPTIONAL TIDY-UP.
;  ======================================================================
;  The 64 key bytes sit in cyw43_arg AND are copied into cyw43_tx by the
;  transport. BOTH SURVIVE UNTIL SOMETHING ELSE OVERWRITES THEM, and on
;  this board both are within reach of Anvil's own `d` memory dump -
;  which is a command a person types when something has gone wrong,
;  which is exactly when a join has just failed. They are wiped here,
;  immediately, ON SUCCESS AND FAILURE ALIKE, which is why the wipe is
;  not behind the rc test.
;
;  THE cyw43_tx OFFSET IS COMPUTED, NOT TYPED, so it follows the headers
;  if they ever move.
;
;  NOTHING IN HERE PRINTS. A library that prints has chosen a transport
;  for every program that includes it, and in this one case it would
;  also have chosen to print the thing that must never be printed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SetPmk(*pass, len.i, structLen.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define keyoff.i

  If len < #CYW43_PASS_MIN Or len > #CYW43_PASS_MAX
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  ; THE TWO SIZES AND NOTHING ELSE. An arbitrary length here would let a
  ; caller's typo become a firmware refusal nobody could read, and the
  ; whole point of making the size a parameter was to let a bench choose
  ; between two DOCUMENTED shapes rather than to open the field up.
  If structLen <> #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_68 And structLen <> #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_132
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  If structLen > #CYW43_ARGBUF
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf

  For i = 0 To structLen - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + i, 0)
  Next
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 0, len & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 1, (len >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 2, #CYW43_PMK_PASSPHRASE)
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 3, 0)
  For i = 0 To len - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + #CYW43_PMK_KEYOFF + i, PeekA(*pass + i))
  Next

  r = Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_WSEC_PMK, @cyw43_arg[0], structLen, timeoutMs)

  ; --- the wipe. Unconditional, both buffers. -------------------------
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_PMK_KEYMAX - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + #CYW43_PMK_KEYOFF + i, 0)
  Next
  keyoff = #CYW43_CTRL_HDRLEN + #CYW43_PMK_KEYOFF
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_PMK_KEYMAX - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + keyoff + i, 0)
  Next

  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SecurityWpa2 - all nine items and the PMK, in order, with the
;  settling delays. For a caller that does not narrate; an instrument
;  drives Cyw43SecItem itself so every item gets its own printed row.
;
;  THE DELAYS ARE THE SUPERSET OF BOTH DRIVERS' AND THAT IS THE RULING:
;  100 ms before the PMK and 3 ms after. G's 2 ms falls inside the 100.
;  These are SETTLING TIMES rather than values, so the longer of two
;  margins is safe in a way the longer of two values would not be, and
;  it costs 103 ms once per join. G's 2 ms is the proof that the
;  firmware needs SOME gap before the PMK ioctl at all.
;
;  STOPS AT THE FIRST REFUSAL WITH ONE EXCEPTION: `mfp`. Its verdict is
;  latched in cyw43_mfpRc and the block continues, because MFP_CAPABLE
;  is a capability advertisement and not a requirement - a firmware
;  without 802.11w refuses the iovar and associates perfectly well
;  without it, and stopping a join over it would turn a missing optional
;  feature into a milestone failure. EVERY OTHER ITEM IS A GATE and
;  cyw43_failReg carries the failing index.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SecurityWpa2(*pass, len.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define i.i
  Define r.i

  cyw43_joinSecured = #CYW43_JOINEXP_SECURED
  cyw43_failReg = -1
  cyw43_mfpRc = 0

  For i = 0 To #CYW43_SEC_PRE - 1
    r = Cyw43SecItem(i, timeoutMs)
    If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
      cyw43_failReg = i
      ProcedureReturn r
    EndIf
  Next

  cyw43_DelayUs(100000)
  r = Cyw43SetPmk(*pass, len, #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    cyw43_failReg = #CYW43_SEC_PRE
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  cyw43_DelayUs(3000)

  For i = #CYW43_SEC_PRE To #CYW43_SEC_ITEMS - 1
    r = Cyw43SecItem(i, timeoutMs)
    If i = 7
      cyw43_mfpRc = r            ; TOLERATED. See the header.
      r = #CYW43_IO_OK
    EndIf
    If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
      cyw43_failReg = i
      ProcedureReturn r
    EndIf
  Next

  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SecurityOpen - join a network with NO encryption at all.
;
;  The Pico driver's Cyw43SecurityOpen, on this target, item for item and
;  in the same order. It exists here because the cab link between the
;  display and the engine-port unit is an OPEN network by ruling: the
;  link carries engine speed, an odometer and engine hours, every frame
;  on it is authenticated by the payload, and the confidentiality being
;  given up is a truck's RPM. The unit hosts the network and THIS BOARD
;  JOINS IT - forced by the two firmware images, because this radio's
;  blob carries no authenticator and cannot host one.
;
;  FIVE ITEMS, AND FOUR OF THEM ARE THE WPA2 BLOCK WITH THE OPPOSITE
;  VALUE. The fifth, bsscfg:sup_wpa = 0, is the one that matters most: it
;  turns the firmware supplicant OFF. Leaving it on for an open network
;  makes the radio wait for a four-way handshake that is never coming,
;  and the symptom is an association that completes and then times out in
;  the keying phase - which reads exactly like a wrong passphrase on a
;  network that has no passphrase.
;
;  NO PMK, AND THEREFORE NO PBKDF2, which is 10.25 seconds off a cold
;  start on this board - measured here, and most of the reason the ruling
;  went the way it did.
;
;  cyw43_joinSecured is set to #CYW43_JOINEXP_OPEN, which is what tells
;  Cyw43JoinPoll not to wait for a supplicant event before calling the
;  join done. Getting that wrong in either direction gives a join that
;  either never completes or completes before it is keyed, so it is set
;  HERE, beside the decision, and not left to the caller.
;
;  cyw43_failReg carries the index of the item that refused, and
;  Cyw43SecurityOpenItemText names it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SecurityOpen(timeoutMs.i)
  Define r.i

  cyw43_joinSecured = #CYW43_JOINEXP_OPEN
  cyw43_failReg = -1

  r = Cyw43IoctlSetU32(#CYW43_C_SET_WSEC, #CYW43_WSEC_OPEN, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    cyw43_failReg = 0
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  r = Cyw43SetIovarU32x2(?cyw43_BsscfgSupWpaName, #CYW43_BSSSUPWPA_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, 0, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    cyw43_failReg = 1
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  r = Cyw43IoctlSetU32(#CYW43_C_SET_INFRA, 1, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    cyw43_failReg = 2
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  ; 0 IS NOT "no authentication". It is 802.11 OPEN SYSTEM
  ; authentication, which is what an open network and WPA2 both use; 1 is
  ; shared-key, which is WEP, and 3 is WPA3.
  r = Cyw43IoctlSetU32(#CYW43_C_SET_AUTH, #CYW43_AUTH_OPEN, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    cyw43_failReg = 3
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  r = Cyw43IoctlSetU32(#CYW43_C_SET_WPA_AUTH, #CYW43_WPA_AUTH_DISABLED, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    cyw43_failReg = 4
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43SecurityOpenItemText(i.i)
  If i = 0
    ProcedureReturn "WLC_SET_WSEC = 0 (no encryption, ioctl 134)"
  EndIf
  If i = 1
    ProcedureReturn "bsscfg:sup_wpa = 0  <- THE FIRMWARE SUPPLICANT, OFF"
  EndIf
  If i = 2
    ProcedureReturn "WLC_SET_INFRA = 1 (ioctl 20)"
  EndIf
  If i = 3
    ProcedureReturn "WLC_SET_AUTH = 0 (802.11 OPEN SYSTEM, ioctl 22)"
  EndIf
  If i = 4
    ProcedureReturn "WLC_SET_WPA_AUTH = 0 (no WPA, ioctl 165)"
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn "no such open-security item"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43Disassoc - ioctl 52, ZERO LENGTH.
;
;  DO NOT SEND IT BEFORE A FIRST JOIN - neither reference driver does.
;  DO send it before a retry, and note that doing so is OUR decision.
;  The Pico bench measured what it buys: attempt-1 success went from
;  4.3% to 75.3% across 144 reset-to-reset cycles with a clean teardown,
;  Fisher exact p = 3e-17, and the keying-stall failure MODE disappeared
;  rather than merely becoming rarer (cyw43.pico:5527-5544). The
;  mechanism is that our own disassociation is what clears the AP's
;  belief that a station which vanished mid-association is still there.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43Disassoc(timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_DISASSOC, 0, 0, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43JoinStart - ioctl 26, 36 BYTES. THE JOIN.
;
;    off  sz  field      order  value
;     0    4  SSID_len    LE    1..32, THE REAL LENGTH, not padded
;     4   32  SSID         -    raw bytes, NOT NUL-terminated
;
;  THE IOCTL REPLY MEANS "REQUEST ACCEPTED" AND NOTHING MORE. Both
;  reference drivers wait on events and neither ever waits on this
;  reply. A status 0 here with no events following is a DISTINCT
;  FAILURE - it is #CYW43_JOINFAIL_TIMEOUT with the phase still ASSOC -
;  and a caller that reported it as "join failed" would be hiding the
;  one fact that separates a radio problem from a transport one.
;
;  RESETS THE STATE MACHINE, INCLUDING THE RING. See cyw43_EvrClear.
;
;  THE CLOCK STARTS AT THE REPLY, which is where the phase budgets are
;  measured from, and events that landed inside the reply window are
;  already in the ring waiting - which is the entire reason the ring
;  exists.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43JoinStart(*ssid, len.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define i.i
  Define r.i

  If len < #CYW43_SSID_MIN Or len > #CYW43_SSID_MAX
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf

  For i = 0 To #CYW43_SSID_STRUCT - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + i, 0)
  Next
  ; cyw43_PutLE32 at offset 0 is word-aligned and PokeL would be legal
  ; here - it is used anyway, because "this one is safe" is exactly the
  ; reasoning that left two unaligned stores in this file until they
  ; killed the board, and a reader should not have to check.
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_arg[0], len)
  For i = 0 To len - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 4 + i, PeekA(*ssid + i))
  Next

  cyw43_EvrClear()
  cyw43_joinState = #CYW43_JS_ACTIVE
  If cyw43_joinSecured <> #CYW43_JOINEXP_SECURED
    ; NEITHER an open network NOR a supplicant-less one will ever
    ; produce a PSK_SUP event, so waiting for one would time out every
    ; single time. G presets the bit at join time for the open case and
    ; the accumulator then needs no special case; the NOKEY case takes
    ; the same mechanical route for a completely different reason, which
    ; is why the expectation is three-valued and stays readable through
    ; Cyw43JoinExpectation(). See #CYW43_JOINEXP_NOKEY.
    cyw43_joinState = cyw43_joinState | #CYW43_JS_KEYED
  EndIf
  cyw43_joinSsidOk = 0
  cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_NONE
  cyw43_joinPhase = #CYW43_PHASE_ASSOC
  cyw43_joinDone = 0
  cyw43_linkLost = 0
  cyw43_joinTlink = 0
  cyw43_jevNew = 0

  r = Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_SSID, @cyw43_arg[0], #CYW43_SSID_STRUCT, timeoutMs)
  cyw43_joinT0 = cyw43_Ticks()
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  cyw43_JoinClassify - one drained event, folded into the accumulator.
;
;  ORDER-INDEPENDENT ON PURPOSE. See #CYW43_JS_ALL for the argument.
;
;    3 AUTH      status 0                    |= AUTH  $0200
;    0 SET_SSID  status 0                    latched separately
;   16 LINK      status 0, FLAGS BIT 0 SET   |= LINK  $0400
;   46 PSK_SUP   status 6 = WLC_SUP_KEYED    |= KEYED $0800
;      state = $0E01                         JOINED
;
;  SET_SSID'S SUCCESS SETS NO BIT, and that is G's actual test rather
;  than the tidier one. It is latched in cyw43_joinSsidOk for the
;  instrument instead. The reason is which way the unknown cuts: if the
;  on-air order is not what is expected - and nothing on this part has
;  ever been measured - then REQUIRING SET_SSID would turn a genuinely
;  successful, fully keyed association into a mute guard timeout and we
;  would learn nothing. Latching it means a join that reaches $0E01
;  without a SET_SSID success reports itself as exactly that, which is a
;  bench fact worth having and cannot be mistaken for a clean pass.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure cyw43_JoinClassify()
  Define t.i
  Define s.i
  Define rr.i
  Define f.i

  t  = cyw43_jevType
  s  = cyw43_jevStatus
  rr = cyw43_jevReason
  f  = cyw43_jevFlags

  If t = #CYW43_EV_SET_SSID
    ; Event 0's status IS in the escan status space - 3 really is
    ; NO_NETWORKS here. That is the coincidence event 46 does NOT share,
    ; which is why the two are decoded in different arms against
    ; different constant families.
    If s = 0
      cyw43_joinSsidOk = 1
    ElseIf s = #CYW43_E_STATUS_NO_NETWORKS
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_NONET
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_NONET
    Else
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_ASSOC
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_FAIL
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_ASSOC
    ; DIAGNOSTIC, AND IT SETS NO BIT ON SUCCESS. The accumulator is G's
    ; and G does not have an ASSOC arm; adding a fourth required bit
    ; would mean a join that completed without this event - which
    ; nothing has ever established is impossible - would hang on a
    ; requirement invented here. A FAILURE is recorded, because a
    ; non-zero status on event 7 is the association refusing itself and
    ; there is no reading of that which is not a failure.
    If s <> 0
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_ASSOC
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_FAIL
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_JOIN
    ; DIAGNOSTIC ONLY, NO ARM AT ALL. Event 1 is the firmware narrating
    ; its own progress and neither reference driver acts on it. It is
    ; printed by the instrument like every other event and that is the
    ; whole of its job.
    cyw43_joinState = cyw43_joinState

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_AUTH
    If s = 0
      cyw43_joinState = cyw43_joinState | #CYW43_JS_AUTH
    ElseIf s = 6
      ; UNSOLICITED, in the escan status space this time. G ignores it
      ; explicitly and so does this. It is still counted and printed,
      ; because "ignored" and "never arrived" are different facts.
      cyw43_joinState = cyw43_joinState
    Else
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_BADAUTH
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_BADAUTH
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_LINK
    ; WITHOUT cyw43_evFlags THIS LINE READS 0 & 1 = 0 ON EVERY LINK
    ; EVENT AND THE JOIN NEVER COMPLETES. See #CYW43_EV_LINK_UP_FLAG.
    If (f & #CYW43_EV_LINK_UP_FLAG) <> 0
      cyw43_joinState = cyw43_joinState | #CYW43_JS_LINK
      If cyw43_joinTlink = 0
        cyw43_joinTlink = cyw43_Ticks()
      EndIf
    Else
      If cyw43_joinDone <> 0
        cyw43_linkLost = 1
      Else
        cyw43_joinState = cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_LINK)
      EndIf
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_PSK_SUP
    ; ==================================================================
    ; THE ONE SENTENCE THAT MATTERS MOST IN THIS WHOLE SECTION:
    ;
    ;   PSK_SUP status 6 is WLC_SUP_KEYED, A SUPPLICANT STATE. It is NOT
    ;   WLC_E_STATUS_UNSOLICITED. THEY MERELY SHARE THE NUMBER 6.
    ;
    ; Every constant named in this arm is #CYW43_SUP_* and none is an
    ; event-status code. Decoding this field against the escan table is
    ; a silent wrong answer aimed at the whole milestone.
    ; ==================================================================
    If s = #CYW43_SUP_KEYED
      cyw43_joinState = cyw43_joinState | #CYW43_JS_KEYED
    ElseIf rr = #CYW43_RSN_SUP_WPA_PSK_TMO
      If s = #CYW43_SUP_AUTHENTICATED Or s = #CYW43_SUP_LAST_BASIC_STATE Or s = #CYW43_SUP_KEYXCHANGE_WAIT_G1
        ; NOT "WRONG PASSPHRASE". See #CYW43_JOINFAIL_KEYTIMEOUT - a
        ; correct passphrase produced this exact event on 21 of 22 first
        ; attempts on the Pico bench and then keyed in 13 ms on retry.
        cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_KEYTIMEOUT
        cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_BADAUTH
      Else
        cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_BADAUTH
        cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_BADAUTH
      EndIf
    Else
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_BADAUTH
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_BADAUTH
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_DEAUTH_IND
    If cyw43_joinDone <> 0
      cyw43_linkLost = 1
    ElseIf s = 0 And rr = #CYW43_RSN_DEAUTH_WRONGKEY
      ; WRONG PASSPHRASE, THE OTHER SIGNATURE - the AP rejecting us
      ; outright rather than our supplicant timing out. SOME APS PRODUCE
      ; ONE, SOME THE OTHER, SOME BOTH, and they are not
      ; interchangeable: whichever arrived is reported with its own
      ; numbers rather than collapsed into one string.
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_WRONGPASS
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_BADAUTH
    Else
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_LINKLOST
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_FAIL
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_DEAUTH
    If cyw43_joinDone <> 0
      cyw43_linkLost = 1
    Else
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_LINKLOST
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_FAIL
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_DISASSOC
    If cyw43_joinDone <> 0
      cyw43_linkLost = 1
    Else
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_LINKLOST
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_FAIL
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_DISASSOC_IND
    ; Diagnostic only before a join - G's arm for this one is COMMENTED
    ; OUT in the reference, so acting on it earlier would be inventing
    ; behaviour. After a join it is a teardown like any other.
    If cyw43_joinDone <> 0
      cyw43_linkLost = 1
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_PRUNE
    If rr = #CYW43_RSN_PRUNE_RSN_MISMATCH
      ; OUR wsec / wpa_auth does not satisfy the AP's RSN IE - the
      ; network is WPA3-only, Enterprise, or mixed in a way $0080 does
      ; not cover. G re-sends WLC_SET_WPA_AUTH and rejoins; THIS FILE
      ; HAS ONE VALUE TO OFFER, so retrying the same configuration would
      ; loop for ever. A different bug from a wrong password and it must
      ; not be retried as one.
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_PRUNE
      cyw43_joinState = (cyw43_joinState & (~#CYW43_JS_KIND_MASK)) | #CYW43_JS_FAIL
    EndIf

  ElseIf t = #CYW43_EV_ICV_ERROR
    ; A MIC failure, unsolicited and often from a NEIGHBOUR's traffic.
    ; COUNT IT, ACT ON NOTHING - two within sixty seconds is the 802.11i
    ; countermeasure condition, which neither reference driver
    ; implements, so the threshold is unverified and the count is what
    ; gets printed.
    cyw43_icvCount = cyw43_icvCount + 1
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43JoinPoll - one slice of the join, or one slice of the hold.
;  THE EVENTS ARE THE LINK MONITOR; the ioctls only confirm.
;
;  Sets cyw43_jevNew and fills the cyw43_jev* fields when an event was
;  drained THIS CALL, so a caller can print it BEFORE acting on the
;  verdict. That split is deliberate: a join that fails on its fourth
;  event must still have printed the first three, and one that succeeds
;  on its fourth must print that one too.
;
;  ONE EVENT PER CALL. The alternative - drain the ring dry each slice -
;  would hand the caller a verdict computed from events it never saw,
;  which is the same loss the ring was added to prevent, one layer up.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43JoinPoll(sliceMs.i)
  Define now.i

  cyw43_jevNew = 0

  ; Only go to the wire if nothing is already waiting. Events latched
  ; during WLC_SET_SSID's own reply window are the NORMAL case, not the
  ; exception, and draining them costs no bus traffic at all.
  If cyw43_evrCount <= 0
    cyw43_PollEvent(sliceMs)
  EndIf

  If cyw43_EvrPop() <> 0
    cyw43_jevNew = 1
    cyw43_JoinClassify()
  EndIf

  ; --- HOLD MODE. Once joined the guards are off and the only question
  ; --- is whether the link is still there.
  If cyw43_joinDone <> 0
    cyw43_joinPhase = #CYW43_PHASE_HELD
    If cyw43_linkLost <> 0
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_LINKLOST
      ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_FAILED
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_JOINED
  EndIf

  If cyw43_joinFail <> #CYW43_JOINFAIL_NONE
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_FAILED
  EndIf

  ; G's test is a LITERAL EQUALITY, not a mask test.
  If cyw43_joinState = #CYW43_JS_ALL
    cyw43_joinDone = 1
    cyw43_joinPhase = #CYW43_PHASE_HELD
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_JOINED
  EndIf

  ; --- the guards ------------------------------------------------------
  If cyw43_joinT0 = 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING
  EndIf
  now = cyw43_Ticks()

  If (now - cyw43_joinT0) > (cyw43_hz / 1000) * #CYW43_JOIN_TOTAL_MS
    cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_TIMEOUT
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_FAILED
  EndIf

  If (cyw43_joinState & #CYW43_JS_LINK) <> 0
    cyw43_joinPhase = #CYW43_PHASE_HANDSHAKE
    If (now - cyw43_joinTlink) > (cyw43_hz / 1000) * #CYW43_JOIN_HANDSHAKE_MS
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_TIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_FAILED
    EndIf
  Else
    cyw43_joinPhase = #CYW43_PHASE_ASSOC
    If (now - cyw43_joinT0) > (cyw43_hz / 1000) * #CYW43_JOIN_ASSOC_MS
      cyw43_joinFail = #CYW43_JOINFAIL_TIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_FAILED
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_JOIN_RUNNING
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43DrainEvents - SPEND THE WHOLE BUDGET PUMPING THE RADIO.
;
;  IT IS A BACKOFF AS WELL AS A DRAIN AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS ON
;  THE PICO FORGOT IT WAS ALSO A BACKOFF. That version stopped as soon
;  as two consecutive polls came back empty, which on a quiet chip is
;  about 40 ms - so it drained correctly and DELETED THE BACKOFF
;  ENTIRELY, and the re-soak caught a retry re-issuing WLC_SET_SSID in
;  the same millisecond as the failure it was retrying
;  (cyw43.pico:5556-5570). Poll for the ENTIRE budget.
;
;  Returns how many events were consumed, for a caller to print.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43DrainEvents(budgetMs.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define n.i

  budget = (cyw43_hz / 1000) * budgetMs
  t0 = cyw43_Ticks()
  n = 0
  While (cyw43_Ticks() - t0) < budget
    If cyw43_PollEvent(20) = 1
      While cyw43_EvrPop() <> 0
        n = n + 1
      Wend
    EndIf
  Wend
  ; Anything latched during the wait belongs to the attempt that just
  ; died, not to the one about to start.
  cyw43_EvrClear()
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  WHAT THE FIRMWARE SAYS ABOUT THE LINK - the read-backs that turn a
;  state machine's opinion into evidence.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ReadBssid - ioctl 23, WLC_GET_BSSID. SIX RAW BYTES.
;
;  THIS IS THE IS-CONNECTED TEST AND IT IS FREE. The firmware answers
;  -17 BCME_NOTASSOCIATED when we are not joined - the only code in the
;  whole BCME table that is a QUESTION ANSWERED rather than a fault.
;
;  Returns  1 associated, bytes in cyw43_linkBssid
;           0 NOT associated - the firmware said -17 in as many words
;          -1 anything else, raw code in Cyw43IoctlStatus()
;
;  A -17 THAT STARTS ARRIVING WITH NO PRECEDING DISASSOC OR DEAUTH
;  EVENT means the link was lost AND the event that said so was lost - a
;  TRANSPORT fault, not an RF one, and a different bug. Worth watching
;  for that pairing rather than for the -17 alone.
;
;  CALL IT BEFORE THE JOIN AS WELL AS AFTER. A baseline that reads
;  "not associated" is what makes the second reading mean something;
;  without it, a firmware that answers 1 to everything would look like
;  a successful association.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ReadBssid(timeoutMs.i)
  Define r.i
  Define i.i

  For i = 0 To 5
    cyw43_linkBssid[i] = 0
  Next

  r = Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_BSSID, @cyw43_arg[0], 6, timeoutMs)
  If r = #CYW43_IO_OK
    If cyw43_replyLen < 6
      ProcedureReturn -1
    EndIf
    For i = 0 To 5
      cyw43_linkBssid[i] = cyw43_Rx8(cyw43_replyOff + i)
    Next
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If cyw43_Signed32(cyw43_ioctlStatus) = -17
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43LinkBssidByte(i.i)
  If i < 0 Or i > 5
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_linkBssid[i] & $FF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ReadRssi - ioctl 127, an i32 little-endian in dBm.
;
;  SIGN-EXTENDED ON THE WAY OUT. Every RSSI in this file goes through a
;  named signed reader for the reason cyw43_RxI16 gives: -45 read
;  unsigned is 4294967251, which is not a number anybody recognises as
;  wrong at a glance and prints happily.
;
;  A 0 HERE IS SUSPICIOUS RATHER THAN GOOD. 0 dBm is not a signal
;  strength any station ever measures; it is what an unassociated
;  firmware, or a failed read, tends to leave in the buffer. Read it
;  next to Cyw43ReadBssid rather than on its own.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ReadRssi(timeoutMs.i)
  Define r.i
  cyw43_linkRssi = 0
  r = Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_RSSI, @cyw43_arg[0], 4, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  If cyw43_replyLen < 4
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_BADFRAME
  EndIf
  cyw43_linkRssi = cyw43_Signed32(cyw43_RxLE32(cyw43_replyOff))
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43LinkRssi()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_linkRssi
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ReadChannel - ioctl 29, WLC_GET_CHANNEL.
;
;  THE ONE IOCTL IN THIS FILE WITH A SINGLE SOURCE, and it is said again
;  here rather than only at the constant. Neither Pico driver ever sends
;  it, so the number 29 comes from fwil.h alone and the reply layout is
;  read as a plain i32 because that is the simplest thing consistent
;  with the name. If this row disagrees with the channel the scan
;  reported for the same BSSID, BELIEVE THE SCAN - it is the reading
;  with two independent implementations behind it - and treat this
;  procedure as the thing that is wrong.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ReadChannel(timeoutMs.i)
  Define r.i
  cyw43_linkChannel = 0
  r = Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_CHANNEL, @cyw43_arg[0], 4, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  If cyw43_replyLen < 4
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_BADFRAME
  EndIf
  cyw43_linkChannel = cyw43_Signed32(cyw43_RxLE32(cyw43_replyOff))
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43LinkChannel()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_linkChannel
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ReadSsid - ioctl 25, the same 36-byte struct WLC_SET_SSID takes.
;
;  READING BACK WHAT WE ASKED FOR IS NOT CIRCULAR HERE, and it is worth
;  saying why because it looks it. The firmware answers with the SSID of
;  the BSS it is CURRENTLY IN, not with the last value written to it -
;  an unassociated station answers with a zero length. So a name coming
;  back is evidence of association; the fact that it matches what we
;  sent is a bonus.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ReadSsid(timeoutMs.i)
  Define r.i
  Define n.i
  Define i.i

  cyw43_linkSsidLen = 0
  cyw43_linkSsid[0] = 0

  r = Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_SSID, @cyw43_arg[0], #CYW43_SSID_STRUCT, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  If cyw43_replyLen < #CYW43_SSID_STRUCT
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_BADFRAME
  EndIf
  n = cyw43_RxLE32(cyw43_replyOff)
  ; CLAMPED, NOT TRUSTED. This is a length from the air by way of the
  ; firmware, and 32 is the 802.11 maximum; a longer one would walk off
  ; a 33-byte array.
  If n < 0
    n = 0
  EndIf
  If n > #CYW43_SSID_MAX
    n = #CYW43_SSID_MAX
  EndIf
  For i = 0 To n - 1
    cyw43_linkSsid[i] = cyw43_Rx8(cyw43_replyOff + 4 + i)
  Next
  cyw43_linkSsid[n] = 0
  cyw43_linkSsidLen = n
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43LinkSsid()
  ProcedureReturn @cyw43_linkSsid[0]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43LinkSsidLen()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_linkSsidLen
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  WHAT THE JOIN STATE MACHINE IS THINKING - accessors, for an
;  instrument. None of it is meant to be poked at from outside.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetJoinExpectation - declare, BEFORE the join, what kind of link
;  is being attempted. See #CYW43_JOINEXP_NOKEY for the whole argument.
;
;  IT IS SET BY THE CALLER AND NOT INFERRED HERE, deliberately.
;  Cyw43SecurityWpa2 sets SECURED because that is what it configured;
;  a caller that drove Cyw43SecItem itself and WATCHED item 2 be refused
;  knows something this file does not, and must be able to say so. The
;  library refusing to guess is the same rule the CLM and the country
;  handling follow.
;
;  A value outside the three is treated as SECURED - the strictest
;  reading, which can only produce a MORE conservative verdict.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Cyw43SetJoinExpectation(mode.i)
  If mode = #CYW43_JOINEXP_OPEN Or mode = #CYW43_JOINEXP_NOKEY
    cyw43_joinSecured = mode
  Else
    cyw43_joinSecured = #CYW43_JOINEXP_SECURED
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinExpectation()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_joinSecured
EndProcedure

; What JOINED actually means for the expectation in force. HERE rather
; than in an instrument because the sentence and the enumeration must
; not be able to drift apart, and because this is the one line in the
; whole file most likely to be quoted out of context into a report.
Procedure.i Cyw43JoinedMeansText()
  Select cyw43_joinSecured
    Case #CYW43_JOINEXP_OPEN
      ProcedureReturn "associated to an OPEN network - there is no key and none was needed"
    Case #CYW43_JOINEXP_NOKEY
      ProcedureReturn "802.11 AUTHENTICATED AND ASSOCIATED ONLY, AND NOT KEYED. WPA2 was configured, the four-way handshake did not happen because nothing on either side can run it, no pairwise key exists, NO DATA FRAME WILL PASS, and the access point may deauthenticate at any moment. This is a measurement of how far association gets, not a working link"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "associated AND keyed - the four-way handshake completed"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinState()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_joinState
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinFail()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_joinFail
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinPhase()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_joinPhase
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinSsidOk()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_joinSsidOk
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinFailReg()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_failReg
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43MfpRc()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_mfpRc
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43IcvErrors()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_icvCount
EndProcedure

; The event this poll drained, or 0 in cyw43_jevNew if it drained none.
Procedure.i Cyw43EventNew()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_jevNew
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43PopEvent - pop ONE async event that the receive path already
;  pulled off the bus into the ring, into the cyw43_jev* fields (read
;  them with Cyw43JoinEventType/Status/Reason/Flags). Returns 1 if there
;  was one, 0 if the ring was empty.
;
;  FOR STEADY-STATE MONITORING. During a join Cyw43JoinPoll owns the
;  ring; after a join nothing pops it, so a DEAUTH/DISASSOC the access
;  point sends would sit unread and the link would look associated until
;  the round-trip timeout. A caller (WifiLinkTick) drains it here so a
;  real teardown is acted on at once. It does NOT poll the bus - the
;  data-frame receive path already fills the ring - so it steals no
;  console frame.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43PopEvent()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_EvrPop()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinEventType()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_jevType
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinEventStatus()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_jevStatus
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinEventReason()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_jevReason
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinEventFlags()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_jevFlags
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43EventsSeen()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_evrTotal
EndProcedure

; NON-ZERO INVALIDATES EVERY VERDICT FROM THE RUN, which is why it is a
; separate accessor and not folded into a count. See cyw43_EvrPush.
Procedure.i Cyw43EventsDropped()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_evrDropped
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The English name of an event and of a failure class. HERE and not in
;  a payload, for the same reason Cyw43SecItemName is: the mapping is
;  this file's, and a second copy would drift the first time a code
;  moves.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43EventName(t.i)
  Select t
    Case #CYW43_EV_SET_SSID
      ProcedureReturn "SET_SSID"
    Case #CYW43_EV_JOIN
      ProcedureReturn "JOIN"
    Case #CYW43_EV_ASSOC
      ProcedureReturn "ASSOC"
    Case #CYW43_EV_AUTH
      ProcedureReturn "AUTH"
    Case #CYW43_EV_DEAUTH
      ProcedureReturn "DEAUTH"
    Case #CYW43_EV_DEAUTH_IND
      ProcedureReturn "DEAUTH_IND"
    Case #CYW43_EV_DISASSOC
      ProcedureReturn "DISASSOC"
    Case #CYW43_EV_DISASSOC_IND
      ProcedureReturn "DISASSOC_IND"
    Case #CYW43_EV_LINK
      ProcedureReturn "LINK"
    Case #CYW43_EV_PRUNE
      ProcedureReturn "PRUNE"
    Case #CYW43_EV_PSK_SUP
      ProcedureReturn "PSK_SUP"
    Case #CYW43_EV_ICV_ERROR
      ProcedureReturn "ICV_ERROR"
    Case #CYW43_E_ESCAN_RESULT
      ProcedureReturn "ESCAN_RESULT"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "event this driver does not name - see fweh.h"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43AssocStatusText - the ACCESS POINT'S OWN WORDS.
;
;  ================================================================
;  THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE IN THE DRIVER WHERE THE OTHER END SPEAKS.
;  ================================================================
;  Everything else in this file is our firmware's opinion of what went
;  wrong. An ASSOC event's REASON field is different: it is the 802.11
;  status code out of the access point's association response, chosen by
;  the AP, and it says why IT refused US.
;
;  MEASURED 2026-08-27 AND THIS PROCEDURE EXISTS BECAUSE OF IT. Joining
;  the bench's 5 GHz radio gave AUTH status 0 and then ASSOC status 1
;  reason 43. Without the ASSOC event all that reached the log was
;  SET_SSID status 1, "the association failed", which named nothing and
;  sent a whole session looking at DFS channel rules - a guess that was
;  WRONG. 43 is AKMP_NOT_VALID and it is a precise, actionable answer
;  about the key management suite, not about the channel.
;
;  ONLY THE RSN BLOCK IS NAMED, 40 to 46. Those are the codes a WPA2
;  station actually meets and every one of them points at a specific
;  field of the RSN information element we sent. The other eighty-odd
;  codes in the table are for cases this driver cannot produce -
;  reassociation, 802.11r, HT/VHT negotiation, load balancing - and
;  naming a code this file cannot cause would be a table nobody can
;  test. Anything else is returned as its number, which is one search
;  away from IEEE 802.11 Table 9-50.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43AssocStatusText(code.i)
  Select code
    Case 0
      ProcedureReturn "SUCCESS"
    Case 40
      ProcedureReturn "40 INVALID_IE - the access point could not parse an information element we sent"
    Case 41
      ProcedureReturn "41 GROUP_CIPHER_NOT_VALID - our group cipher is not one it offers"
    Case 42
      ProcedureReturn "42 PAIRWISE_CIPHER_NOT_VALID - our pairwise cipher is not one it offers. WSEC is the knob"
    Case 43
      ProcedureReturn "43 AKMP_NOT_VALID - THE KEY MANAGEMENT SUITE. We offered exactly one, WPA2-PSK ($0080), and this access point does not accept it. That is what a WPA3-only or a WPA2/WPA3-transition radio says to a station offering plain PSK: it wants SAE, or PSK-SHA256 if it also requires management frame protection. wpa_auth is the knob and this driver has one value to give it"
    Case 44
      ProcedureReturn "44 UNSUPP_RSN_VERSION - the RSN IE version we sent is not one it knows"
    Case 45
      ProcedureReturn "45 INVALID_RSN_IE_CAP - the capabilities field, which is where the management-frame-protection bits live. Look at mfp"
    Case 46
      ProcedureReturn "46 CIPHER_SUITE_REJECTED - the suite is understood and refused by policy"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "an 802.11 status code this driver does not name - see IEEE 802.11 Table 9-50"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43JoinFailText()
  Select cyw43_joinFail
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_NONE
      ProcedureReturn "no failure recorded"
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_NONET
      ProcedureReturn "SET_SSID status 3 NO_NETWORKS - the SSID was not on the air on any channel this scan walked. Re-asking 300 ms later asks the same question; check the name byte for byte and check the band"
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_WRONGPASS
      ProcedureReturn "DEAUTH_IND reason 2 - THE ACCESS POINT REJECTED THE KEY. This is the AP's own verdict, not a timeout, and it is the one signature that does mean the passphrase is wrong"
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_PRUNE
      ProcedureReturn "PRUNE reason 8 RSN mismatch - our WSEC/WPA_AUTH does not satisfy the AP's RSN IE. The network is WPA3-only, Enterprise, or mixed in a way WPA2_PSK does not cover. RETRYING UNCHANGED WILL LOOP FOR EVER"
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_TIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn "OUR guard fired, not the firmware's - see the phase, which says whether we were still associating or already handshaking. A timeout in ASSOC with no events at all is a transport fault, not an RF one"
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_BADAUTH
      ProcedureReturn "AUTH status not 0 or 6, or PSK_SUP in a state this driver does not accept - the raw event, status and reason are the report"
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_ASSOC
      ProcedureReturn "THE ASSOCIATION ITSELF WAS REFUSED. If the event was 7 ASSOC then its REASON field is the access point's own 802.11 status code and Cyw43AssocStatusText names it; if it was 0 SET_SSID then the status is the firmware's summary and carries no code"
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_LINKLOST
      ProcedureReturn "DEAUTH / DISASSOC / LINK-down - the link went away. Before a join completes that is a refusal; after one it is a teardown"
    Case #CYW43_JOINFAIL_KEYTIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn "PSK_SUP reason 15 - THE FIRMWARE'S SUPPLICANT TIMED OUT AND THIS IS NOT PROOF OF A WRONG PASSPHRASE. On the twin's bench a correct passphrase did this on 21 of 22 first attempts and keyed in 13 ms on the retry. Disassociate, drain, and try again"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "unknown join failure code"
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 9 - THE DATA PATH. 802.3 frames in and out, over SDPCM
;  channel 2 inside a four-byte BDC header.
;
;  THE MAPPING, SAID PLAINLY, BECAUSE NOTHING ELSE IN THIS TREE DOES IT.
;  On Ethernet a driver hands the MAC a bare 802.3 frame. Here every
;  frame is wrapped twice:
;
;      [ 12 bytes SDPCM ][ 4 bytes BDC ][ the 802.3 frame ]
;
;  The SDPCM header is the same one every ioctl already uses - length,
;  its complement, sequence, channel, data offset, flow control, credit.
;  Only the CHANNEL differs: 2 rather than 0. The BDC header between it
;  and the frame is four bytes:
;
;      +0  flags        $20, BDC_PROTO_VER 2 in the top nibble
;      +1  priority     0
;      +2  flags2       0
;      +3  data_offset  IN 32-BIT WORDS, 0 on transmit
;
;  So SENDING is: build the 802.3 frame at +16, fill in four bytes of
;  BDC at +12, and let the transport put the SDPCM header on the front.
;  RECEIVING is the reverse and is already done - cyw43_RxFrame's data
;  arm reads the BDC data_offset (which IS non-zero in practice) and
;  leaves cyw43_dataP pointing at the first byte of the 802.3 frame.
;
;  net.pi4 THEREFORE NEEDS NO WI-FI IN IT. It performs no I/O by design;
;  the caller hands it a received frame and takes back a frame to send.
;  The pump is Cyw43Receive -> NetInput -> Cyw43Send, which is the same
;  shape as the Ethernet path's GenetRecvWait -> NetInput -> GenetSend
;  with two different procedures at the ends.
; ======================================================================

#CYW43_BDC_HDRLEN  = 4
#CYW43_BDC_FLAGS   = $20     ; BDC_PROTO_VER 2, shifted into the top
                             ; nibble. bcdc.c's BDC_FLAG_VER_SHIFT is 4
#CYW43_BDC_OFF_FLAGS = 0
#CYW43_BDC_OFF_PRIO  = 1
#CYW43_BDC_OFF_FLAG2 = 2
#CYW43_BDC_OFF_DOFF  = 3

; The most a single 802.3 frame may be. 1514 is the classic Ethernet
; maximum without the FCS, and the transmit buffer has to hold that plus
; both headers.
;
; THAT SENTENCE USED TO END "which is what fixes #CYW43_TXBUF's 1152 as
; TOO SMALL for a full-size frame ... the day something wants one,
; #CYW43_TXBUF is the number to raise." IT WAS RAISED ON 2026-09-07, to
; 1536, and the arithmetic is written out beside the constant. So a
; full-MTU frame now fits and Cyw43Send accepts it; the refusal below is
; still a refusal rather than a truncation, because a truncated Ethernet
; frame is not a shorter message, it is a corrupt one - it simply has
; nothing left to refuse at this size.
#CYW43_ETH_MAX = 1514

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43DataPtr - where a caller BUILDS an outgoing 802.3 frame.
;
;  BUILD IN PLACE, DO NOT COPY. The alternative - Cyw43Send(*frame, n)
;  copying from the caller's buffer - is one memcpy per packet on a
;  board with no cache enabled, and it also means every caller needs its
;  own 1514-byte buffer. Handing out the address inside cyw43_tx costs
;  nothing and net.pi4 is already written to be handed a buffer.
;
;  THE POINTER IS ONLY VALID UNTIL THE NEXT IOCTL. cyw43_tx is one
;  buffer and an ioctl builds its request in the same bytes. That is the
;  one rule, and it is the same rule the receive side has.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43DataPtr()
  ProcedureReturn @cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BDC_HDRLEN
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43DataMax()
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_TXBUF - #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN - #CYW43_BDC_HDRLEN
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43Send - transmit the 802.3 frame already sitting at
;  Cyw43DataPtr(), `length` bytes of it.
;
;  IT REFUSES RATHER THAN TRUNCATING. A truncated Ethernet frame is not
;  a shorter message, it is a corrupt one, and it would be dropped by
;  the far end with no error anybody here could see.
;
;  THE CREDIT RULE IS THE SAME AS AN IOCTL'S and is not re-argued here:
;  drain briefly, send anyway, count the stall. A hard block would turn
;  one lost credit into a board that stops responding, and over Wi-Fi
;  that would take away the way to fix it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43Send(length.i)
  Define total.i
  Define padded.i
  Define i.i

  If length < 1 Or length > #CYW43_ETH_MAX
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  total = #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BDC_HDRLEN + length
  padded = (total + 3) & (~3)
  If padded > #CYW43_TXBUF
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf

  ; The BDC header, four bytes, immediately after the SDPCM one.
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BDC_OFF_FLAGS, #CYW43_BDC_FLAGS)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BDC_OFF_PRIO, 0)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BDC_OFF_FLAG2, 0)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN + #CYW43_BDC_OFF_DOFF, 0)

  ; The pad, so the bus moves whole words and the slack is zero rather
  ; than whatever the last frame left behind.
  For i = total To padded - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + i, 0)
  Next

  ; The SDPCM header. Same fields as an ioctl's, channel 2 instead of 0,
  ; and the data offset is 12 because the BDC header is the payload's
  ; first four bytes rather than part of the header.
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_LEN, total & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_LEN + 1, (total >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_COMP, (~total) & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_COMP + 1, ((~total) >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_SEQ, cyw43_seq & $FF)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_CHAN, #CYW43_CHAN_DATA)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_NEXT, 0)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_DOFF, #CYW43_SDPCM_HDRLEN)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_FC, 0)
  PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + #CYW43_SDPCM_OFF_WIN, 0)

  If cyw43_credit = (cyw43_seq & $FF)
    cyw43_Trace(#CYW43_TR_IOCTL_STALL)
    cyw43_Poll(-1, 0, 5)
    If cyw43_credit = (cyw43_seq & $FF)
      cyw43_stalls = cyw43_stalls + 1
    EndIf
  EndIf

  If cyw43_F2Xfer(1, @cyw43_tx[0], padded) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_BADFRAME
  EndIf
  cyw43_seq = (cyw43_seq + 1) & $FF
  cyw43_txFrames = cyw43_txFrames + 1
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43Receive - poll for one 802.3 frame, for up to timeoutMs.
;
;  Returns the frame's LENGTH, or 0 if none arrived. The bytes are at
;  Cyw43ReceivePtr() and ARE ONLY VALID UNTIL THE NEXT CALL INTO THIS
;  FILE - cyw43_rx is one buffer. That is the rule the caller keeps:
;  process one frame to completion before polling again, and never
;  issue an ioctl while a received frame is still being read. A reply
;  being BUILT is legal, because replies build in cyw43_tx.
;
;  EVENTS ARRIVING DURING A DATA POLL STILL GO INTO THE RING. They are
;  not discarded and they are not returned here: a DEAUTH landing while
;  a ping is in flight is exactly the thing a caller needs to be able to
;  find afterwards, and losing it would turn "the link died" into "the
;  ping timed out".
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43Receive(timeoutMs.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define r.i

  cyw43_dataLen = 0

  budget = (cyw43_hz / 1000) * timeoutMs
  If budget < 1
    budget = 1
  EndIf
  t0 = cyw43_Ticks()

  Repeat
    r = cyw43_RxFrame(-1)
    If r = -1
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If r = 5
      cyw43_rxData = cyw43_rxData + 1
      ProcedureReturn cyw43_dataLen
    EndIf
    If (cyw43_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43ReceivePtr()
  ProcedureReturn @cyw43_rx[0] + cyw43_dataP
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43TxFrames()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_txFrames
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43RxDataFrames()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_rxData
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43MacAddress - the station's own six bytes, from the firmware.
;
;  NOT READ OUT OF THE NVRAM TEXT, and the distinction is not pedantry.
;  "macaddr=b8:27:eb:74:f2:6c" is line 10 of this board's NVRAM file and
;  reading it there would give the right six bytes and would be a lie
;  about where they came from - the firmware is entitled to override it,
;  and a driver that reported the file's value would keep reporting it
;  after the firmware had stopped using it.
;
;  THE IOVAR IS "cur_etheraddr". It is a GET_VAR like any other and the
;  reply is six raw bytes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_CURETHER_NAMELEN = 13       ; "cur_etheraddr"

Procedure.i Cyw43MacAddress(*sixBytes, timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define r.i

  n = cyw43_PutName(@cyw43_arg[0], ?cyw43_CurEtherName, #CYW43_CURETHER_NAMELEN)
  For i = n To 19
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + i, 0)
  Next
  r = Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_VAR, @cyw43_arg[0], 20, timeoutMs)
  If r <> #CYW43_IO_OK
    ProcedureReturn r
  EndIf
  If cyw43_replyLen < 6
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_BADFRAME
  EndIf
  For i = 0 To 5
    PokeB(*sixBytes + i, cyw43_Rx8(cyw43_replyOff + i))
  Next
  ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_OK
EndProcedure

DataSection
  ;        c    u    r    _    e    t    h    e    r    a    d    d    r
  cyw43_CurEtherName:
    Data.b  99, 117, 114,  95, 101, 116, 104, 101, 114,  97, 100, 100, 114
EndDataSection

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43Leave / Cyw43LinkUp - the two names the old stub list promised.
;
;  Cyw43LinkUp IS THE STATE MACHINE'S OPINION AND NOTHING MORE, which is
;  why it is one line and why Cyw43ReadBssid exists beside it. The
;  difference between "our accumulator reached $0E01" and "the firmware
;  says it is in a BSS" is the whole difference between a driver that
;  believes itself and one that can be checked.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43Leave(timeoutMs.i)
  Define r.i
  r = Cyw43Disassoc(timeoutMs)
  cyw43_joinDone = 0
  cyw43_joinState = 0
  cyw43_joinPhase = #CYW43_PHASE_IDLE
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Cyw43LinkUp()
  ProcedureReturn cyw43_joinDone
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 10 - WHAT A HOST SUPPLICANT NEEDS FROM THIS FILE.
;
;  ======================================================================
;  READ THIS BEFORE ARGUING WITH IT. THE FIRMWARE ON THIS BOARD HAS NO
;  SUPPLICANT, AND THE PROOF IS THE FIRMWARE'S OWN BUILD STRING.
;  ======================================================================
;  Every Broadcom image carries the list of components it was built
;  with. Extract the printable strings from the two blobs this bench
;  owns and put them side by side:
;
;    THE PICO'S RADIO - Firmware/CYW43439/wb43439A0-7.95.49.00.combined.bin
;      43439a0-roml/sdio-g-pool-p2p-IDSUP-IDAUTH-pktfilter-keepalive-
;      aoe-lpc-swdiv-srfast-fuart-btcx-noclminc-clm_min-fbt-mfp-SAE-
;      wowlpf-tko-nvd-btsdio  Version: 7.95.61 (abcd531 CY)
;
;    THIS BOARD'S RADIO - RaspberryPi4/Reference/firmware/brcmfmac43455-sdio.bin
;      43455c0-roml/43455_sdio-pno-aoe-pktfilter-pktctx-wfds-mfp-
;      dfsradar-wowlpf-noclminc-clm_min-obss-obssdump-swdiv-gtkoe-
;      roamprof-txbf-ve-EXTSAE-dpp-sr-okc-bpd  Version: 7.45.265
;      (28bca26 CY)  Date: Tue 2023-08-29
;
;  `idsup` is the IN-DONGLE SUPPLICANT and `idauth` the in-dongle
;  authenticator. THE PICO'S IMAGE HAS BOTH. THIS ONE HAS NEITHER, and
;  where the Pico's says `sae` this one says `extsae` - EXTERNAL SAE,
;  meaning the host does it. That is the whole difference, and it is a
;  difference in the FIRMWARE IMAGE, not in the chip: a CYW43455 built
;  with `idsup` would take a passphrase exactly as the 43439 does.
;
;  So the four refusals the bench measured on 2026-08-27 were not four
;  bugs to be worked around. They were one fact arriving four times,
;  and the build string is the fifth and cheapest statement of it -
;  it costs no bench run at all.
;
;  ======================================================================
;  WHAT THAT MEANS FOR THIS FILE - AND WHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN.
;  ======================================================================
;  It does NOT mean cyw43.pi4 grows a supplicant. The four-way
;  handshake is 802.1X, not SDIO, and it belongs in wpa2sup.pi4 where
;  it can be read and gated on its own. What this file owes a
;  supplicant is only the things that ARE its business - the ioctls and
;  iovars that carry key material and IEs across the bus:
;
;    Cyw43SetIovarData     name + NUL + arbitrary bytes
;    Cyw43GetIovarData     the same, read back
;    Cyw43SetBsscfgData    name + NUL + u32 index + arbitrary bytes
;    Cyw43SetWpaIe         the RSN IE the association request will carry
;    Cyw43GetAssocReqIes   what the association request ACTUALLY carried
;    Cyw43SetWsecKey       one PTK or GTK into the firmware
;    Cyw43SetPmkBinary     the 32-byte PMK, brcmfmac's shape
;    Cyw43SupWpaEnable     the BARE `sup_wpa` set, brcmfmac's spelling
;
;  Every one is cited to brcmfmac, which is the driver that works with
;  THIS firmware image on every Raspberry Pi in the world.
; ======================================================================
; ======================================================================

#CYW43_WPAIE_NAMELEN    = 5     ; "wpaie"
#CYW43_WSECKEY_NAMELEN  = 15    ; "bsscfg:wsec_key"
#CYW43_ASSOCIES_NAMELEN = 13    ; "assoc_req_ies"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetIovarData - name + NUL, then `datalen` raw bytes.
;
;  THE SHAPE brcmf_fil_iovar_data_set SENDS (fwil.c). Cyw43SetIovarU32
;  is the special case of this with four bytes of little-endian integer
;  behind the name; an IE or a key struct is not an integer and putting
;  one through the u32 setter would send its first four bytes and drop
;  the rest, which the firmware would accept as a well-formed set of
;  the wrong thing.
;
;  A NULL *data WITH datalen 0 IS LEGAL AND MEANS "clear it". That is
;  how brcmfmac removes a stale user-space RSNE (cfg80211.c:2489,
;  `brcmf_fil_iovar_data_set(ifp, "wpaie", NULL, 0)`), so the bound
;  test below is >= 0 rather than > 0 and the copy loop simply does
;  not run.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SetIovarData(src.i, namelen.i, *data, datalen.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  If datalen < 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  n = cyw43_PutName(@cyw43_arg[0], src, namelen)
  If (n + datalen) > #CYW43_ARGBUF
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  For i = 0 To datalen - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + n + i, PeekA(*data + i))
  Next
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_VAR, @cyw43_arg[0], n + datalen, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43GetIovarData - ask for `buflen` bytes back. The reply is at
;  Cyw43ReplyPtr() for Cyw43ReplyLen() bytes, like every other GET here.
;
;  IDENTICAL TO Cyw43GetIovarStr in what it puts on the wire, and it
;  exists anyway. The str version's name says "this reply is text" and
;  callers print it; `assoc_req_ies` is a run of binary TLVs with
;  embedded zero bytes, and a reader who saw Cyw43GetIovarStr against
;  it would reasonably conclude the reply was NUL-terminated. The cost
;  of the honest name is one procedure.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43GetIovarData(src.i, namelen.i, buflen.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  If buflen > #CYW43_ARGBUF
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  n = cyw43_PutName(@cyw43_arg[0], src, namelen)
  If n > buflen
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  For i = n To buflen - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + i, 0)
  Next
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlGet(#CYW43_C_GET_VAR, @cyw43_arg[0], buflen, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetBsscfgData - name + NUL + u32 LE index + raw bytes.
;
;  brcmf_create_bsscfg (fwil.c) builds exactly this, and
;  Cyw43SetIovarU32x2 is its two-integer special case. `bsscfg:wsec_key`
;  is the only caller today and it carries 164 bytes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SetBsscfgData(src.i, namelen.i, idx.i, *data, datalen.i, timeoutMs.i)
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  If datalen < 0
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  n = cyw43_PutName(@cyw43_arg[0], src, namelen)
  If (n + 4 + datalen) > #CYW43_ARGBUF
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_arg[0] + n, idx)
  For i = 0 To datalen - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + n + 4 + i, PeekA(*data + i))
  Next
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_VAR, @cyw43_arg[0], n + 4 + datalen, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetWpaIe - hand the firmware the exact RSN information element
;  the association request is to carry.
;
;  ======================================================================
;  THIS IS THE ONE THAT MAKES A HOST FOUR-WAY HANDSHAKE POSSIBLE AT ALL.
;  ======================================================================
;  Message 2 of the handshake carries the supplicant's RSNE, and the
;  authenticator VERIFIES IT IS BYTE-FOR-BYTE THE ONE IT SAW IN THE
;  ASSOCIATION REQUEST (IEEE 802.11 12.7.6.3; hostapd does this in
;  wpa_validate_wpa_ie and deauthenticates on a mismatch). If the
;  firmware synthesised the association request's IE from `wsec` and
;  `wpa_auth`, the host would have to GUESS those bytes - and a guess
;  that is one capability bit out fails the handshake with a
;  deauthentication and no diagnosis.
;
;  brcmfmac does not guess either. brcmf_cfg80211_connect finds the RSN
;  IE inside the IEs userspace gave it and sends them down verbatim:
;  `brcmf_fil_iovar_data_set(ifp, "wpaie", ie, ie_len)`
;  (cfg80211.c:2409). The host chooses the bytes; the firmware carries
;  them. Then the same bytes go into message 2 and the comparison is a
;  memcmp of something we control at both ends.
;
;  CALL IT BEFORE WLC_SET_SSID. It configures the request; after the
;  request has gone out it configures nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SetWpaIe(*ie, len.i, timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarData(?cyw43_WpaIeName, #CYW43_WPAIE_NAMELEN, *ie, len, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43GetAssocReqIes - read back the IEs the association request
;  actually carried, so `wpaie` can be CHECKED rather than trusted.
;
;  brcmfmac reads the same iovar to fill in station info
;  (cfg80211.c:6294). The reply is a run of 802.11 elements: one byte
;  id, one byte length, `length` bytes of body. The RSN element is
;  id 48 ($30).
;
;  ======================================================================
;  IT IS THE SOURCE, NOT A CHECK, AND THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY THE
;  OPPOSITE. CORRECTED BY THE BOARD, 2026-08-28.
;  ======================================================================
;  It read: "A driver that read the IE here and used it in message 2
;  would be trusting the firmware to hand back what it sent, which is
;  precisely the thing worth verifying." That is backwards, and the
;  bench said so:
;
;      RSN element in the request:  30 14 01 00 00 0F AC 04 01 00
;                                   00 0F AC 04 01 00 00 0F AC 02 0C 00
;      IT MATCHES THE ONE WE ASKED FOR (want 1)  0
;
;  `wpaie` was given capabilities $0000 and the firmware transmitted
;  $000C - the PTKSA Replay Counter field, its own capability, written
;  over ours. Every other byte was identical.
;
;  THE AUTHENTICATOR COMPARES MESSAGE 2's RSNE WITH WHAT IT RECEIVED,
;  not with what we intended to send (IEEE 802.11 12.7.6.3). So the
;  firmware's report of what it transmitted is the closest thing to the
;  truth that exists on this side of the air, and the value we asked
;  for is demonstrably NOT it. A supplicant that used our copy sends a
;  message 2 the access point drops in silence - message 3 never
;  arrives and it reads exactly like a wrong passphrase.
;
;  So: send known bytes with Cyw43SetWpaIe, then READ THEM BACK HERE
;  and use what comes back. Print both when they differ; that
;  difference is a fact about the firmware and worth seeing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43GetAssocReqIes(buflen.i, timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43GetIovarData(?cyw43_AssocIesName, #CYW43_ASSOCIES_NAMELEN, buflen, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SupWpaEnable - the BARE `sup_wpa` iovar, set to 1.
;
;  NOT `bsscfg:sup_wpa`, and the 2026-08-27 bench sent the prefixed one.
;  brcmf_cfg80211_connect writes the BARE name -
;  `brcmf_fil_iovar_int_set(ifp, "sup_wpa", 1)` (cfg80211.c:2477) - and
;  the interface is carried in the BCDC header, not in the payload. The
;  prefixed spelling is a different variable in a context that has no
;  bsscfg attached to the question.
;
;  EXPECTED TO BE REFUSED ON THIS IMAGE, and it is asked anyway. It
;  costs one ioctl, it is the exact call the working driver makes, and
;  a -23 from THIS spelling closes the question that the build string
;  already answered - which is how a fact stops being an inference.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SupWpaEnable(timeoutMs.i)
  ProcedureReturn Cyw43SetIovarU32(?cyw43_SupWpaName, #CYW43_SUPWPA_NAMELEN, 1, timeoutMs)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetPmkBinary - WLC_SET_WSEC_PMK carrying a 32-byte BINARY PMK.
;
;  THE OTHER SHAPE OF THE SAME IOCTL, and the one brcmfmac sends:
;
;      brcmf_set_pmk(ifp, sme->crypto.psk, BRCMF_WSEC_MAX_PSK_LEN)
;      pmk.key_len = 32;  pmk.flags = 0;  memcpy(pmk.key, pmk_data, 32);
;      brcmf_fil_cmd_data_set(ifp, BRCMF_C_SET_WSEC_PMK, &pmk,
;                             sizeof(pmk));       /* 132 */
;
;  Cyw43SetPmk sends the PICO's shape: flags = 1, key_len = the number
;  of ASCII characters, and the firmware runs PBKDF2. This one sends
;  flags = 0 and 32 raw bytes the HOST derived, which is what a driver
;  sends when userspace already did the arithmetic.
;
;  BOTH ARE HERE BECAUSE THEY ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. On 2026-08-27
;  the passphrase form answered -2 BCME_BADARG at both documented
;  struct sizes - BADARG, not -23 BCME_UNSUPPORTED, so the command
;  exists and the ARGUMENT was wrong. A firmware with no in-dongle
;  supplicant has nothing to run PBKDF2 with and would reject flags = 1
;  exactly that way, while still accepting a binary PMK for the paths
;  that do use one. That is a bench question and this is the call that
;  asks it.
;
;  THE WIPE IS UNCONDITIONAL, for the reason Cyw43SetPmk gives at
;  length: these 32 bytes are the passphrase for every practical
;  purpose and both buffers are within reach of Anvil's `d`.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43SetPmkBinary(*pmk32, timeoutMs.i)
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define keyoff.i

  For i = 0 To #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_132 - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + i, 0)
  Next
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 0, 32)
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 1, 0)
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 2, 0)
  PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + 3, 0)
  For i = 0 To 31
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + #CYW43_PMK_KEYOFF + i, PeekA(*pmk32 + i))
  Next

  r = Cyw43IoctlSet(#CYW43_C_SET_WSEC_PMK, @cyw43_arg[0], #CYW43_PMK_STRUCT_132, timeoutMs)

  For i = 0 To #CYW43_PMK_KEYMAX - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + #CYW43_PMK_KEYOFF + i, 0)
  Next
  keyoff = #CYW43_CTRL_HDRLEN + #CYW43_PMK_KEYOFF
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_PMK_KEYMAX - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + keyoff + i, 0)
  Next

  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43SetWsecKey - plumb one key, pairwise or group.
;
;  THE STRUCT IS struct brcmf_wsec_key_le, fwil_types.h:558-575, and it
;  is 164 bytes because of THREE PADDING RUNS a reader will not guess:
;
;      +0    4   index            key index, 0..3
;      +4    4   len              16 for CCMP
;      +8   32   data             the key
;      +40  72   pad_1[18]        <- eighteen u32 of nothing
;      +112  4   algo             CRYPTO_ALGO_AES_CCM = 4
;      +116  4   flags            BRCMF_PRIMARY_KEY = 2, or 0
;      +120 12   pad_2[3]
;      +132  4   iv_initialized
;      +136  4   pad_3
;      +140  4   rxiv.hi
;      +144  2   rxiv.lo   (+2 tail padding to the next u32)
;      +148  8   pad_4[2]
;      +156  6   ea               the peer, for a pairwise key
;      ----------------------------------------------------------
;      164 with the struct's own 4-byte alignment
;
;  Sent as `bsscfg:wsec_key`, per send_key_to_dongle (cfg80211.c:548).
;
;  PAIRWISE AND GROUP DIFFER IN TWO FIELDS AND NOT IN THE CALL.
;  brcmf_cfg80211_add_key decides `ext_key` from whether a MAC address
;  was supplied (cfg80211.c:2811): with one, ea holds it and flags is
;  0; without, ea is zero and flags is BRCMF_PRIMARY_KEY. Passing *ea
;  as 0 here selects the second, which keeps the caller's two calls
;  readable side by side instead of hiding the difference in a boolean.
;
;  *rsc IS THE GROUP KEY'S RECEIVE SEQUENCE COUNTER, six bytes, little-
;  endian on the wire, and it goes in as rxiv exactly the way
;  cfg80211.c:2827-2836 splits it: hi = bytes 5,4,3,2 and lo = bytes
;  1,0. A pairwise key passes 0 and iv_initialized stays 0. GETTING
;  THIS WRONG IS SILENT: broadcast and multicast traffic is simply
;  dropped as replay while unicast works perfectly, which looks like a
;  routing problem and is not one.
;
;  EVERY BYTE IS CLEARED FIRST. The padding runs are not decoration -
;  the firmware reads the whole struct, and cyw43_arg holds whatever
;  the last ioctl left in it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#CYW43_KEY_STRUCT      = 164
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_INDEX   = 0
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_LEN     = 4
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_DATA    = 8
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_ALGO    = 112
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_FLAGS   = 116
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_IVINIT  = 132
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_RXIVHI  = 140
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_RXIVLO  = 144
#CYW43_KEY_OFF_EA      = 156
#CYW43_KEY_DATA_MAX    = 32

#CYW43_CRYPTO_ALGO_OFF     = 0
#CYW43_CRYPTO_ALGO_AES_CCM = 4
#CYW43_PRIMARY_KEY         = 2      ; BRCMF_PRIMARY_KEY, (1 << 1)

; The key struct is staged HERE and not in cyw43_arg, for the reason
; the CLM buffer gives at length a few thousand lines up:
; Cyw43SetBsscfgData copies into cyw43_arg and Cyw43Ioctl may drain the
; bus for a credit on the way out, and a buffer that is both the source
; and the destination of the same copy is a bug waiting for a stall.
; 168 bytes of BSS - #CYW43_KEY_STRUCT rounded up to a whole number of
; 8-byte words - so that the question does not arise.
Global Dim cyw43_keybuf.l[42]

Procedure.i Cyw43SetWsecKey(index.i, *key, keylen.i, algo.i, *ea, *rsc, timeoutMs.i)
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define flags.i
  Define hi.i
  Define lo.i

  If keylen < 0 Or keylen > #CYW43_KEY_DATA_MAX
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf
  If index < 0 Or index > 3
    ProcedureReturn #CYW43_IO_TOOBIG
  EndIf

  For i = 0 To #CYW43_KEY_STRUCT - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + i, 0)
  Next

  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_INDEX, index)
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_LEN, keylen)
  For i = 0 To keylen - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_DATA + i, PeekA(*key + i))
  Next
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_ALGO, algo)

  flags = #CYW43_PRIMARY_KEY
  If *ea <> 0
    flags = 0
    For i = 0 To 5
      PokeB(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_EA + i, PeekA(*ea + i))
    Next
  EndIf
  cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_FLAGS, flags)

  If *rsc <> 0
    hi = (PeekA(*rsc + 5) << 24) | (PeekA(*rsc + 4) << 16) | (PeekA(*rsc + 3) << 8) | PeekA(*rsc + 2)
    lo = (PeekA(*rsc + 1) << 8) | PeekA(*rsc + 0)
    cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_RXIVHI, hi)
    PokeB(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_RXIVLO, lo & $FF)
    PokeB(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_RXIVLO + 1, (lo >> 8) & $FF)
    cyw43_PutLE32(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + #CYW43_KEY_OFF_IVINIT, 1)
  EndIf

  r = Cyw43SetBsscfgData(?cyw43_WsecKeyName, #CYW43_WSECKEY_NAMELEN, #CYW43_IFACE_STA, @cyw43_keybuf[0], #CYW43_KEY_STRUCT, timeoutMs)

  ; The key is gone from OUR staging copy the moment it is on the wire.
  ; cyw43_arg and cyw43_tx still hold it; those two are wiped by the
  ; caller's Cyw43KeyWipe, which is one call rather than three because
  ; a caller installing a PTK and a GTK should not have to remember the
  ; buffer names of a file it does not own.
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_KEY_STRUCT - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_keybuf[0] + i, 0)
  Next
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43KeyWipe - zero the two transport buffers that a key passed
;  through.
;
;  SEPARATE FROM Cyw43SetWsecKey ON PURPOSE. The transmit buffer still
;  holds the frame the firmware may not have finished reading when the
;  procedure returns, and wiping it inside the setter would be a race
;  with the bus. The caller wipes once, after the last key is in.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Cyw43KeyWipe()
  Define i.i
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_ARGBUF - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_arg[0] + i, 0)
  Next
  For i = 0 To #CYW43_TXBUF - 1
    PokeB(@cyw43_tx[0] + i, 0)
  Next
EndProcedure

DataSection
  ;        w    p    a    i    e
  cyw43_WpaIeName:
    Data.b 119, 112,  97, 105, 101
  ;        b    s    s    c    f    g    :    w    s    e    c    _    k    e    y
  cyw43_WsecKeyName:
    Data.b  98, 115, 115,  99, 102, 103,  58, 119, 115, 101,  99,  95, 107, 101, 121
  ;        a    s    s    o    c    _    r    e    q    _    i    e    s
  cyw43_AssocIesName:
    Data.b  97, 115, 115, 111,  99,  95, 114, 101, 113,  95, 105, 101, 115
EndDataSection


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Cyw43ErrorText - a NUL-terminated English sentence for
;  Cyw43LastError().
;
;  The sentences are long on purpose. These are the strings a person
;  will actually meet on a bench at midnight, and "not implemented"
;  would send them to read this file instead of telling them the
;  answer. Where a message can name the NEXT THING TO TRY, it does.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Cyw43ErrorText()
  Select cyw43_err
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NONE
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: ok"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NO_IO
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: no SDIO primitives - call Cyw43SetIo with all four before anything else"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: CNTFRQ_EL0 is not a usable frequency - cannot bound any wait, nothing attempted"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_F1_ENABLE
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: function 1 (the backplane) would not enable"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_F2_ENABLE
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: function 2 (WLAN data) would not enable"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_BLKSIZE
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: could not set a function block size - is function 1 enabled?"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_CLK_WRITE
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: CHIPCLKCSR would not read or write - enable SDIO function 1 first, and check WL_ON"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_CLK_ECHO
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: CHIPCLKCSR did not read back what was written - nothing is answering on function 1"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_ALP_TMO
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: the chip never reported ALP available - the crystal is not running"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_WINDOW
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: could not program the backplane window registers"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_READ32
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: a 32-bit backplane read failed"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_WRITE32
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: a 32-bit backplane write failed"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_CHIPID
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: chip ID reads all-zero or all-ones - the backplane is not clocked, or nothing is there"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_SOCI
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: the chip reports a Silicon Backplane, not AXI - this file only walks an AXI EROM"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_EROM
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: the core enumeration table is unreadable or malformed"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_TOO_MANY
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: more cores than this build records - raise CYW43_MAX_CORES rather than trusting a short list"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NOT_ATTACHED
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: not attached - Cyw43Attach did not succeed"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_ALIGN
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: buffer is not 4-byte aligned"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NO_FIRMWARE
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: REFUSED - this call needs the SDPCM transport, which is not in this file yet. Firmware upload and start ARE implemented; scan, join and frames are not, because they are ioctl and event exchanges inside a framing layer nobody here has written. The reference is RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico from line 2544."
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NO_BLOCK_IO
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: block mode was asked for but Cyw43SetBlockIo was never called - install SdioCmd53ReadBlocks and SdioCmd53WriteBlocks, or leave block mode off and accept the slower byte-mode path"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NO_CORE
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: a core this step needs is not in the chip's own enumeration - if the ARM CR4 is the one missing then this is not a CYW43455 and nothing above here applies to it"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NO_WRAPPER
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: the core exists but the EROM gave it no AXI wrapper, and every reset register lives in the wrapper - refusing rather than writing to the core's own base, which would hit a different core's registers"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_RAMINFO
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: the chip's RAM size is missing or impossible - call Cyw43PrepareDownload first, because the CR4's bank registers only answer once the CR4 is out of reset"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_CORE_RESET
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: a core would not leave reset after fifty attempts - see Cyw43CoreIsUp, and check that the backplane still has its ALP clock"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_XFER
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: a bulk transfer across the backplane failed - the SDIO layer's own error says which, through SdioLastError and SdioLastXferBlock"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_VERIFY
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: the read-back did not match what was written - Cyw43BadAddr, Cyw43BadGot and Cyw43BadWant name the first byte that disagreed. Try again with Cyw43SetBlockMode(0): if byte mode verifies and block mode does not, the fault is in the block-mode path and not in the memory"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_BAD_LEN
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: a length, or a caller-supplied buffer, is outside what this call accepts - see the procedure's own comment for the sizes it needs"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NVRAM
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: the NVRAM text produced no usable entries, or none of them was boardrev - this file refuses to invent a default board revision for a board it has never seen"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_HT_TMO
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: the chip never reported the HT clock available - the PLL did not lock, which usually means the NVRAM's xtalfreq never reached the firmware"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_F2_TMO
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: function 2 never reported ready, which is the firmware saying it did not boot - the image and the NVRAM both verified, so look at the reset vector and at whether the ARM actually left reset"
    Case #CYW43_ERR_NOT_LOADED
      ProcedureReturn "cyw43: Cyw43Start refused - releasing the ARM needs BOTH a verified image and a verified NVRAM, and one of them has not been uploaded"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "cyw43: unknown error code"
EndProcedure
